(2, 'Dear [Person''s name address to],\n\n[Content]\n\n[Sign Off]\n[Your Name]', 'Email'),
(3, '[Content]', 'Report'),
(4, '[Title of item to review]\n\n[Author''s Name]\n\n[Content]\n\n[Remarks]', 'Review'),
(5, '[Content]', 'Summary'),
(6, '[Title of Advertisement]\n\n[Content]', 'Advertisement'),
(7, 'Dear [Target Audience],\n\n[Content]\n\n[Sign Off]\n', 'Notice'),
(8, 'Dear [Target Audience],  \n\n[Content] ', 'Annoucement'),
(9, '[Title]\n\n[Content in point form]', 'Instruction'),
(10, 'Dear [Person''s name address to],  \n\n[Content]  \n\n[Sign Off] \n[Your Name]', 'Note'),
(11, 'Dear [Person''s name address to],   \r\n\r\n[Content]  \r\n\r\n[Your name] ', 'Postcard');
INSERT INTO `astarcompo_templates` (`templateID`, `template`, `templateType`, `theme`) VALUES
(1, 'The new school year signalled a new beginning. I was excited to go back to school to hit the books once again. There were many new things to learn, new friends to make and new challenges to face. Above all, I could not wait to find out who my new classmates were. Who among my previous classmates would be joining me again this year? Who were the fresh faces I was going to meet? \r', 'Introduction', 'School and You'),
(2, 'On my first day back at school, I was filled with anticipation. I was later overjoyed to learn that many of my good friends, including Alice, Sue, Damien and Roy were posted to the same class as me. However, my heart sank when I saw Alvin Foo into the classroom.\n\nAlvin was a notorious school bully who had earned himself the reputation of being the only student in the history of the school to have been publicly caned by the discipline master the previous year after he had slapped a female trainee teacher whet she had caught him cheating during a test. In fact, Alvin''s string of misdemeanour! stretched all the way back to the time he first joined the school.\n\nI had had a first-hand experience of Alvin''s bullying in Primary One. While I was queueing up at the noodle stall during recess, Alvin came up to me and tried to cut the queue. When I told him off, he left in a huff. After I had bought a bowl of piping hot soup noodles, I was gingerly carrying my purchase to a vacant table when Alvin crept up behind me and yanked my ponytail forcefully. The surprise attack caused me to drop the bowl of noodles onto the floor. Till today, I still remember the searing pair as the hot soup spilt onto my arms, leaving in its wake a patch of almost raw, tender pink skin. Thankfully, the soup was not at boiling point or my injuries would have beer much worse.\n\nIn the years that followed, Alvin''s pranks on his schoolmates never ceased. He once pushed Damien during a Physical Education lesson, causing the latter to fall down and graze his knee against the rough running track. When we were in Primal Three, Sue once went hungry for an entire month because Alvin had forced her to give him all her pocket money daily. He threatened that he would snip off her long, ponytail when no one was looking if she failed to comply or told on him. Fearing to lose her lovely tresses, Sue did not report about the extortion until she fainted in class one day due to starvation and dehydration. Only then did she reveal the truth to her teacher.\n\nWhy was Alvin allowed to continue with his seemingly endless spate of bullying% In fact, he had been hauled up to the principal''s office countless times to face the music for various misdeeds. His parents were also called up so often that they were embarrassed to face their son''s teachers and principal to hear of yet another wrongful act he had committed. However, throughout the years, Alvin remained unrepentant. Given all that he had done to terrorize me and my friends in the past\n\nI was understandably apprehensive to be his classmate this year. In the next few days, my friends and I kept a distance from Alvin. As the days passed, we were quite surprised that he did not try to bully us or any of our classmates. During lesson time, Alvin also seemed very attentive. Not once did he attempt to disrupt the lesson by misbehaving in class or talking loudly. In fact, on a few occasions, he even raised his hand to volunteer the correct answer when the teacher asked a question. Alvin was always at his best behaviour. Gradually, we let our guard down and accepted Alvin as our friend.\n\nAlvin later told us that his parents had sent him for professional counselling during the school holidays. During the counselling sessions, he had come to realize that his arts of bullying others were actually his way of trying to attract the attention of those around him. Inside, he was a lonely boy who craved acceptance and friendship. His counsellor had made him understand that in order to be loved and accepted by others, he had to change his ways or he would risk ending up alone and friendless.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'School and You'),
(3, 'On hearing Alvin''s honest confession, we were glad to bury the past, welcome him into our fold and be his friend. We admired his courage in wanting to change. Above all, we were glad that the biggest bully in our school was no more a bully. The new school year was indeed going to be a happy and peaceful one. ', 'Conclusion', 'School and You'),
(4, 'Early evening had given way to dusk. The sun was no longer shining and darkness had started to envelop the surroundings. My friend, Sunil, and I were walking along Changi Beach. We had broken away from our friends who were having a barbecue so that we could enjoy some peace and quiet and soak in the cool night breeze. \r', 'Introduction', 'Criminal Acts'),
(5, 'We were walking along a deserted stretch of the beach when we spotted a speedboat heading in our direction. Before we knew it, the speedboat had reached the shore. From where we were standing, we saw a group of five or six small-built men disembark from the boat. Afraid to be seen by them, we ran and hid behind a clump of bushes.\n\nFrom our vantage position, we saw the men, all dressed in singlets and shorts. They were of Asian origin. Our first idea was that they were illegal immigrants who were trying to enter the country without valid documents. However, we next saw the men unload some wooden crates from their boat. One of the men stood on the shore and acted as a lookout.\n\nSeeing the mens furtive behaviour, we smelt a rat and immediately called the police using Sunils mobile phone. We gave the police a detailed description of what we had seen, along with our exact location. The mainland police alerted the police coastguards, who radioed a message to all police coastguard vessels in the vicinity.\n\nWithin minutes, a police coastguard vessel sailed inland to where the boat was. Their arrival was so sudden and unexpected that the men on the boat had no time to escape. We witnessed all six men being apprehended and handcuffed by the police. By that time, two police cars and several customs officers had also arrived at the scene. One of the officers pried open one of the wooden crates. It contained contraband cigarettes. All the suspects were taken away by the police and the entire consignment of contraband cigarettes was seized.\n\nWe later learnt that in order to escape the high tax imposed by the government on cigarettes imported into Singapore, the six men had attempted to illegally smuggle cigarettes into Singapore. However, they failed to realize that the harsh penalties they had to pay if they were caught made their shady venture a very risky one indeed.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Criminal Acts'),
(6, 'Following the arrest of the smugglers, Sunil and I were commended by the police for our vigilance and public-spiritedness. The feeling of pride and joy upon receiving our plaque and letter of commendation in front of our family and many reporters Was a moment we would forever cherish in our hearts. However, we were even more satisfied that we had helped the police in preventing a crime from taking place. ', 'Conclusion', 'Criminal Acts'),
(7, 'A passing shower had just bathed the vicinity. On that dark, moonless night, the weather was cool and yet the air was still.  At nine, Mr Lim started his shift as a taxi driver. The roads were still wet and the forecast of intermittent rain throughout Mr the night kept most people indoors. Lim was expecting a slow night, but hopeful that he would at least get a few passengers to cover his taxi rental and fuel costs. Fortunately, he was flagged down by a passenger soon after. \r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(8, 'Throughout the night, Mr Lim ferried a number of passengers. Business was not all that bad after all. Finally, at around four the next morning, fatigue began to set in and Mr Lim decided to drive home for a well-deserved rest. As he was driving along Choa Chu Kang Road, a lady flagged down his taxi. She was carrying a newborn baby in her arms. With her slender figure, porcelain clear complexion and long, flowing hair, she appeared rather attractive. She had light make-up on her face and was dressed in a simple pink blouse paired with a long, white skirt. Her beautiful, rosy-cheeked baby was sound asleep in her arms, a picture of peace and serenity. The lady told Mr Lim that she wanted to go to Block 12A, Serangoon Avenue One.\n\nMr Lim jokingly asked what a pretty young woman like her was doing out in the middle of the night with her young child. She told him that her abusive husband had come home drunk that night. Afraid that his drunken stupor might induce yet another violent outburst, she had left the house with her baby daughter while he was in the bathroom to go to her mother''s house to stay until he became sober. Mr Lim found her explanation for her late-night journey a logical one, although he felt that she was rather well-dressed for someone who had to leave her home in such a hurry. However, he kept his thoughts to himself. Before she alighted at her destination, Mr Lim told her to take care and advised her to call the police if her husband ever tried to lay a hand on her or her baby.\n\nAfter Mr Lim dropped the lady off at her mother''s place, he headed home. He was worn out after the long drive and eager to get home to have a good sleep. When he reached home and parked his taxi in a vacant lot downstairs his flat, he turned his head to check the back seat to see if anyone had inadvertently left anything behind. He could hardly believe his eyes when he found a newborn baby on the floorboard behind the driver''s seat! He must have been so exhausted that he had failed to notice that the lady had alighted from his taxi without her child!  Mr Lim thought that he might have been tricked by the lady and her sob story. She had the intention of abandoning her baby in his taxi all along.\n\nMr Lim''s first instinct was to take the abandoned infant to the nearest police station and make a police report. However, seeing how cute and adorable the chubby baby looked, Mr Lim, who had been childless after ten years of marriage home, decided to take the baby home to his wife for a while. He thought  for a few hours before taking the baby to the police station later that day.\n\nBack home, Mrs Lim welcomed the baby. She wrapped it in a blanket and caressed it in her arms as she lay on the bed. Soon, she and Mr Lim fell fast asleep. When they woke up at around ten in the morning, Mrs Lim jumped up in fright. In her arms was the thick, warm blanket, but the baby was gone!\n\nWhen Mr Lim counted his takings for the previous night he was short of twenty-two dollars and sixty cents, the exact amount which the lady passenger had paid him. It was as if he had never picked up his last passenger and her baby. Could their intense longing for a child have affected Mr and Mrs Lim so deeply that they dreamt up the whole episode? But how could both husband and wife have had the exact same dream of having the baby right in their house?\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(9, 'The following day, there was a report in the newspapers. A young housewife and her month old baby daughter had been found dead in a flat in Choa Chu Kang the previous morning. They had been knifed to death by the lady''s husband, who had since surrendered himself to the police. He had admitted to killing his wife and daughter while under the influence of alcohol after accusing her of seeing another man. He said that he had lost control of his anger after he had caught her trying to sneak out of the house with their baby. The numerous defensive stab wounds all over the lady''s body showed that she had used her body as a human shield to protect her child during the brutal attack. Forensic pathologists put the time of death at around four the previous morning ... ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(10, 'The mid-year examinations were just round the corner. Being in the final year of their primary school and with the dark cloud of the all-important Primary School Leaving Examination looming ahead towards the end of the year, the whole class was especially hardworking since January. Four months on, everyone had settled comfortably into study mode and was eager to do well in their mid-year examinations. They know how well they fared in the examinations would be a good gauge of how well they had absorbed their lessons and how prepared they were for their preliminary examinations and the actual PSLE later in the year. For the students in Primary 6A, the best class in the level, the competition was even more intense. \r', 'Introduction', 'School and You'),
(11, 'Jeremy was starting to feel the heat from all directions. His parents had high expectations of him to ace the examinations and he did not want to disappoint them. He himself was a high achiever and put a lot of pressure on himself to do well. Jeremy was a conscientious and consistent student who had been constantly paying attention in class and doing his assignments. He also did many assessment books for revision and to expose himself to a variety of possible examination questions. In spite of all this, Jeremy still suffered from pre-examination jitters. He was especially nervous about his Mathematics examination since Mathematics was his weakest subject. As the day of the Mathematics examination drew near, Jeremy''s anxiety increased.\n\nThe day before the Mathematics examination, Jeremy''s form teacher, Mrs Tan, asked him to carry a pile of workbooks to the staff room during recess. Inside the staff room, Jeremy met his classmate, Hasli. Hasli was standing by the photocopier with his back facing Jeremy. Curious about why Hasli was in the staff room, Jeremy moved closer towards him and looked over his shoulder. He saw that Hasli was about to make a photocopy of a set of examination papers. Jeremy caught sight of the cover page and was shocked to see that it was a copy of the Mathematics examination for that year!\n\nJeremy immediately confronted Hasli about his dishonest act. Hasli told him that he knew the teachers were having a meeting during recess and he had actually sneaked into the staff room to try to secretly get back his mobile phone which had earlier been confiscated by Mrs Tan when she caught him using it in class. He was desperate to get it back because his father would cane him if he knew he had brought the phone to school.\n\nHasli had rummaged through Mrs Tan''s handbag and found a set of keys. He tried every key and finally found the right one which opened the drawer of her desk. When he opened the drawer, he found his mobile phone atop a copy of the next days Mathematics examination script. He SOW it as a ''golden opportunity'' he was not going to pass up on. He planned to photocopy a set of the examination paper and put it back in the drawer. He would also put his mobile phone back into the drawer so that Mrs Tan would not suspect anything. A caning from his father Was a small price to pay for acing the examination.\n\nFor a moment, Jeremy was tempted to join Hash in making a photocopy of the coveted examination paper. However, he also thought about how he would be letting his parents and teacher down if he committed such a dishonest act. After all, he had studied very hard and should face the examination bravely. He felt that he should have more confidence in his own ability, especially since he had been preparing very hard for the examinations. He told Hash that they would be making a big mistake if they tried to cheat in the examination. What if they got caught? Having made it thus far in their primary school education without cheating, they should maintain a clean record and a clear conscience.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'School and You'),
(12, 'On hearing what Jeremy had to say, Hasli too realized that the risk he was taking was not worth it, both for attempting to cheat in the examination and for trying to retrieve his confiscated mobile phone. With that, he put the examination paper back into the drawer and placed his mobile phone above it before putting the key back into Mrs Tan''s handbag. Jeremy and Hash then walked out of the staff room with their heads held high. They were going to try their best to ace the next day''s examination based on their own merit and nothing more. ', 'Conclusion', 'School and You'),
(13, 'The school bell rang and the only thing that could be heard was the screeching of chairs being pushed back abruptly against the floor, and the excited chatter of students eager to get home. Everybody rushed towards the stairs, and ran out of the school gate. \r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(14, 'On my way out, I suddenly noticed a commotion at the school gate. Many pupils were present and some who were too short, stood on tiptoes. Out of curiosity , I made a beeline towards the crowd. "I did not do that on purpose!" I heard a boy say. I pushed my way through the crowd, and finally got to the front.\n\nTwo big and burly boys stood facing one another and they glared at each other menacingly. I recognised one of them as a notorious boy in school, Ben. He had gotten himself into trouble many times, mostly due to fighting, and it looked like a fight was going to break out any moment.\n\nI was right. Ben made the first move. He punched the other boy in the face and said, "There, Danny, this is what you get for not saying sorry!" Danny froze momentarily, before kicking Ben in the shin. Both of them then started hurling vulgarities at each other.\n\nThe crowd gasped when Ben whipped out a penknife from his pocket. He brandished it, and the shiny stainless steel blade gleamed in the sunlight. Danny held up his hands in defeat and backed away. However Danny was not fast enough and Ben cut his forehead! My eyes opened wide in shock as I saw blood stream down Danny''s forehead. An ominous rage seemed to have taken hold of Danny and he drew in his breath with a long hiss. Danny then kicked Ben''s leg a few times, before hitting him hard on the chest, causing Ben to fall. Ben''s leg must have twisted as he fell. A sickening crack was heard and I saw Ben''s leg lying at such an awkward angle that it was apparent that it was broken. Ben let out a shocked gasp before fainting. Danny too, clutched his forehead and collapsed to the ground.\n\nA few pupils called for the ambulance immediately. After what seemed like an eternity, we could hear the incessant wailing of an ambulance. Paramedics climbed out of the ambulance, and ran towards Ben and Danny. The paramedics'' eyes widened in shock when they saw the horrendous bloodstains on Danny''s clothes. They carried both boys onto the stretchers and drove off. The crowd slowly dispersed, with some students shaking their heads as they walked away.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(15, 'The next day, our principal, Mrs Tan, talked about the fight. She said that the boys were lucky to have got to the hospital in the nick of time. Both boys would be suspended for a month as a result of their recalcitrant behaviour. Mrs Tan also told us not to follow in their footsteps. Ben and Danny were punished for their errant act and I hoped that I would never have to witness such a gruesome incident ever again.', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(16, 'It was a bright and sunny day. While walking home from his tuition class, David passed a street hawker along the roadside. The hawker was selling snacks and cakes, which were displayed in the open on a tray. \r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(17, 'Seeing the delicious-looking snacks, David felt hungry and bought a slice of kayo cake. He did not think much about the flies hovering around the food, nor was he worried about the dust settling on it. After David paid the hawker, he was handed his chosen slice of cake and given his change. The hawker used the same bare hand to handle the food and money.\n\nTwo hours later, David was at home doing his homework when he was hit with a bad stomachache. This was followed by diarrhoea and vomiting. Poor David was in agony.\n\nWhen his mother asked him what he had eaten earlier, David told her about the hawker from whom he had bought a slice of kayo cake. His mother was horrified to learn that David had bought food from an unhygienic, illegal street hawker. She told him that it was unsafe to consume food bought from such illegal hawkers. The food sold was exposed to the dust and flies around. The hawker also handled the food with his dirty hands. Imagine how many germs might be present on the food!\n\nDavid''s mother took him to the doctor''s and he recovered within a few days. After all the suffering he had to go through, he learnt his lesson and never dared to patronize illegal hawkers again.\n\nTo prevent others. from suffering a similar fate, David''s mother later lodged a complaint to the Ministry of Health about the illegal street hawker. Following the tip-off, health officers investigated the case and the illegal hawker WQS arrested and fined five thousand dollars for illegal hawking.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(18, 'He later changed his livelihood and became a lawful businessman. After his application for a licence was approved, he started selling food at a hawker centre. He followed all hygiene procedures and no one ever got foodpoisoni ain. ng after consuming his food again ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(19, 'I woke up to an exceptionally chilly morning. My entire body was shivering. When my bare feet stepped onto the icy cold surface of the ceramic flooring of my bedroom, I jumped up in shock. I put on my warm bedroom slippers and went to the windows to take a look. Outside, everything was covered in a blanket of pristine white snow. I could not believe that I was in Singapore. \r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(20, 'I turned on the radio to listen to the news broadcast. According to the news report, the El-lina monsoon had caused a temporary dip in temperatures to an all-time low for this region. The low temperature had caused the rain drops to freeze before they could reach the earth, resulting in snowfall for the very first time in Singapore. This freak phenomenon would probably last for only a day before the EI-lina monsoon made its way eastwards away from Singapore.\n\nWhile dad was frustrated with the massive traffic jams caused by the snow piling up on the street and Mum was displeased that the snow had ruined the flowers she had painstakingly planted, I was in seventh heaven. I put on my thermal underwear and warm clothes which were usually only used when my family travelled overseas to cold countries for holidays.\n\nAs it was a Saturday, I did not have to go to school. I rushed into my backyard to experience the snow. All around me was a blanket of dazzling white snow. It was no longer snowing, but the thick layer of snow that had fallen earlier had turned the landscae into a winter wonderland. I called out to my neighbours, Reuben and Ruth, to come and play in the snow with me. We tumbled around in the soft snow, getting our warm clothes covered with snowflakes. Reuben rolled a handful of snow into a ball and hurled it in my direction. Not to be outdone, I retaliated by throwing a bigger snowball at him. We were having a snow fight.\n\nRuth then suggested that we build a snowman. We made a large snowball for the body and a smaller one for the head. I used two black buttons for its eyes and a carrot for its nose. For a mouth, Reuben used a short piece of red ribbon. He stuck two twigs in the snowman''s sides for arms and wrapped his scarf around its ''neck''. To complete the look, Ruth placed a top hat above its head. When we were done, we stood back and admired our very own Mr Frosty. After that, our parents called us back indoors. They did not want us staying out in the cold for too long for fear that we might catch a chill.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(21, 'The next day when I arose, it felt warmer. Warm rays of gentle sunshine were streaming in through the windows. I rushed to look out of the window. The scarf, top hat, twigs, buttons, carrot and piece of ribbon lay in a wet heap on the ground. Mr Frosty had melted, along with most of the rest of the snow. Everything was wet and soggy. Although I wished that the snow would last for a few more days, I was glad that I had the unique experience of one day of snow in tropical Singapore.', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(22, 'I had been looking forward to our family trip to Tembusu for weeks. I was curial to have first-hand experience of how the natives lived. It was said that Ternbul was one of the last few places on earth yet to be transformed by modernization. was a nature lovers'' paradise, taking its visitors back to another era in history whe life was simple and man lived off the land. To me, it was an excellent opportunity to escape the hustle and bustle of city life in Singapore for a while. Images of a tranquil village setting where my family and I could relax and rejuvenate came to my mind. \r', 'Introduction', 'Overseas Trip'),
(23, 'The day of our departure finally dawned. I had packed my luggage well in advance and checked to ensure that I had brought along everything I needed. My parents, younger sister, Joyce, and I took a taxi to the airport. There, we caught a flight which would take us to our destination.\n\nThe flight was a pleasant one. I spent my time watching the in-flight movies and catching up on some sleep. Finally, our plane landed and it was time for us to disembark. There was a long queue at the customs and immigration checkpoint, but the queue moved quite quickly as each person in line was waved through after having his or her passport checked and stamped. My parents and Joyce went ahead of me and passed through immigration with no problems.\n\nNext, it was my turn. The immigration officer scrutinized my passport carefully. He then shook his head and said, "I''m sorry, your passport seems to have been tampered with. I can''t let you pass." What was I to do? My parents and sister were already on the other side.\n\nSensing that something was wrong, my father turned back and asked the immigration officer what the problem was. The officer told my father that my passport looked like it had been tampered with. He said that for security reasons, he had to deny my entry into the country.\n\nI was overcome with confusion. I remembered the time I had accidentally spilt some lemonade on my Passport during my last vacation. It had left a stain on my passport. However, the stain was barely visible. My photograph and personal particulars could still be seen clearly. Thinking that the stain might be the reason why I was denied entry into the country, I tried to explain the situation to the immigration officer. My father, too, assured him that my passport was genuine and added that I was after all a child who posed no threat to the country.\n\nAt first, the immigration officer seemed unrelenting. For a moment, I thought that my holiday dreams were dashed. The officer then said to my father that he fifty , was . able to let me through my father was willing to pay an ''administrative fee'' of US dollars. Catching his hint, my father agreed to pay the ''administrative fee'' and the officer happily stamped my passport and waved me through. Our whole family heaved a collective sigh of relief that our holiday could go on as planned.\n\nMy father later explained to me that in certain countries, the government officers were not very honest when dealing with the public. He believed that the immigration officer had detained me on purpose so that he could demand the ''administrative f fte` , which was actually a lame disguise for a bribe. The officer had resorted to such underhand tactics to earn some extra cash. However, as he was a government employee, his dishonest act had certainly brought shame upon his government. By preying on tourists, he was actually tarnishing the reputation of his government.\n\nIn Singapore, there are harsh penalties for government officers who accept bribes or commit other dishonest acts This has helped to bring about a clean and honest public service which is efficient and trustworthy. In fact, Singapore is known for being one of the least corrupt countries in the world. Many multinational companies have chosen to do business in Singapore for this reason.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Overseas Trip'),
(24, 'Apart from my encounter with the immigration officer, I enjoyed the other aspects of my holiday in Tembusu. Nevertheless, I was glad to return home to Ingopore after the trip. My overseas experience was indeed an eye-opener and had ode me even more proud to call myself a Singaporean. ', 'Conclusion', 'Overseas Trip'),
(25, 'I could not believe what I was seeing. Chaotic thoughts raced through my mind and my legs were trembling. I slumped to the ground, lapsing into unconsciousness ... \r', 'Introduction', 'Action'),
(26, 'I woke up in a hospital bed with a splitting headache. There was a bandage around my head. Mr Tan and my camp mates were all by my side, looking at me with anxious faces. I also spotted my parents in my ward. Mr Tan told me that I had been knocked unconscious by a wooden pole. My attacker was the big, burly man''s accomplice. He had seen me peeping through the window of the hut and realized that I had stumbled upon their hiding place where they held a girl they had kidnapped while awaiting her parents'' ransom. The kidnappers wanted to get rid of me so that I would not be able to tell the police what I had seen.\n\nFortunately, Mr Tan and my camp mates had come looking for me at just the right moment after they got wormed I failed to rejoin the group for a long time. From afar, they witnessed a man hitting me on the head. I then slumped onto the ground and the man went into the hut. Mr Tan Immediately crept up to me and carried me to safety without drawing the .attention of the men in the hut. Meanwhile, John, one of my camp mates, called the Police on his mobile phone. He also called for an ambulance.\n\nThe kidnappers were taken by surprise when the police stormed their hideout, arrested them and rescued the kidnapped girl. While these events unfolded, I was sent to hospital.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Action'),
(27, 'Lying in the safety of my hospital bed, I realized how close I had been to danger. If Mr Tan had not rescued me in time, I might have been dead. I was lucky to have survived. Curiosity killed the cat. In this case, my curiosity could have killed me. Yet, looking at things from another angle, I felt that it was as if it had been the intervention of fate. I was glad that not only was I safe, the little girl who had been kidnapped was, too. ', 'Conclusion', 'Action'),
(28, 'As my father and I were approaching our lift lobby, we saw a man push our elderly neighbour, Mrs Chin, into the lift. Before we could find out what was happening, the lift doors closed. My father and I were stunned. We had just returned from our morning jog and were anticipating a hearty breakfast, not the alarming scene that just unfolded before us.\r', 'Introduction', 'Humour'),
(29, '"Who is that man who just pushed Mrs Chin into the lift? Do you know him?" I asked my father anxiously. My father shook his head.\n\n"We had better make sure that Mrs Chin is alright," he said as he pressed the lift button urgently. We took the lift to our floor and were just in time to see the man entering Mrs Chin''s house.\n\n"Stop right there!" my father shouted and ran up to Mrs Chin''s flat to prevent the man from locking the gate.\n\n"Who are you and what are you doing in Mrs Chin''s house?" My father asked the man sternly.\n\n"Mr Wu?" Mrs Chin appeared beside the man with a surprised expression. "What is happening?"\n\nMy father explained how he had seen the man pushing her into the lift, so he was concerned for her safety. Mrs Chin laughed and said that the man was actually her nephew who had just returned from overseas! He had stumbled but appeared to us that he had pushed her. When we heard that, we felt a little embarrassed for making\n', 'Main Body', 'Humour'),
(30, 'such a big fuss, but were relieved that Mrs Chin was all right.', 'Conclusion', 'Humour'),
(31, 'I was waiting for my mother outside a shop in a shopping mall when I heard a strange notse. I had been tasked to accompany my mother and help her with\r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(32, 'her Christmas shopping that day. I had been constantly peering at my watch asI wanted to return home to catch my favourite television programme, but it\nhad not seemed likely as my mother did not look like she would be finishing any\ntime soon.\n\nI heard the strange noise again. What was that? It sounded like someone gasping for breath. I turned around and saw a girl in her teens clutching her chest and looking very pale. She was wheezing in agony and appeared to be in great pain. Some other shoppers had also noticed her. However, none of them went up to help her. I immediately ran into the shop to inform my mother. My mother was a former nurse and she would most probably know what to do in a situation like this, I thought.\n\nMy mother ran to the girl''s side and held her in her arms. My mother seemed to know what was wrong with the girl because she proceeded to search the girl''s bag and passed a tube-like device to her, which she promptly took and inhaled. After a while, colour returned to the girl''s face ond her breathing became normal. The girl thanked my mother for her help. It turned out that she had an asthma attack as there were too many people in the shopping mall. By this time, the crowd which had gathered around had dispersed. My mother offered to send the girl home to rest but she politely\ndeclined. However, my mother insisted and proceeded to hail a taxi. We sent\nthe girl home before returning home ourselves. As I opened the door to our house.\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(33, 'I inadvertently glanced at my watch again. It was almost eight. "Don''t worry, I recorded your television programme for you," said my mother. I smiled sheepishly and gave my mother a hug.', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(34, 'My friends and I were at a bus stop when we notice an elderly woman walking in an unsteady manner  towards us. Joel, Rachel and I were not expecting this. It was a hot and humid afternoon and we simply wanted to go home to take a shower after a tiring day in school. The elderly woman looked extremely pale and kept clutching her neck.\r', 'Introduction', 'Criminal Acts'),
(35, '“Are you alright?” I asked worriedly as I ran to help her to a seat at the bus stop.\n\n“My gold chain,” she cried. “It’s been stolen!”\n\nWhile I tried my best to calm her down, Joel ran back to school to find a teacher who could help us. Thankfully, our English teacher, Mrs Goh, was talking to some pupils at the school gate. She rushed over to render assistance. After we told her what had happened to the elderly woman, she promptly dialed for the police.\n\n“Are you heard in any way?” Mrs Goh asked the elderly woman.\n\n“I believe she’s in a state of shock,” Rachel answered. “Her hands are slightly bruised too.”\n\nThe police arrived shortly after. A police officer spoke gently to the elderly woman, who after recovering from her shock, gave a vivid description of the robbers’ appearance. After taking down the statements from Joel, Rachel, Mrs Goh and myself, they accompanied the elderly woman to a nearby clinic for a check up.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Criminal Acts'),
(36, 'During morning assembly the next day, our school principal praised the three of us  and Mrs Goh for our helpfulness. The elderly woman’s son, Mr Wee, had personally contacted our school principal. He was extremely grateful to us for assisting his mother in a time of distress. Although the thief was still at large, Mr Wee was relieved that his mother suffered only minor bruises to her hands. It was a proud moment for my friends and me and we gave one another a pat on the back.', 'Conclusion', 'Criminal Acts'),
(37, 'I was walking towards a shop. Suddenly, I saw people running out of the shop. They looked fearful arid some were even screaming hysterically. I had been tasked by my mother to purchase some groceries from the shop located near a park, and I was certainly not expecting this scene. Alarmed, I walked ahead cautiously and peeked into the shop to see what was causing the commotion. \r', 'Introduction', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(38, 'The whole shop was in disarray. Food items and tins that had been neatly stacked on the shelves now lag scattered on the floor. The contents of milk cartons and packets of fruit juices had also spilt on the floor. Some low shelves had also toppled over, adding to the mess in the shop. "What could have happened?" I wondered to myself as I slowly ventured into the shop.\n\nSuddenly, there was a chattering sound as well as some loud shouting, the voice I recognised as belonging to Mr Wong, the owner of the shop. Walking further into the shop, I caught sight of Mr Wong. His face was red with fury and there were beads of perspiration on his brow. He was yelling loudly and brandishing a broom at two monkeys in a corner of the shop. So that was what caused the mess in the shop! The monkeys looked aggressive and were chattering loudly. However, Mr Wong was not intimidated and stood his ground. As he approached the monkeys to chase them away, one of them scratched Mr Wong on his hand, causing it to bleed.\n\nI instinctively approached Mr Wong to help him. However, before I could do so, a hand held me back. "Get out of the shop, boy. It is too dangerous. We''ll take care of the monkeys.” The voice belong to a man wearing a shirt bearing logo of the National Parks Board. I stood outside the shop and with other curious onlookers, watched as the man and his three colleagues used a net to caputure the two monkeys. Mr Wong was treated for his injury by the paramedics who had also been called to the scene.\n\nAccording to the officer from the National Parks Board, the monkeys had entered the shop to look for food since it was near the park, which was probably where they were living, They advised Mr Wong to add more security measures like a fence as well as to lock his back door, as that was probably how the monkeys had entered the shop.\n\nThey also advised us not to feed the monkeys so that they would not be encouraged to venture from the park to seek food.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(39, 'Relieved that Mr Wong was all right, I joined everyone in helping him clean up his shop. I certainly hope that there would not be any more incidents of this kind in future. ', 'Conclusion', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(40, 'I was in a library when I heard an unusual sound that grew louder and louder. At first, I thought it was my imagination. However, the quietness of my surroundings confirmed that what I heard was real. I looked around me trying to locate the source of the sound. It seemed like it was coming from the corner of the library. It sounded like a machine had broken down and was getting more audible by the minute. Curious, I venture over to see what could be causing the strange sound. \r', 'Introduction', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(41, 'I had my answer soon enough  - the sound was coming from the air conditioner It seemed that it had malfunctioned and was making a loud whirring noise.  As I approached, I realised that the air conditioner was emitting smoke. Alarmed, I immediately went to inform librarian on duty about it. At this time, a crowd had gathered as they had probably heard the sound too. A librarian on duty asked the crowd to evacuate the library for safety reasons while another called for the electrician to come and check the malfunctioning air conditioner.\n\nI decided to wait outside the library to see what was causing the problem since I was in no hurry to return home. Soon, the electrician arrived carrying his box of tools and a librarian immediately directed him to the faulty air conditioner. After half an hour or so, the electrician reappeared. I overheard him telling the librarian that the cause of the problem was because the air conditioner was very old and had not been checked in a while. As a result, the machinery inside the air conditioner had corroded, causing it to malfunction.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(42, 'Subsequently, the library had to be closed for a week while maintenance work was carried out on all the air conditioners in the library. I was glad that the problem had been identified before any serious damage was caused. ', 'Conclusion', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(43, 'It was Monday afternoon and Sam was at Bishan MRT station, waiting for the train so that he could return home. He was extremely hungry and hoped that the train would arrive soon. He was standing on the platform as all the seats had been taken. After five minutes of waiting, the train arrived. The doors opened and the passengers alighted from the crowded train. Two boys rushed out of the train, while their mother, who was behind them, shouted at them to stop running. However, they did not heed their mother’s shouts and continued to run about the platform.\r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(44, 'Sam was preparing to board the train when the boys ran into this path. Quickly, he took a step back to avoid colliding into them. However, Sam tripped over a bag on the floor, causing him to lose his footing and fall. The folder he was holding also flew out of his hands, scattering his documents all over. They were documents that he had painstakingly prepared for an important meeting the next day!\n\nFull of anguish, Sam immediately picked himself up and tried to retrieve the papers. Some people who had noticed his predicament also offered a helping hand. The two boys had stopped in their tracks and were looking at Sam sheepishly. The mother, looking very apologetic, ordered her sons to help him. The boys obediently did as they were told this time. When Sam had gathered all his documents and placed them safely in his folder, the mother apologised for causing a delay as the train had already pulled out of the station. The boys knew they were in the wrong and offered their apologies too.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(45, 'By then, Sam’s anger had subsided so he forgave them, but not without warning the two boys not to play on the platform again as it could be dangerous. The next train arrived and Sam proceeded to board it, clutching his folder in his arms tighter this time.', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(46, 'Joel and Emma were hiding under a table in the living room. It was the last part of their plan to surprise their mother for it was her birthday. Earlier, they had wrapped the present they had purchased. It was a bag in her favourite colour. They had also thought of decorating the living room and had used their art supplies to point some pictures on the wall. They hoped that their mother would like their masterpiece.\r', 'Introduction', 'Celebrations'),
(47, 'Just as they were finishing up, they heard the door opening. Thinking that it was their mother, they had hidden under the table, waiting in anticipation to surprise her. As the figure entered the room, the two of them emerged from their hiding place and yelled, “Surprise!” Their excitement, however, soon died down when they realised that it was not their mother.\n“Dad! Why are you home so early?” asked Joel.\n\n“I left the office early to get a birthday cake for your mother,” answered Mr Fong, as he took in the sight before him. The wall had pictures in various colours drawn on it and the floor was littered with paints, brushes and wrapping paper.\n\n“Do you like our drawings? We spent a lot of time on it.” announced Emma proudly. As Mr Fong looked at what his children had done to the wall, he was sure that his wife would not be pleased. Looking at his watch, he made up his mind.\n\n“Kids, your work is lovely. However, I don’t think your mother would like her clean walls to have any other decorations. Why don’t we clean the place up before your mother gets home? Besides, nothing pleases your mother like a clean home. You can draw your pictures on your sketchbooks and present them to her instead,” Mr Fong said.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Celebrations'),
(48, 'The two children understood their father’s intentions. They knew what they had done was not very right-there were proper places to draw on and the wall was not one of them. Mr Fong used liquid detergent to wash away the paintings on the wall while the two children helped to clean up the mess on the floor. When Mrs Fong arrived home, she was greeted enthusiastically by her family. Joel and Emma presented their drawings and present to her and wished her a happy birthday. They were overjoyed when they saw how happy their mother was.', 'Conclusion', 'Celebrations'),
(49, 'Dave opened the door and was immediately greeted by cries of “Happy birthday!” He broke into a smile as he saw the beaming faces of his parents as well as his sister, Marilyn. His mother then took out a cake with twelve lighted candles and set it before Dave. He closed his eyes to make a wish before blowing out the candles to much applause from his family members.\r', 'Introduction', 'Celebrations'),
(50, 'After everyone had taken a slice of the cake, Marilyn announced excitedly, “It’s time for the presents!” She took out a card and a bookmark and presented them to Dave, declaring proudly, “I made them myself.” Dave thanked his sister for her gifts. Just then, his father walked into the hall with a large box. His mother made him sit down on a chair and covered his eyes with her hands for she wanted to create some suspense. After Dave’s father opened the box, his mother told him to feel inside the box and try to guess what the gift was.\n\nBy now, Dave could not wait. Could it be the laptop that he had been wishing for the past few months? He slowly lowered his hands into the box. Initially, he could not feel anything and realised that the gift was something small. Then, he felt it. A pang of disappointment crept into his heart as he realised that it was not a laptop. Rather it was something spherical, probably a football. Dave enjoyed playing football but he already owned two footballs. However, he tried to mask his feelings as he played along with his parents, trying to figure out what the gift was.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Celebrations'),
(51, 'When Dave’s mother finally removed her hands, Dave’s eyes gleamed when he saw what was in the box. It was not just any football; rather, it was a football bearing the signature of his favourite footballer! Dave shouted in glee as he hugged the football before thanking his parents for their gift. It was a memorable birthday gift that he would always treasure.', 'Conclusion', 'Celebrations'),
(52, 'Gareth marvelled at the graceful giraffes and their long slender necks. They looked so calm and peaceful feeding on the leaves on the trees. After admiring them for a while, he followed his mother to the next enclosure. Gareth''s mother had taken him to the zoo that day as a treat for doing well in his test. Gareth was very excited as he loved animals and this was an opportunity to see them up close. \r', 'Introduction', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(53, 'Gareth and his mother approached the elephant enclosure. There were two majestic grey elephants inside. A woman was feeding bananas to one of them while her young son hid behind her, seemingly afraid of the gentle elephant. As Gareth was observing the elephants, he noticed a man with a camera on his right. He was trying to take a photograph of a girl, probably his daughter.\n\nAll of a sudden, the elephant Gareth had been observing used its truck to suck some water from a small pool in its enclosure and mischievously sprayed the water at the man. This happened so fast that the man had no time to react, resulting in him being drenched. A little boy who was standing nearby pointed and laughed at the man’s plight. He found the scene amusing. The man did not look angry but seemed stunned by what had happened.\nAt this moment, two zookeepers who had seen what had happened came up to the elephant. As one of them chided the elephant for misbehaving, the other apologised profusely to the man and offered him a change of clothing which he gratefully accepted. The man managed to see the funny side of his situation and even managed a few chuckles, as he walked off with his daughter.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(54, 'Gareth was glad that he had not been the victim of the elephant’s prank. As he walked off with his mother, he made a mental note to himself not to wander too close to elephants in future.', 'Conclusion', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(55, '"Who''s there?" I asked in a nervous whisper. In the dark, all I could see  were the shadows of the tall trees that lined the deserted street. Feeling slightly unnerved, I resumed my walk. However, the minute I started  walking, the footsteps started again! I quickened my pace, and the  footsteps got faster as well! My pulse raced as my imagination ran wild.  Was a masked murderer stalking me? Was a phantom pursuing me? I started to run, but the footsteps pursued me relentlessly. There was a crowded food centre up ahead. I decide to seek safety there..\r', 'Introduction', 'Humour');
INSERT INTO `astarcompo_templates` (`templateID`, `template`, `templateType`, `theme`) VALUES
(56, 'The food centre was well lit and more importantly, it was packed. The stalker would not dare to try anything there. I took a few steps forward, confident that I had shaken off the unwanted attention. To my horror, the unmistakable "tippity-tap" still echoed behind me. I decided to face my fears. I took a deep breath and turned around. "Who are you? What do you want?" I shouted. The diners sitting around stared at me in alarm. "Show yourself!" I demanded.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Humour'),
(57, 'In the ensuing silence, a little boy''s voice caught my undivided attention.  "Mum," he began, "look at that girl''s shoe. It''s broken!" With a growing sense of dread, I looked down at my shoes. True enough, the rubber sole had detached itself from my right shoe. Tentatively, I took a few steps forward. "Tippity-tap, tippity-tap," came the sound again. This time, a sense of embarrassment replaced my initial feeling of fear. It was I who was making the sound of the unidentified footsteps all along! My face  flushed and I smiled apologetically. I wished that the ground would crack open and swallow me up. I resolved never to allow panic to rule my actions again. It would be a long while, if ever, before I let my imagination run wild again.', 'Conclusion', 'Humour'),
(58, 'Ding Dong! I dragged myself off the sofa and went to see who was at the door. It was a Sunday afternoon and my whole family was engrossed in the suspenseful movie that was being shown on television. \r', 'Introduction', 'Criminal Acts'),
(59, 'Two men dressed in jeans and T-shirts were standing at the door. They smiled warmly and said that they were from the Public Utilities Board. They had come to check for leaking pipes. As I was eager to get back to the movie, I hurriedly pointed out the bathrooms to them and left them to do their  job. Ten minutes later, the men reappeared. With looks of extreme satisfaction on their faces, they informed us that they had completed their inspection. Then, they left.\n\n"Such hardworking people," remarked my mother, "they even work on Sundays!" When Father heard this, a shadow of doubt flickered across his face. He turned to me and asked whether the men had shown me their identification passes. I shook my head. With a furrowed brow, Father rushed into his bedroom. He headed straight for Mother''s jewellery box and flung it open. Mother and I gave cries of despair when we saw that the box had been emptied! All of Mother''s treasured gold jewellery was gone! They had even taken the ornate mother-of-pearl pendant that had belonged to Granny!\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Criminal Acts'),
(60, 'Father quickly called the police. Two hours later, we received the news that the crooks had been arrested and that the stolen items had been recovered. The sense of relief that we felt was beyond description. I felt very ashamed that I had been so careless as to let a robbery take place right under my nose. Nevertheless, I learnt an important lesson that day. I learnt that it is always necessary to be extra careful before letting strangers into the house. ', 'Conclusion', 'Criminal Acts'),
(61, 'I was curled up comfortably on a plush armchair with my favourite mystery book. Just as the plot thickened, I was rudely interrupted by my sister, Lisa, who came barging into the room. At the top of her voice she shouted, "You despicable creature! You stole my money!"\r', 'Introduction', 'Family'),
(62, 'Lisa is famous for her quick temper. This time she was seething with rage because she was convinced that I had taken her money. In actual fact. I was completely innocent! "Lisa," I said in a calm and composed manner, "perhaps you misplaced your money or dropped it in school. Please don''t accuse me without any evidence." However, Lisa refused to be reasoned with. She retorted by saying, "Don''t try to deny it You took my ten dollar note to buy a new mystery book!" As Lisa continued to hurl accusations at me, I felt the tips of my ears tinge with anger. I felt deeply hurt by her unjust treatment.\n\nThat evening, as Lisa and I were setting the table for dinner, our mother said, "Lisa, I found this ten dollar note in your skirt pocket. Please be more careful in the future." On hearing this, Lisa''s face turned crimson and she gave me an apologetic smile.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Family'),
(63, 'The next day, Lisa came into my room with something in her hands. It was the new mystery book I had been saving up for! "I''m really sorry," Lisa said meekly, "I should have trusted you. Please accept this gift as an apology." I forgave Lisa and we agreed to trust each other from then on. ', 'Conclusion', 'Family'),
(64, 'As I was walking home from school one sweltering afternoon, an object on the ground caught my eye. It was a small, shiny box that glittered in the sunlight. Full of curiosity, I peered at it before deciding that it would be perfect for my collection of odds and ends. As I bent over to pick it up, a thought pricked my conscience. What if the box belonged to someone? Should I bring it to the nearest police post? However, the box was too beautiful to resist. I darted my eyes to the left and right. The coast was clear. There was no one in sight. T quickly seized the shining box. \r', 'Introduction', 'Criminal Acts'),
(65, 'Just then, a tall man grabbed my arm. A wave of panic swept over me. Before I could say or do anything, he ordered me to drop the box. Feeling very frightened, I quickly obeyed. He then pulled me behind a large pillar. In an urgent whisper, he explained that he was a plain-clothes policeman and that the box I had picked up contained ransom money for a kidnapper. "We are waiting for him to turn up so that we can arrest him," he concluded.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Criminal Acts'),
(66, 'Soon, a dishevelled character appeared. When he spotted the box, he broke into an evil grin. As he bent over to retrieve it, several policemen jumped out of their hiding places. The man was encircled. He had walked right into their trap! In no time, the efficient policemen had the criminal handcuffed. I was exhilarated to have witnessed such an adventure. I could hardly wait to tell my friends about it! ', 'Conclusion', 'Criminal Acts'),
(67, 'The sun hung in the sky like a blazing fireball. It was a perfect day for a swim with my friends, Lily and Amelia, at the neighborhood swimming complex. We were laughing and splashing in the cool waters when I suddenly noticed something big and white nose-diving towards us. "Look out!" I cried. \r', 'Introduction', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(68, 'There was a tremendous splash as the object hit the water. Lily, Amelia and I moved cautiously towards it to get a closer look. Peering up at us was a beautiful white swan. It was a sight to behold! Unfortunately, its long graceful neck was drooping to one side. "The poor swan is injured," Lily said, pointing to its great white wing that stuck out awkwardly. "We should take it to a vet," Amelia chipped in. By this time, a small crowd had gathered around us. A distinguished looking gentleman with greying hair and spectacles stepped forward. With a charming smile, he introduced himself as Dr. Mike Tan, a trained vet. l le offered to take a look at the swan. My friends and I gently lifted the swan out of the water so that Dr. Tan could tend to it. After a quick but thorough inspection, Dr. Tan explained that the swan''s wing had been injured in mid-flight and that it needed some time to recuperate. He decided to take it back to his clinic. He thanked us for our help and we bade the kind doctor and the swan farewell.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(69, 'Two weeks later, I received a call from Dr. Tan. He said that the swan was in the pink of health. He invited my friends and I to visit him at his clinic. At the clinic, we saw the swan in its full splendour. Its feathers were white and shiny and it even flapped its wings excitedly! We exchanged smiles. In our hearts, we felt proud that we had helped a feathered-friend in need. ', 'Conclusion', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(70, 'I was just about to board the MRT train when I heard a voice calling my name. Alarmed, I turned around. Aunty Ho, the mother of my best friend, Peter, was waving_ frantically in the crowd. "Tom! Tom!" she cried. It was obvious that she was in great distress. \r', 'Introduction', 'Family'),
(71, '"What is the matter, Aunty Ho?" I asked anxiously as I walked towards her. Peter had been absent from school that day, perhaps he was seriously ill or injured. "Oh Tom," Aunty Ho said, "Peter is missing! He did nut come home from school yesterday. No one knows where he is! Please Tom, you have to help me find him!"\n\nWhen I saw the tears coursing down her cheeks, fear crept into my heart. My mind raced with wild thoughts of all the horrible things that could have happened to Peter. Had he been kidnapped? Perhaps he had been brutally murdered! I took deep gulps of air and tried not to panic. Suddenly, I remembered that Peter had been gravely depressed after he had received his report card the day before. He had not performed up to mark and was sorely disappointed. Could he have run away because of that? If that was the case, I knew exactly where Peter would be. I led Aunty Ho to the beach where Peter and I had once discovered a cave perfect for hiding in. There, true to my instinct, we found an exhausted and famished Peter. As soon as he saw us, Peter''s eyes widened in  surprise. Aunty Ho gave a cry of relief and embraced him.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Family'),
(72, 'It was a tearful reunion between mother and son. As they embraced each other, it dawned on me that nothing is more precious than the love and support of family and friends. ', 'Conclusion', 'Family'),
(73, 'Tears of horror streamed down her pale and sallow face. Her round eyes were wide with terror and her lips trembled with fear. She tugged at my hand, wordlessly imploring me to follow her. "What happened little girl?" I asked repeatedly. "I found something," she replied in an almost inaudible whisper. \r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(74, 'She led me to a stream that gurgled restlessly. There, lying motionless in the shallow water was a grotesque human figure. My heart palpitated wildly and I bid back a scream.  I felt icy fingers of fear crawl up and down my spine. It was a horrendous sight. The corpse was mercilessly mutilated. There was a deep gash in her neck where she had been slashed. Her face was blue and her eyes stared up blankly at the sky. The little girl who led me to this brutal sight stood frozen. She was clearly traumatized by her grisly discovery.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(75, 'I quickly called the police and related what I had seen. They arrived at the scene with lightning speed. The detective informed me that the deceased woman often went to the park and sat listlessly by the stream li)r long hours. "Why?" I asked curiously. With a sad shake of his head, he said that her young daughter had drowned in the stream several months ago and that the woman had never forgiven herself for letting that happen. At that moment, I remembered the little girl who had led me to the corpse. I quickly scanned the area but she was nowhere to be seen. After a while, the detective took a photograph out from his case file and showed it to me. "This was her daughter," he said sorrowfully. I looked at the photograph. My blood froze. The little girl''s pale face stared back at me. ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(76, 'The crash echoed in the peaceful silence and the scream pierced my ears. I rushed out of my bedroom in astonishment and ran to the living room. A scene of chaos greeted my eyes.\r', 'Introduction', 'Action'),
(77, 'The fish tank had broken. Shattered glass was strewn across the carpeted floor and a pool of water was rapidly spreading. My three pet goldfish squirmed and wriggled helplessly on the floor. Their beautiful fantails and shimmering golden scales quivered as they thrashed about. My little brother, Ravi, who had let loose the blood-curdling scream, stood transfixed in the corner. I quickly filled a large pail with water and scooped the goldfish to safety. Back in the water, the fish quickly regained their graceful poise. Then, I carefully picked my way through the trail of glass and turned towards Ravi. Deliberately keeping my voice calm, I asked him to explain what had happened. Ravi''s face was a picture of sorrow and shame. "I accidentally hit the tank with my Frisbee," he said in dismay. I checked to make sure that he was unhurt. Then, I reprimanded him for playing with the Frisbee in the house.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Action'),
(78, 'When Mother came home, she was furious to see the mess Ravi had made. As a punishment, she decided to withhold his allowance until there was enough money to pay for a new tank. Poor Ravi was devastated! He had learnt his lesson the hard way. From then on, he realized that there is always a right time and place for everything and that without a doubt, the Frisbee belongs outside. ', 'Conclusion', 'Action'),
(79, 'I tilted my head to the left. A faint rustling sound was coming from the bushes behind me. I was intrigued and decided to investigate. My hands quivered with excitement as I parted the thick branches. I wondered what I would see. Then, I heard a soft mew. Nestled behind the bush was a family of cats!\r', 'Introduction', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(80, 'A newborn litter of kittens was cuddling with a mother cat. The mother cat had a shiny coat of fine golden Fur. She looked tired but content. I stood entranced  by the blissful scene before me. As I moved closer, I noticed that the mother cat had a tag around its neck. Upon closer inspection, I realized that a name and address was inscribed on the tag. It read: Missy, 10 Olive Grove. There was only one thing to do. I had to return the cat and her kittens to its owner. I found a large box and gently lifted the mother cat and her kittens into it. They did not resist. Perhaps they were aware of my good intentions. As I made my way towards Olive Grove, I wondered if I would receive a reward. "Maybe even a sum of money!" I thought gleefully. Within no time, I arrived at my destination. I knocked on the door and was greeted by an elderly couple. The minute they saw Missy and her litter, their faces lit up with joy.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(81, 'I realized that they were a lonely couple who treated Missy like a part of their family. Their eyes lighted up excitedly when they saw the newborn kittens. They thanked me from the bottom of their hearts and apologized that they could not afford to reward me. However, I felt that I had already received the best reward. The warmth and satisfaction that came with reuniting the couple with their beloved cat was far better than anything money could buy! ', 'Conclusion', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(82, 'Beads of perspiration trickled down my forehead. I knitted my brow in concentration. The algebra test I was sitting for was a very difficult one. Silently, I wished for something to happen so that the test would be postponed. \r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(83, 'As I struggled with the sums, some teachers rushed past my classroom with anxious faces. They whispered nervously amongst themselves. Distracted by all the action, I wondered what the fuss was about Suddenly, Mr Lim, our science teacher, burst into our classroom. He looked disconcerted and tense. The whole class stared at him in surprise as he rattled on like a train. He spoke so rapidly that we could barely catch a word he was saying! Mrs Lee, our math teacher, asked him to slow down. Mr Lim swallowed hard and said, "There''s a bomb in the school! Everyone file out now!" What resulted was a flurry of activity as we quickly and efficiently made our way to the school field. Fear and anxiety was written on everyone''s face. When I wished for something to happen, a bomb scare was the last thing on my mind!\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(84, 'In no time, the school building was evacuated and everyone was assembled on the school field. The noise made by the sea of pupils was like the heavy drone of busy bees. Mr Gopal, the principal, ordered us to calm down. We were told that firemen were in the building searching for the bomb. With a feeling of dread, we waited for the outcome. After what seemed like an eternity, the team of firemen reported that they had located the bomb and detonated it in the nick of time. A wave of relief swept across the field. I gazed in rapt admiration at the heroes. Thanks to their selflessness and courage, we were all safe. ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(85, 'The sound of hawkers boasting their best bargains rang out loudly. The aroma of exotic spices tickled my nose. My brother, Andrew, and I were accompanying our mother on her weekly trip to the market. This weekend, it was particularly crowded. Hordes of busy buyers pushed and shoved us from all directions. "Alison, take your brother and wait for me outside. It''s safer there," said my mother. I nodded obediently and led Andrew to a bench outside the market.\r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(86, 'The bench was right in front of a brightly-lit fish shop. From where I was seated, I could see the fish swimming languidly in their tanks. It was a mesmerizing sight. There were graceful angelfish, electric blue guppies and topaz-coloured seahorses. I sat hypnotized as I watched the fish. I wondered what it would be like to lead their carefree lives. Wanting to share my thoughts with Andrew, I turned towards him. Alas, he was gone! Frantic with worry, I called out his name repeatedly but there was no reply. Just then, my mother came out of the market. When I told her that Andrew was missing, she dropped her bags in alarm. Together, we searched all over for him. Just as we were about to lose hope, a familiar voice cried, "I tricked you!" I whirled around and saw Andrew''s cheeky face peeking out from behind an enormous oak tree. "Watching the both of you running around like that was hilarious!" he giggled.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(87, 'Mother''s face turned a deep shade of red. She gave Andrew a sound scolding for pulling such a naughty prank on us. Andrew''s face was marked with remorse. He promised that he would never do such a thing again. ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(88, 'I sank contentedly into my favourite armchair and looked forward to my favourite local comedies on television. Just as I was getting comfortable, I detected a faint whiff of smoke. Then, I heard the scream of my next door neighbour, a newly married young woman by the name of Annie. I jumped up and ran to the rescue.\r', 'Introduction', 'Action'),
(89, 'When I got there wispy strands of smoke were snaking out from beneath her front door. Other neighbours who had heard her call for help had also gathered to offer their assistance. People ran along the corridors, absorbed in the task of carrying buckets of water to the scene. The whole place was a picture of pandemonium and panic! Meanwhile, Annie continued to cry for help. A few of us kicked the front door open and ran into the kitchen. T here, we found p oar Annie bawling uncontrollably. On the table in front of her was a very charred roast chicken giving off a last tiny flame. This was the cause of the great panic! I filled a jug with water and doused the chicken. Sizzle! Splat! Fizz. Just like that, the ''fire'' was put out. "Wah! Wall! I just wanted to impress my mother-in-law!" bawled the failed cook. I found the whole situation quite hilarious and tried my best to suppress the laughter welling up inside of me.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Action'),
(90, 'The men who had rushed to the rescue with buckets of water exchanged sheepish looks. They stared foolishly at the charred chicken and the crying woman before hastily making their way out. I patted Annie on the shoulder and led her to the living room. With a small sigh, I picked up the yellow pages. I flipped to the caterers'' section and solemnly handed it to the startled young wife. ', 'Conclusion', 'Action'),
(91, 'Ring! Ring! I rushed to answer the phone. It was my friend, Daniel. He was so excited that he could barely get the words out of his mouth. "Come quickly! Hurry!" he exclaimed breathlessly. "What? Why?" I asked, astounded. "Meet me in the park! Quick!" was all he said in reply.\r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(92, 'Daniel''s excitement was contagious. I ran all the way to the park. Once there, I spotted Daniel crouching beside a drain. His face was encrusted with dirt  but he was grinning from ear to ear. -What happened''?" I asked. Daniel flashed a grin at me and said, "I climbed into the drain to retrieve my ball. You will not believe what I found while I was down there! It was tucked away in the far corner, wedged between the crevices." With an air of secrecy, he extracted a velvet pouch from his pocket and handed it to me. I took a peek inside. A flash of brilliant white light hit my eyes and dazzled me. I gaped in awe. The pouch was filled to the brim with diamonds!\n\n"I''m rich!" exclaimed Daniel with a glint in his eye. I was flabbergasted. "You''re not going to keep them, are you?" I asked incredulously. Daniel shook his head reluctantly. "I wish I could," he said with a wistful sigh, "but I''m sure the owner misses his jewels." That same afternoon, we reported our find to the police. Daniel was a little sore at having to part with the diamonds but he knew that it was the right thing to do.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(93, 'A week later, Daniel called again. Once again, he was bursting with excitement. The man to whom the diamonds belonged had given Daniel a two hundred dollar reward! I was overjoyed for him. Indeed, honesty is always the best policy! ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(94, '"James, there''s a parcel for you. It was on the doorstep when I came home," said my mother. "Oh wow!" I gushed. I was delighted, especially since it was neither my birthday nor Christmas. \r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(95, 'Thrilled, I picked up the parcel. It was wrapped in brown paper and had my name scrawled on it in big bold letters. "Hmm," I mused, "I wonder who it''s from." With mounting excitement, I ripped off the wrapping paper. Underneath was an expensive-looking black leather box with an intricate gold clasp. With trembling fingers. I undid the clasp. The sight that greeted me made my heart skip a beat. Lying there, nestled between yellow satin, was a gun. A genuine gun complete with a row of bronze coloured bullets! I exhaled slowly and touched the shining object. A chill ran down my spine. "Mum! Come quick!" I cried. When my mother saw the gun, her hand flew to her mouth in astonishment. "Don''t touch it," she ordered. She quickly reached for the telephone and dialled 999.\n\nWhat ensued was a whirlwind of activity. Inspectors in smart black suits together with policemen in crisp blue uniforms came to our house. They piled me with questions. After hours of interrogation, they finally took their leave, taking the parcel and the gun with them.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(96, 'A few days later, I was informed that the gun was smuggled into our country by a band of underworld gangsters. It was meant for a triad member who shared my name. By some mistake, the gun had ended up with me. It was because of this that the police had managed to track down the gangsters. I was greatly relieved that the whole episode was over. Deep in my heart, I applauded the crime fighters who had handled the case so efficiently. ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(97, 'Sounds of merriment filled the entire house. It was my twelfth birthday party and there was an unmistakable mood of celebration in the air. Suddenly, the doorbell rang. As all my guests had already arrived, I wondered who it could be.\r', 'Introduction', 'Family'),
(98, 'I raced to the door. Standing there looking back at me was a girl who was a mirror image of myself! I could not believe my eyes! I stood there with my mouth agape, spellbound. My parents invited the girl and the two adults accompanying her to come in. I searched my parents'' faces for signs of shock and disbelief, but all I could see there was joy. It was as if they had been expecting this body double of me to turn up all along "What''s going on?" I asked in disbelief.\n\nAfter an uncomfortable silence, my father spoke. "Jane, this is Mr and Mrs Leong," he said, introducing the two adults. After a dramatic pause he said, "This is your twin sister, Jeanette." I felt hot tears prick my eyes as I struggled to understand what I had just heard. "How can this be?" I asked in astonishment. My mother took a deep breath and explained the whole story to me. She told me that my real parents had died in a car crash shortly after the birth of Jeanette and I. While she and father had adopted me, Mr and Mrs Leong had adopted Jeanette. The four of them had agreed to reunite us on our twelfth birthday.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Family'),
(99, 'A whirlwind of emotion raced through my mind. As I looked at my long lost twin sister, Jeanette, I felt an indescribable bond between us. With tears of joy, we embraced each other. We had so much to catch up on! It was a new beginning for the both of us. Finding Jeanette has been the most precious birthday present I have ever received. ', 'Conclusion', 'Family'),
(100, 'Last weekend, my family and I decided to spend a day relaxing at me beach. We were lounging on our straw mats and enjoying the peaceful lull of the waves and the soothing cool breeze when all of a sudden, our peace was shattered. Cries for help filled the air as people began running frantically in different directions.\r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(101, 'My family and I were greatly appalled. We wondered what could have created such frenzy. Then I saw a dishevelled man chasing after the people. His hair was in wild disarray, his clothes were dirty and he was wielding an axe that glinted in the sunlight! A menacing smile lurked on  his face. We were all in grave danger. I knew that I had to act fast before someone got hurt. Without a second thought, I got on my feet and ran towards the man. In no time, I was face to face with him. His eyes flashed wildly as he stared at me. Without warning, he charged towards me with his deadly weapon. I had nothing to defend myself with except for my black-belt karate skills. I executed a powerful hand chop to make him drop the axe. I then delivered a merciless flying kick straight to his stomach. He fell to the ground moaning in agony.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(102, 'Soon the police arrived. They explained that the man had escaped from the mental hospital that very day. They praised me for my courage and quick-thinking. They even wanted to reward me with a medal, but I humbly declined. Knowing that no one had been hurt was enough of a reward for me. As my family and I headed home, we felt tired but happy with the way the day had unfolded. ', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(103, 'I had just returned home from school and was taking the lift up to the ninth storey. When I stepped out of the lift, I saw two men with their backs towards me standing in front of Aunty and Uncle Loh''s flat. Aunty and Uncle Loh were my relatives as well as my neighbours. At present, their flat was unoccupied as they were both vacationing overseas. I was just about to offer my assistance to the men, when the taller of the two started struggling violently with the Loh''s front door!\r', 'Introduction', 'Criminal Acts'),
(104, 'The phrase "Daylight robbery" popped into my mind. I stood rooted  to the spot and quivered with dread  as I imagined all the horrors that could unfold. However, when the men turned around, all my misgivings vanished instantly. Standing in front of me was none other than Theodore, my cousin, who was supposed to be overseas studying!\n\nTheodore rushed over to me and affectionately ruffled my hair. He embraced me in an enthusiastic bear-hug. "I''m really happy to see you!" he exclaimed. He then introduced the young man at his side as Bala. Bala was his friend who had come to spend the holidays with him. After we had exchanged pleasantries, I informed Theodore that his parents were not expecting him back for another week. As, such, they had not yet returned from their vacation. With a wry smile, Theodore explained that due to a misunderstanding he had arrived sooner than scheduled.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Criminal Acts'),
(105, 'In the end, Theodore and Bala spent the week at my place. My whole family welcomed them wholeheartedly. I must admit that I was a little sad when Theodore returned to his own home, even though his home was only two doors away! ', 'Conclusion', 'Criminal Acts'),
(106, 'I watched, aghast, as the beautiful ring with the glittering red ruby sank slowly to the bottom of the swollen river. I was leaning over the railing, admiring the sparkling gem, when all of a sudden, the ring slipped off my finger and disappeared into the murky waters below. I had pleaded with my sister to lend it to me. She would never forgive me if I went home without it! \r', 'Introduction', 'Family'),
(107, 'There was no other alternative. I mustered up my courage and dived into the river. The water was freezing cold! suddenly, the current grew stronger and I was dragged along by it, I panicked and thrashed about  ineffectually. In my flustered state, I swallowed large volumes of dirty water. Soon, it became difficult to draw a breath and I struggled just to stay afloat. Within no time, my limbs felt like lead. Against my will, I started to sink. Then, everything went black.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Family'),
(108, 'I woke up in the hospital. A passerby had rescued me and brought me there. My sister was sitting by my bedside. Her brow was creased with worry. "You foolish girl!" she said, "What on earth were you doing?" With a sinking heart, I told her that I had lost the precious ring she had entrusted to me. I expected her to blow up. Instead, she hugged me and said, "You are more precious to me than all the jewels in the world! Your life is something that money cannot buy." When I heard her loving words, I felt a warm glow in my heart. Indeed, nothing is more precious than the gift of life and the love of your family.', 'Conclusion', 'Family'),
(109, 'I could tell that Mother was trying hard to control her emotions even as fresh tears rolled down her cheeks. I felt afraid. I had never seen her in such a state before. In a voice choked with grief, she said, "Son, your lather was involved in a serious accident. He has slipped into a coma. The doctors think that he might not make it through the night."\r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(110, 'I could not believe my ears. My mind whirled and my heart pounded. I felt as if the earth was crashing down around me. My first instinct was to deny what I had heard. However, as Mother''s tear-stained face stared back at me, the ugly truth sank in. My heart contracted painfully.\n\nMy father had been involved in a car accident. He was driving along the expressway when another motorist, travelling at high speed, lost control of his vehicle and rammed straight into him. The sudden impact was so great that my father was flung through the windscreen of his car. He suffered multiple injuries, the worse of which was the blow to his head. The driver of the speeding vehicle was drunk. His inconsiderate behaviour had placed my family in our present predicament.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(111, 'There was nothing we could do but pray and wait. Mother and I spent a harrowing and helpless night outside the hospital''s intensive care unit. We paced up and down. Time seemed to come to a standstill. Finally, at the break of dawn, the news we had been praying for came. Father was going to pull through. He was going to live! Eventually, Father was able to resume life as normal. This experience taught me a valuable lesson - driving while drunk is unforgivable. ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(112, 'There goes that snobbish new girl," Jane, my classmate, hissed. I fumed around and there was Melissa. As usual, her nose was in the air mid she had a look of superiority on her face. Melissa had transferred to our school a week ago and the whole class felt intimidated by her apparent arrogance. Nevertheless, as the class monitor, I felt that it was my duty to help her fit in. With a warm and welcoming smile on my lace, I walked towards her.\r', 'Introduction', 'School and You'),
(113, '"Hi Melissa, my name is Meiling. I am pleased to meet you," I said, extending my hand. However, Melissa simply gave me an icy glare and turned her back on me. I felt rejected and discouraged, but I was determined to befriend her. I volunteered to take her on a school tour and even asked her to join me for recess. However, regardless of how hard I tried, Melissa continued to give me the cold shoulder. Just as I was about to give up, I discovered a side of Melissa that I had never seen before.\n\nAs I was walking home from school, I saw Melissa offer her assistance to an old lady. As she helped the old lady, her face radiated with warmth and compassion. I could hardly believe my eyes! When I confronted her about her split personality she burst into tears! She told me that she had been cheated and hurt by her friends at her old school. Thus, she was reluctant to make new friends for fear of being hurt again.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'School and You'),
(114, 'My heart went out to her. Not knowing what else to do, I gave her a hug. I told her that I would be a good friend to her if she would let me. It was a heartwarming moment when Melissa gave me a small smile in return. I was filled with a glimmer of hope for the start of a new friendship. ', 'Conclusion', 'School and You'),
(115, 'The whistle blew. The crowd erupted in thunderous cheers as we launched a fierce attack. It was the semi-final match of the National School Basketball Championship. Our team was up against the team of our rival school. Tension hung like a thick cloud in the air. On the court, my teammates and I knew that we had to emerge victorious in this crucial match in order to clinch a place in the finals. \r', 'Introduction', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(116, 'It was a close fight. Both sides were evenly matched. After some time, it was still a draw. Suddenly, our team captain, I im, secured possession of the ball. Just as he was about to pass it to me, he was pushed roughly from behind and fell to his knees. He got up immediately and confronted the player who had pushed him. His eves flashed with anger and his cheeks flushed with fuly. The player was the captain of the other team, Sean. He stared back at Tim with a steady gaze and an indignant  expression. "It was an accident," Sean claimed. Upon hearing this blatant lie, Tim quaked with rave. He raised his fist in the air and delivered a solid punch to Sean''s jaw. Sean retaliated with a blow. The referee blew his whistle repeatedly but the captains ignored him. Totally oblivious, they continued their brawl. Finally, the other players and I managed to drag the two hotheaded captains apart. The referee was furious at them. He delivered a crushing punishment. Both teams were disqualified from the championship!\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(117, 'When the news sank in, both captains were filled with regret and remorse. However, it was too late. Their inability to control their tempers had shattered their teams'' championship dreams. ', 'Conclusion', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(118, 'It was a breathtaking evening. The dazzling stars twinkled in the heavens as the full moon shimmered beautifully. It was the night of the mid-autumn festival and my father, brother and I exchanged happy smiles as we strolled along the cobbled pathway of the neighbourhood park. \r', 'Introduction', 'Celebrations'),
(119, 'All around us, happy families lifted their multi-coloured paper lanterns high in the air. Each lantern gave off a mystical glow as the candle secured within burned steadily. My brother and I had opted for the smaller lanterns. Surprisingly, it was Father who had chosen an elaborately decorated and enormous lantern which required not one, but three candles! We looked on with great delight as he paraded in the park with his lantern proudly held up high. All the children we came across gaped in open-mouthed admiration at Father''s lantern. Unfortunately, a few minutes later, disaster struck! As we walked on, Father''s fabulous lantern suddenly burst into flames! Aghast, Father gave a cry of dismay  as the fire devoured his beauty. We had to ask him three times before he agreed to let go of the burning lantern! Within seconds, all that remained of it was a smoldering pile of ashes.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Celebrations'),
(120, 'The next year, we all expected Father''s lantern to be of a more sensible size. To our amazement, the lantern he chose was twice the size of last year''s! Although we teased him, we admired his great spirit. ', 'Conclusion', 'Celebrations'),
(121, 'I crept stealthily into the pet shop, tempted by the singing birds and the irresistible gold fish. I had not eaten for nearly two days. Scavenging around for scraps of food was getting more and more difficult. I was Famished. However, as soon as the owner of the pet shop saw me, this mangy, scrawny cat with a matted coat of fur, he gave a yelp, seized his broom and chased me away. I scrambled for cover behind a stack of boxes. \r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(122, 'I was petrified of human beings. They always treated me with contempt and cruelty. Once, a group of nasty boys had tried to cut off my tail! Ever since I was born, I roamed the streets as a stray cat. Most of the lime, I was cold, hungry and lonely. As I was wallowing in these thoughts of self-pity, I heard a gentle voice say, "You poor thing, you must be very frightened." I looked up to see a young girl with an angelic Face beaming down at me. I had noticed her earlier as she was admiring the fish at the front of the shop. To my great delight, she picked me up and declared that I would make the perfect pet!\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(123, 'With the consent of her father, the gentle girl with the smiling eyes brought me home. She gave me a warm, soapy bath and fed me a saucer of'' creamy milk. I felt as if I was in heaven! In the days that followed, she took great care of me. When it was cold, she cuddled me. Whenever I felt hungry, she served me scrumptious meals. I felt that I had finally found a place to call home. ', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(124, 'I paced up and down the void deck with my older brother, Josh. Five minutes to show time! I looked at Josh and rolled my eyes. I still could not get used to his new image. His hair was spiked and styled with gel. He had replaced his spectacles with contact lenses and was wearing a bright yellow T-shirt with tight fitting pants. I sighed. The people that strolled past cast bemused glances in our direction. \r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(125, 'This was all part of Josh''s grand scheme to impress the pretty, new girl who had just moved into our block. Her name was Emma and Josh had fallen for her instantly. In an attempt to impress her, he had cajoled me  into assisting him with the scene we were about to stage. It was simple enough. Every afternoon, Emma went down to the void deck at four o''clock. Today she would witness a supposedly powerful and macho  Josh ticking me off for a wrongdoing. Josh hoped that this would make Emma swoon in admiration. DING! The bell signalled the arrival of the lift. I quickly got into position. Josh towered over me and passionately launched into the speech which he had been practicing all week. "Don''t ever let me catch you doing that again!" he concluded, pointing a threatening finger in my face. From the corner of my eye, I saw Emma''s startled face looking in our direction. What happened next was far from  our expectations.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(126, 'Emma walked straight up to us and took me protectively to her side. She reprimanded Josh for abusing his position of authority. "This is no way to teach him, Josh," she chided gently. Her eyes widened in shock as she took in Josh''s new look. Poor Josh, he looked crestfallen! In the end, Josh and I learnt that power itself is not attractive. It is how you use it that counts. ', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(127, '"She''s coming round," whispered Candy, my classmate. We were sitting at the bedside of Sulin, our friend. True enough, Sulin stirred slightly as her eyes fluttered open. "Hi there," we murmured. The corners of Sulin''s mouth turned up slightly in a smile of greeting. Gently, Candy brought her hand to Sulin''s forehead and touched the white gauze bandage. "Does it still hurt?" she asked. \r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(128, 'The previous day, Sulin had risked her own safety for my sake. Unfortunately, her brave and noble act had landed her in the hospital with five stitches on her forehead. It had all started out innocently. Sulin''s pet dog had just delivered a litter of puppies and Sulin had invited Candy and I to her house to have a look at them. Under the mother dog''s watchful gaze, the puppies frolicked playfully. No one knows exactly what triggered the mother dog''s sudden, vicious attack. Maybe, as I lifted the puppies high in the air, it thought that I meant to harm them. Whatever the reason, the huge dog growled deeply and bared its fangs. Its unblinking eyes bore steadily into mine. I will never forget that hair-raising sound. Just as it reached out its massive paw towards me, Sulin pushed me out of harms way. The dog''s paw slashed through the air and landed heavily on Sulin''s forehead.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(129, 'Now, as I stood by Sulin''s bedside, I was oppressed b_y an unbearable feeling of guilt. I should have been the one in bed with the stitches. Yet, I knew that Sulin held me blameless. She had done what she had thought was necessary. Blushing, she dismissed all praise of bravery. I will never forget her act of courage. ', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(130, '"Please Mum, let us go and see what is happening over there!" Lionel and I pleaded with our mother. We were walking along Orchard Road when loud music accompanied by a round of applause caught our attention. It was the grand opening of Sun Plaza and celebrations were in full swing. "Okay," relented our mother, "but we cannot stay for long." \r', 'Introduction', 'Celebrations'),
(131, 'Excitedly, Lionel and I joined the large crowd gathered at the entrance of Sun Plaza. Dazzling lights flashed across the stage set up there. Dancers in sequined costumes moved energetically to the pulsating music. Balloons in a bright array of colours were released into the air. It was a feast for the eyes! After the dance, the master of ceremonies announced that the staff of Sun Plaza would be distributing free hot dogs to everyone present. The crowd cheered in exaltation. The delicious aroma of hotdogs filled the air and my stomach rumbled in anticipation. I sank my teeth into the piping hot treat and flashed a satisfied grin at Lionel.\n\nSoon, the master of ceremonies asked for a volunteer to assist him with a magic trick. I waved my hand enthusiastically. To my delight, I was picked! As I ran upstage, my heart pounded wildly. I was asked to clench a pebble in my hand. The master of ceremonies waved his magic wand over it. Presto! The pebble magically transformed into a white pigeon! What an illusion!\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Celebrations'),
(132, 'When I rejoined the crowd, Mother said that it was time to go. Reluctantly, Lionel and I obeyed. As we walked off, I knew that I would always remember the day with fond memories. ', 'Conclusion', 'Celebrations'),
(133, 'Camping outdoors is one of my favorite activities. Taking in the clean, fresh air and enjoying the gifts of Mother Nature makes me feel healthy and alive! Thus, I could barely contain my excitement when Mr Loh, our teacher, announced that our boy scouts group was going camping at a remote farm area in Singapore.\r', 'Introduction', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(134, 'On the first day of our camp, my friend, Jim, pulled me aside. Jim is the most mischievous member of the boy scouts. I wondered what he had up his sleeve this time. "Psst! Raj! Let''s go and explore the farm next door!" he urged. It was an enticing idea. I glanced around furtively. When Mr Loh turned his back on us, Jim and I crept away.\n\nAt the entrance of the farm, a goat stood blocking our path. To distract the goat, Jim bent down, picked up a stone and threw it. He aimed for a spot behind the goat but the stone hit the goat squarely on its head! Without warning, the goat charged angrily towards us! Screaming and yelling at the top of our voices, Jim and I turned and fled back to the camp. I quickly scrambled up a tree and clung on for dear life. I was safe from the goat''s attack! Jim, on the other hand, was not so fortunate. He ran round the tree in circles with the enraged goat hot on his heels! Mr Loh and the other scouts laughed hilariously at us. Finally, Mr Loh managed to coax the goat to leave us alone.\n', 'Main Body', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(135, 'Jim and I became laughing stocks. Everyone teased us mercilessly. The next time Jim tries to involve me in one of his adventures, I think I will decline. ', 'Conclusion', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(136, '"If you want to be in my gang, you will have to perform an initiation rite," Kim Seng snarled. I listened eagerly. I desperately wanted to be a member of his "cool" and notorious gang. "What must I do?" I asked earnestly. With a twinkle in his eye Kim Seng said, "If you can steal a bottle of fruit soda from the minimart over there, you''re in." I gulped hard. "I''ll do it," I said, trying to sound brave. In fact, inside I was shaking like a leaf.\r', 'Introduction', 'Action'),
(137, 'Plucking up my courage, I made a mad dash towards the minimart and seized a bottle of fruit soda. As I ran off, my heart thumped wildly. Suddenly, a loud, shrill voice screamed, "Come back here you little thief!" It was the minimart owner. I ignored his command and ran like the wind. The passerbys cast me questioning looks and pointed fingers in my direction. Then, to my horror, I spotted a policeman coming round the corner. As soon as he saw me, he gave chase. In no time, he closed  the gap between us. I knew that it was all over for me. In the distance, I saw Kim Seng run away. He had deserted me! The policeman grabbed me and pinned my arms behind my back. I hung my head in shame.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Action'),
(138, 'I was brought to the neighborhood police post. I felt deeply ashamed that had succumbed to peer pressure. The policeman advised me to stay away from bad company. He let me off with a stern warning never to steal again. From then on I chose my friends carefully. That is, based on how much I could trust and depend on them rather than how "cool" they appeared to be. ', 'Conclusion', 'Action'),
(139, '"Look at all these presents Father brought back from Europe!" my little brother squealed with delight. Our father had just returned from a business trip and our whole family had gone to the airport to welcome him home. As I peered curiously into the brightly coloured shopping bags, I suddenly heard a voice shouting, "Help! Stop thief!"\r', 'Introduction', 'Humour'),
(140, 'A man in a striped shirt and sunglasses was running towards us. In his hand he clutched a red handbag. Behind him, a frail and elderly lady cried for help. The man had snatched the old lady''s handbag and was trying to make a quick get away! I knew exactly what I had to do. As he ran past me, I stuck my foot in his path. The man tripped and fell flat on his face. Without hesitation, I jumped on top of him, pulled his hair and pounded on his back with my fists. "How dare you prey on an old lady!" I hollered. The man begged for mercy, "Let go of me! It''s not what you think!" he pleaded. Suddenly, a voice from a distance yelled, "Cut!" Only then did I notice a film crew stationed in the background. I had disrupted a scene that was being filmed! I flushed with  embarrassment. My face felt as if it was on fire!\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Humour'),
(141, 'Fortunately, the director was a kind and understanding man with a sense of humour. He even praised me for my bravery and said that my actions were commendable. Father gave me a loving pat on the head. He said that even though it was only a staged robbery, I was still a hero in his eyes. When I heard this, m_y spirit soared and my heart surged with  pride. I might have made a fool of myself but I knew that I had done the right thing! ', 'Conclusion', 'Humour'),
(142, 'My grandfather is a gentleman whom I look up to with awe and respect. He always handles awkward and dangerous situations with an air of regality. He never allows panic to take control of him. Just last week, he won the admiration of our neighbours when he rescued a young boy from danger.\r', 'Introduction', 'Outdoor Activities');
INSERT INTO `astarcompo_templates` (`templateID`, `template`, `templateType`, `theme`) VALUES
(143, 'Every evening, Grandpa takes a leisurely stroll in the park. It makes him happy to hear the cheerful sounds of children at play and to watch the ducks swim tranquilly on the lake. Last week, as he was sitting serenely on one of the benches, he heard a voice shout, "Don''t!" The explosive shout was followed by a loud ''whack'' and all of a sudden, a wild buzzing noise broke out. A playful boy had hit a bees'' hive with his slingshot. The angered bees swarmed out in full force to launch a counter-attack. When Grandpa saw that the boy was in danger, he swiftly sprung into action. He grabbed the boy in his arms, jumped into the lake and used his newspapers to shield their heads and faces from the angry bees.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(144, 'Grandpa remained in this position and reassured the boy with soothing words until help arrived. The people who had witnessed the scene spread the news of Grandpa''s heroic act. Now the whole neighbourhood always greets Grandpa with wide-eyed admiration and respect. The little children feel safe in his presence and the adults trust him. As for me, I feel proud and honored to be his grandchild. ', 'Conclusion', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(145, 'It was a particularly bright and sunny morning. My classmates and I were busy doing our warm-up exercises with our Physical education teacher, Mrs Poon, when the unmistakable wail of a police siren caught our attention.\r', 'Introduction', 'Criminal Acts'),
(146, 'We scrambled towards the school fence just in time to witness two men atop a midnight black motorcycle travelling towards us at a reckless speed. The men wore identical helmets with shaded visors. Despite the clamour and uproar that their presence had excited, I could not help noticing that one of the men was clutching a black briefcase in his free hand ''as if his life depended on it. My heart pounded wildly as I watched a police car race after the motorcycle in hot pursuit. "Obviously," I thought to myself, "this is a get away chase!"\n\nIn the course of the chase, the motorcycle tilted dangerously towards its side. I looked on at the amazing spectacle with breathless anticipation. "Surely it would topple over!" I exclaimed. True enough, the motorcycle fell on its side with an unceremonious crash. The two men were trapped beneath its considerable weight.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Criminal Acts'),
(147, 'By this time, the surrounding environment was abuzz with horror and excitement. As it turned out, the motorcyclists were snatch-thiefs who had just robbed an unsuspecting citizen of his briefcase. In the end, the criminals were handcuffed and law and order was restored. As my friends and I filed back towards the field, we realized the undeniable fact that crime never pays! ', 'Conclusion', 'Criminal Acts'),
(148, '"Don''t run!" cried our elder sister. Her words fell on deaf ears as m) brother and I continued to chase after each other in the jam-packec MRT station. Engaged in our game of catch, we did not notice a mar rushing towards the train. "Make way! I''m late for a meeting!" he warned, but it was too late. \r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(149, 'I collided head-on with him. His files tumbled to the floor and loose sheets fluttered all over. To make matters worse, he then tripped ovet one of the many shopping bags lying carelessly about. He went crashing to the floor with a loud thud! I stared in mute horror as he lay there groaning in pain. "I am so sorry Sir!" exclaimed my sister who had rushed to my side. She continued to apologize profusely as she bent over to help the man up. The man said that his arm was in pain and that he could not move it. We insisted on taking him to the hospital to express our heartfelt apology.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(150, 'The man, Andy, sustained a fractured arm from the fall. He was sent home with strict orders to rest. We exchanged telephone numbers and addresses. Over the next few weeks, my sister and I visited him frequently. As time went by, romance blossomed between my blushing sister and Andy. My brother and I promised that we would never run around in crowded places again. However, in this case, perhaps our untimely playfulness was a blessing in disguise!', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(151, 'Grr! My stomach growled hungrily as I made my way towards school. I licked my lips as I thought of the eggs that I would buy during recess. However, a quick peek into my wallet confirmed that the fifty-cent treat was beyond my budget. I sighed. I was so disappointed that I scarcely noticed the bright red lorry driving past. It was loaded with numerous wire cages and packed with squawking chickens. As the lorry made its way past our school, the door to one of the cages at the rear end of the lorry flew open. Pandemonium broke out as half a dozen noisy chickens spilled out on to the street!\r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(152, 'I stood rooted to the spot as I watched the chickens flap about excitedly. Other students who had witnessed the accident broke out in laughter. In the hubbub that ensued, I knew that something had to be done. I quickly mobilized my friends to form a circle in which to trap the chickens. With a harried look on his face, the lorry driver kept an eye on our progress. He too had his hands full as he chased after a lone escapee. Within no time, my friends and I had the six chickens surrounded. One of the more excited hens flapped its wings and lifted itself in mid air. Quickly, I reached out and grabbed hold of it. The driver thanked us as he replaced his chickens in their cage. He was drenched in perspiration. "I could never have done it without your help!" he exclaimed. When he noticed the hen cradled in my arms, he grinned and said, "Keep that one as a token of appreciation!- I could not believe my ears!\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(153, 'As he drove off, I stroked the speckled feathers of my new pet. I smiled happily and said, "How wonderful! Now I will never have to long for eggs that I cannot afford!"', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(154, 'I hurried down the school corridor towards the staff room. There was so much to do! As I hastened along, pupils greeted me dutifully. "Good afternoon, Mr Tan," they chorused as they strolled by in groups. I smiled at them. As I rounded the corner, I came to a sudden halt. Lloyd and his friends were at it again! \r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(155, '"Cards!" I muttered under my breath as I struggled to control my temper. My hands flew to my hips and my foot started its habitual tapping. I felt myself grow hot under my collar as I waited for the boys to notice my presence. Mentally, I counted the number of times I had warned them about gambling. "Countless!" I fumed. Yet here they were again, flagrantly breaking the school rule in plain view of everyone! "This time they even have a pile of money in front of them!" I thought angrily. Just as my temper reached boiling point, Lloyd glanced up. "Oh! Hi Mr Tan," he said sheepishly as his friends scrambled to remove all incriminating evidence. With cards and dollar notes slipping out of their pockets, Lloyd and his friends slowly started backing away. Suddenly, I remembered what it was like to be in their shoes. "Boys," I said resignedly, somewhat calmed by the recollection. "We know Mr Tan. We are truly sorry!" they said. I shook my head and held out my hand. Lloyd gave me the deck of cards. As I pocketed the cards, they solemnly promised never to gamble again.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(156, 'Back in the staff room, I stretched backwards in my chair. I took out the deck of cards and shuffled them. Reminiscing, I laid them out and settled down to a quiet game of solitaire. ', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(157, '"Raise your weapon brave soldier!" I said in a tone of mock bravado, brandishing my broom in Yusofs face. He chuckled and pointed the mop handle he was holding at me. It was our school''s annual spring cleaning day and everyone was busy with their tasks. Yusof and I had been appointed to sweep and mop the floor. It was a rather dull and monotonous job, so I decided to inject some fun into it by pretending that we were medieval warriors. \r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(158, '"Cling! Clang!" I said, imitating the sound of metal weapons as my broom clashed with Yusofs mop. "Surrender yourself, Sir Matthew," Yusof challenged, "you are no match for my sword fighting skills!" I chortled and refuted his challenge, "In your dreams, Sir Y use Twill  triumph in this battle!" I thrust my broom in Yusof s direction, causing him to stumble backwards. As he did so, he knocked over a pail of soapy water and created a huge puddle on the floor. In the heat of our duel, we did not notice this. At least, not until we heard the shrill shriek of Miss Lim, our form teacher, as she slipped and fell. The stack of worksheets she was carrying flew into the air. Yusof and I looked on in horror as Miss Lim slowly stood up. She stared daggers at us. I could practically see smoke coming out of her ears! "As your punishment," she said in a deadly whisper, "the both of you will sweep and mop all the classrooms on the second level." Yusof and I rushed to obey her. Deftly, we made our way up the staircase with our broom, mop and pail.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(159, 'Once upstairs, we quickly set to work. It was an incredibly dull activity! glanced about furtively. There was not a soul in sight. I felt my face break into an impish grin. "Psst! Sir Yusof!" I whispered mischievously, "Raise your weapon!" ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(160, 'The sound of lapping waves and merry laughter greeted my ears. The sun-spangled sea glistened like a huge sapphire. It was a picture-perfect day at the beach. My family and I picked out a shady spot under a tall coconut tree and lay down our mat and picnic basket. \r', 'Introduction', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(161, 'Just as Mother was preparing to hand out ham and cheese sandwiches, my brother, Samuel, let out a cry of shock. "Look out!" he exclaimed, pointing up the coconut tree. There, a cheeky brown monkey sat with a ripe coconut in its hands. It suddenly let go of the coconut, sending it plummeting down towards us like a cannon ball! Before we could respond, it grabbed another coconut and hurled it down at us. Two more coconuts followed in quick succession! Dad, Mum, Samuel and I quickly bounded out of the way. We watched in amazement as the monkey chattered gleefully to itself It looked proud of its work. Then, in the blink of an eye, the monkey scrambled down the tree and disappeared into the forested area nearby.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(162, '"What a rare occurrence! Do you think the monkey meant to hurt us?" asked my mother. "I don''t think so, I think it meant to give us a treat!" said my father with a wide smile on his face. I looked down at the pile of coconuts. There were exactly four golden-green coconuts. One for each of us! I laughed in delight as my father cut open the coconut husks. We sat down and indulged in the sweet and refreshing drink. I decided there and then that monkeys were my favourite animals! ', 'Conclusion', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(163, 'We wish you a Merry Christmas!" The popular Christmas carol blared from the loudspeakers. The shopping centre was particularly crowded. It was Christmas Eve. All around me, people were laughing gaily. The beautifully decorated Christmas trees, laden with exquisite baubles and twinkling lights, added to the atmosphere of festivity. As I shopped, I scanned the crowd and smiled warmly at all who walked past. To my great delight, I spotted my classmate, Mei Mei, up ahead. As I made my way towards her, she reached out and took something from the counter in front of her. Unaware that I had witnessed her actions, she slipped the item into her bag.\r', 'Introduction', 'Celebrations'),
(164, 'I stopped in my tracks. The spirit of joy I felt quickly evaporated. I did not know what to do. Then, Mei Mei turned around. Our eyes met. Her face was flushed and her hands were trembling. "Why did you do that?" I asked. At first, Mei Mei feigned ignorance. However, as I continued to look at her, she finally broke down and confessed. In a voice tinged with shame, she told me that she had taken the watch for her grandfather. "I only wanted to make him happy!" she sobbed. Not wanting to draw attention to ourselves, I guided her to a quiet corner. There, she removed a handsome, leather-strapped watch from her bag. Together, we went back to the counter and replaced it on its velvet bed.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Celebrations'),
(165, 'Mei Mei had realized her mistake. She turned towards me with an abashed expression and said, "I don''t know what overcame me." I gave her a reassuring smile. She decided to make a homemade card for her grandfather. That day, we learnt that the true spirit of Christmas lies in the well wishes we have, rather than in the exchange of expensive gifts. ', 'Conclusion', 'Celebrations'),
(166, 'As I made my way home from school one particularly humid afternoon, I was bursting with anticipation. Mother had promised to add a specially grafted orchid to my collection of potted plants. We lived at block 315, which was just a stone''s throw away from school. Eager to tend to my new plant, I quickened my pace.\r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(167, 'As I rounded the corner, my gaze automatically flew to the second storey. There on the corridor stood my mother. She was busy watering my plants. In a voice filled with exuberance, I called out to her. She waved back and gestured to her left where the new plant stood in prime position. In her excitement, she failed to notice that one of the other pots was perched precariously close to the edge. Everything happened all at once. The plant, an exceptionally beautiful rose cutting in full bloom, toppled over the edge just as an unsuspecting pedestrian strolled past. CRASH! The pot shattered just inches away from the startled man. "Are you okay?" Mother and I exclaimed simultaneously. Fortunately, apart from the shock he had received, the young man was unscathed. From her position on the corridor, mother apologized profusely. The young man dusted his hands on his trousers and assured us that he was fine. He then turned to the task of picking up the broken pieces. I rushed to assist him and insisted that he leave the job to me.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(168, 'It turned out that the young man, Kenny, was a botanist. He gave me some invaluable advice on how to t end to my plants, not to mention, where to place them in future! ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(169, 'I stretched lazily and lifted my trunk in the air as the sun rose. It was another glorious morning at the Zoo. Soon, the visitors would arrive. I snuggled up to my newborn baby, Popi the elephant, and raised my head to see what was for breakfast. It was bananas again! I stifled a groan.\r', 'Introduction', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(170, 'In a few minutes, the visitors arrived. They were armed with cameras  and wasted no time in snapping pictures of Popi and I. I did not mind visitors, as long as they kept their distance and behaved well. However, those who threw things at us and made a lot of jarring noise got on my nerves. As I posed for the pictures, I spied two boys inching their way towards my enclosure. "Ooh! Look at the baby elephant!" one of the boys gushed. "He''s cute!" his friend added. Then they did something unpardonable. They reached out and pulled Popi''s tail! I was extremely vexed. They had no regard for privacy! I let out a mighty bellow and glared at them with displeasure. I expected them to back away. Instead, they threw a banana at me! That was the last straw! I chased after the boys, flapping my ears in anger. Their cheeky expressions gave way to fear and they ran away as fast as they could. I chased them past the giraffe enclosure, past the parading peacocks and past the monkeys that jumped in excitement. The other visitors at the Zoo looked on with shocked faces. As a last resort, the two boys jumped into the duck pond. I dipped my trunk into the pond and sprayed the boys with a fountain of water. They looked utterly miserable.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(171, 'I was pleased with myself for having taught those boys a lesson. Let us see if they will disturb animals again! ', 'Conclusion', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(172, 'KABOOM! The deafening clap of thunder signaled the approach of rain. Just as I took shelter under my umbrella, it started to pour. I moved closer to my older sister, Lana. She took hold of my hand and together we made our way home. \r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(173, 'As we approached the pedestrian crossing, I saw a big tree lying on its side by the road. It had been uprooted during the storm and its branches and leaves waved about as if it was calling for help. I could not help but feel sorry for it. Suddenly, I thought I heard the excited chirping of birds. I strained my ears to listen for it. The heavy rain drowned out all  other sounds. I tugged at Lana''s hand. "Can you hear that?" I questioned excitedly. Lana shook her head and tried to get me to move on. I stubbornly resisted as my eyes scanned the area. Then, I saw a sparrow. It was chirping desperately! Its nest of eggs was trapped in the branches of the fallen tree and was in danger of toppling over! "There!" I exclaimed, pointing it out to Lana. In my eagerness, I dropped my umbrella. Sensing that I would not budge until an attempt was made to help the bird, Lana made her way towards the nest. I followed closely behind.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(174, 'Calmly and gently, Lana disentangled the nest from the gnarled branches. Inside the nest were six fragile sky-blue eggs. As the sparrow watched in silence, Lana transferred the nest to the lower branches of a neighbouring tree. She then looked towards the sparrow and beckoned gracefully. The sparrow needed no further invitation. It flew over and spread its beautiful wings protectively over its nest. This time, as we walked away, it was to the sound of happy, melodious chirping. ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(175, 'By the time I arrived, the party was in full swing. Balloons filled with helium bobbed about gaily and coloured streamers added the perfect finishing touch. The place was packed with the familiar faces of my classmates. The farewell party was held in honor of Shala, the boy who had come from Cambodia on a student exchange programme. He was a joy to have around and we were all sorry that he was returning home soon.\r', 'Introduction', 'Celebrations'),
(176, 'As I mingled with my friends, I saw Shala standing by the buffet table. I made my way over to him. The buffet table was laden with a feast fit for a King! There was an assortment of goodies and sparkling fizzy drinks. "A perfect feast for a perfect party!" I thought to myself. However, just as I was about to say hello to Shala, a terrible thing happened. A giant pizza whizzed past my ear and landed on the floor with an  unceremonious splat! I looked on in disbelief as a beautiful cream cake, topped with fresh strawberries, catapulted in the opposite direction. A food fight had broken out! I glanced over at Shala to gauge his reaction  but he was no longer standing by my side. He had rushed over to the scene of action. He was on the verge of tears as he lay a restraining hand on Abel, who was about to launch another pizza in the air. A silence descended on the room as Shala said, "Please, I beg of you, let''s not continue with this. In my country, many people don''t have enough to eat!" All of reddened with embarrassment.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Celebrations'),
(177, 'Soon, the party drew to a close. Shala had imparted a lesson we would never forget. As he waved goodbye, we promised him that we would always appreciate our blessings. With a grin, Shala quipped, "Waste not! Want not!" ', 'Conclusion', 'Celebrations'),
(178, 'It was a Sunday morning. As the weather was fine, my parents decided to have a picnic at West Coast Park. When I heard that, I was so excited that I jumped for joy. Mother quickly packed a picnic basket while Father packed a mat and some other items for the picnic.\r', 'Introduction', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(179, 'When we reached the park, we quickly placed our mat on the grass and unpacked our food and drinks. Being a lovely day, many people were at the park and everyone seemed to be enjoying themselves. There was a boy playing with a football and two other boys flying their kites. The kites looked lovely against the clear blue sky.\n\nWhile enjoying our food, we watched one of the boys, in particular the one in a striped shirt, flying his kite. He was running excitedly, pulling the kite along,  which flew higher and higher in the sky. In his excitement, he did not notice a large hole in front of him, and he fell into it.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(180, 'Everyone was shocked, and my father quickly ran over to see if he could help. Some other people in the park also ran over to provide assistance. Luckily, the boy had landed on his feet and was unhurt. The hole was quite a shallow one and so my father was able to pull him out easily and quicky. My mother made sure he was not hurt. The boy thanked us and ran off. We then went back to enjoying our interrupted picnic.', 'Conclusion', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(181, 'It was a bright and sunny day, the perfect weather to be at the beach. However, I was at the beach neither for a picnic nor for o swim in the sea. Rather, my class was at the beach to pick litter as part of the school''s efforts to clean up the environment. \r', 'Introduction', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(182, 'We were decked out in our RE. attire and equipped with metal tongs and garbage bags to accomplish our task. Led by Mr Kang, our form teacher, the whole class started the task with enthusiasm. I was startled at how much litter there was on the beach and wondered why people could not be more considerate and clear up after themselves, instead of littering the beach, which could have profound consequences on the ecosystem.\n\nAs I started filling my second garbage bag, I heard someone laughing. Looking up. I realised that it was Derek. He had grown tired of the task and had diverted his attention to some crabs that were crawling on the sand. I ignored him and carried on picking up litter. Within minutes, a piercing yell rang out. I looked up, wondering what had happened. Did someone injure himself? The source of the cry was from Derek again. Apparently, one of the crabs had pinched his finger with its pincer, causing him to yell in agonising pain. Some of my classmates laughed at him for being so foolish as to disturb the crabs while others looked on in concern, not knowing how to help him.\n\nJust then, Mr Kang came running up for he, too, had heard Derek yelling. When he realised what had happened, he grabbed a twig from the sond and jabbed the eyes of the crab. The crab, to our astonishment, released its grip and dropped to the ground. Derek, whose tears could not stop flowing and whose face had become white from the shock and pain, choked back his tears and heaved a sigh of relief. Mr Kong then\ntook some antiseptic cream from the first aid box that he had brought along and\napplied it on Derek''s finger, gently chiding him for his behaviour at the same time.\nThe class was relieved that Derek was all right, for we knew that there was no\nmalice behind his actions.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(183, 'We continued to clean up the beach and were proud of how beoutiful it looked after our hard work. To reward ourselves, we proceeded to fill our stomachs with a satisfying lunch, which was ironically fried rice with morsels of crab meat.', 'Conclusion', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(184, 'It was a Friday evening and Hillside all was packed with shoppers. Holly and Grace had stayed back in school for choir practice. After that, they decided to get some ice cream from their favourite ice cream shop at the mall. After purchasing an ice cream cone each, they stopped by the railing to chat while enjoying their delicious ice cream tones. Engrossed in the conversation, Grace loosened her grip on the cone she was holding and it slipped from her hands.\r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(185, 'A smartly-dressed man in a shirt and tie had stopped directly under the railing where Grace was. He seemed preoccupied with locating some documents from his bag. Grace gasped loudly knowing that the ice cream cone would land on him. An Indian lady who was walking down the staircase gave a shout to warn him but it was too late. Splat! The man''s face was a mask of anger and disgust as chocolate ice cream streamed down his face and stained his shirt.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(186, 'Grace and Holly ran down the stairs, apologising profusely to the man who was rooted to the spot. A quick-thinking lady who had witnessed the scene from a bench nearby came forward and offered him tissue paper to clean himself. Grace was red with embarrassment. Fortunately the man had just got off from work and was on his way home. Although irritated at his misfortune, he accepted the girls'' apologies and told Grace to be careful in future. It would be a longtime before she went around eating ice cream cones in the mall again! ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(187, 'As I walked into the barber''s with my Mom to wait for my turn for a haircut, I had a flashback to the day where I showed my mischevious side. No doubt I was given a tongue-lashing and was punished after that incident. No, it is not an elaborate prank I performed. It only involved a pair of scissors.\r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(188, 'That school day, I walked into class to find a huge crowd gathering around Jane, my classmate. I guessed that she was probably showing off again as usual. I was right. When it comes to this kind of things, I usually am.\n\nI really hated Jane. She is just a show-off. I recalled an incident where she was the only person in the class who got an ''A'' for her essay. During recess, she walked around the class showing every single person her essay. Not only that, she passed off comments like "See? I''m the only person to get an ''A''. Then again, it''s nothing out of the ordinary when I''m gifted with good looks and of course, smarts".\n\nMy thoughts were interuppted when the school bell rang, signalling the start of lessons. By the way, if you are wondering what Jane was bragging about this morning, it was her new haircut which supposedly was done by the best hairdresser in town.\n\nAt this moment, Mr Eng, our art teacher, came in. After the class settled down, he started teaching us how to make a collaige. Soon, everyone was lining up to get the art materials provided. As I was lining up, I saw Jane strutting down the classroom with her art materials, flicking her inky, black hair in the process. Seriously, why does she do that? She is just plain irritating.\n\nWhen we all had our art materials, we got down to work. Jane, who sat beside me, starting cutting her white paper neatly into strips. As I looked at my black piece of paper, I imagined Jane flicking her hair about but shook the image out of my mind. When I was about to cut it, a mischevious idea came into my mind.\n\nI picked up my scissors and... "Snip!" This was followed by an ear-piercing scream echoing throughout the class. As I looked at that thick lock of hair on the ground, I grinned.\n\nHowever, my grin was wiped of my face when the teacher approached. "What happened?" he asked in a worried tone. Before I could even open my mouth to explain, Jane shouted "This horrid boy cut off my hair! Oh my beautiful hair! It had to be cut off by this monster!"\n\nThe expression on the teacher''s face changed. My, my! If looks could kill, I would be lying in a coffin right now.\n\nNow, it is obvious what happened next. I was given a harsh scolding and a long lecture. Even when I mentioned how vain Jane was, the teacher gave me another long lecture about tolerance. However, I gave in and even felt repentent. I apologised to Jane, not expecting her to forgive me. This was one rare occasion when I was wrong.\n\nI was shocked. "Why?" I said, my eyes growing as big as dinner plates. She replied "You can''t expect a good girl like me who has all the character traits in the world to not be forgiving? Won''t Mom be proud! I even forgave a monster like you!"\n\nSigh. Looks like she was still vain Jane. I wanted to shout that she lacked the character trait called humility, but I controlled myself. However, being an optimist, I found a silver lining in this cloud. I had learnt about thinking before I act and I learnt to be tolerant.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(189, 'I came back to earth as I heard the barber call my name. I walked over to him, the events of that day still lingering in my mind.', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(190, '"Mom, please let us go to the park. I want to fly my new kite!" said Tim. "Okay. But you have to let Henry have a go too." Mom replied. "I will", said Tim.\r', 'Introduction', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(191, 'It was the perfect day to go to the park. The sun was shining and there was an occasional breeze. They even saw a duck or two in the pond. When there was a breeze, Tim started flying his kite. It was a lovely sight. The kite''s yellow and orange tail stood out against the pale blue sky and the picture of an eagle on the kite looked as if it was really flying.\n\n"My turn, my turn!" shouted Henry. "Not yet, not yet!" said Tim. "But you have flown it for five minutes already!"\n\n"Well, it is my kite."\n\n"Mom said to share!"\n\nSo, an argument began. Both of them were arguing so badly that they did not notice the kite being blown towards a tree. When they finally noticed, the kite was already tangled up in the tree''s branches.\n\n"Here is the deal", said Tim, "the first person to untangle the kite gets to fly it for the whole day." "Okay!" said Henry as he climbed up the tree while Tim tried to pull the kite down from below. Then, as Henry was about to reach for the kite, he lost his balance.\n\nHe fell of the tree, landed on the grass and rolled into the lake. "Help!" said Henry, "I''m going to drown!" "But you can swim. I can''t help you because I cannot swim", said Tim. "I can''t swim either with a sprained ankle. It hurts," said Henry.\n\nLuckily, a passer-by stopped by to help. He jumped into the lake and saved Henry. "T.. Thank you", said Henry. "My pleasure!" said the kind passer-by. He even dropped the boys home. Henry''s mother was told about the situation. She was too relieved to be angry and thanked the passer-by.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(192, 'From that day on, Tim took up swimming lessons and Henry never climbed another tree. As for the kite, it is still stuck up there to this day!', 'Conclusion', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(193, 'The sky was dark and raindrops made a "pitter patter" sound on my window. The wind was howling loudly outside my window. In the past, I hated storms. The flashes of lightning and claps of thunder made me feel uneasy, sometimes scared. Now, with my furry companion asleep in my lap, nothing about the storm scares me. He probably has a calming influence on me. I marvel at the fact that he is not afraid of storms himself, after all, he had been through a terrible ordeal involving a storm...\r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(194, 'Alicia and I were walking at the park. We both loved that park. The occasional breeze would seem to whisper to me, as if it is saying: "Do you remember all the fun you and Alicia had in this park?" and I would recall how we used to sit on the swings while waiting for the ice-cream man to come and how we rode on the round-a-bout until we got dizzy. That day, the park was cooler than usual because of the rain the previous night and the playground was wet. Because of this, we had almost the whole place to ourselves and the park was peaceful without the laughter of young children sliding down the slide.\n\n"Sis! Did you hear that?" Alicia asked, her voice trembling.\n\n"Hear what?"\n\n"The bush, it rustled!"\n\n"The bushes always rustle when there''s a breeze."\n\n"I know that, but this one it... whined."\n\n"Bushes don''t whine!"\n\n"But this..."\n\nHer sentence was cut short. Then, I heard it too. A faint whine coming from a bush. I peeked in but I saw nothing. I felt something brush against my calf. It was hairy and a little wet. What was it? An image of a big, slimy hairy caterpillar crossed my mind.\n\n"Yuck! Get it of my leg!" I shrieked.\n\n"Get what of, Sis?"\n\n"The caterpillar!"\n\nShe started giggling.\n\n"Some fine sister you are! Help me now!"\n\n"Does that look like a caterpillar?"\n\nI turned around and saw the muddiest dog in the world. Its fur was matted and drenched and it looked as if it had rolled around in a pigsty.\n\n"Aww... Sis! Let''s take him home!"\n\nWas she nuts?\n\n"Why?"\n\n"He is in the most sorry state ever!"\n\n"Why would you want him? Just because Mom and Dad are vets and would take in an animal anytime doesn''t mean that you should take him home! You don''t even know what breed he is, let alone know how to take care of him!"\n\n"He is a Jack Russell."\n\nHow did she know that? Anyway, I stood my ground. I wasn''t going to let Alicia take that dog home. Being a Jack Russell, he would probably be hyperactive and would be able to tear the house down.\n\nI dragged a protesting Alicia home leaving the dog behind. Our parents were not home yet. Thank God they work on weekends and come home late at night after bedtime or they would instruct me to bring the Jack Russell home after hearing Alicia''s story. By the time Alicia wakes up tommorow, she would have forgotten about the dog. She is very forgetful.\n\nKnowing Alicia, she would probably sneak out to go and get the dog. However, she could not as a storm was brewing.\n\nAs I looked out the window at those dark clouds, I could not help thinking about the dog who was going to get soaked again in the rain. On imagining how sickly it would look if it caught a chill, my heart softened. I wanted to go back to the park to bring it home. But one thing stood in my way. My fear of storms. The lightning and thunder always scared me, since young. However, I pluckered up my courage and went out the door.\n\nI returned home with a shivering dog in my arms. Both of us were drenched, but it was worth it.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(195, 'A clap of thunder brought me back to earth. The wind was still howling outside, reminding me of the day Fluffy came into my life.', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(196, 'It was recess. I was walking towards the staffroom to see my teacher. Before I reached the staffroom, I heard someone sobbing. It seemed to come from the flight of stairs ahead.\r', 'Introduction', 'Family'),
(197, 'Surprised, I went to investigate the source of the sound. The sobbing gradually got louder as  I approached the stairs. Sitting on the top of the stairs was my classmate,  Cheryl. My sudden presence startled her and she promptly wiped away the tears on her face. Cheryl stumbled to get up and was about to walk away when I called out, “Cheryl! Wait!”\n\n“I’m going to the canteen,” Cheryl muttered quietly with her head down. “What’s wrong?” I asked. Suddenly, Cheryl broke down in fresh tears, her shoulders heaving. She began to explain her problem to me, pausing between fits of uncontrollable crying. Cheryl’s mother was  a very strict parent and she would constantly punish her with a cane. Bad results, inappropriate behavior, and even the smallest of things would trigger off her mothers’s temper. I finally comprehended the cause of all the streak  marks  on  Cheryl’s arms that were often present. Cheryl was deeply afraid  of her mother and her dismal mid-year results would be the reason for another violent course of breating.\n\nCheryl’s predicament reflected her helplessness and I vowed to help her. Her mother’s tendency to overreact was taking a toll on Cheryl’s emotional and mental state. Together, Cheryl and I approached Miss Tan in the staffroom to inform her of Cheryl’s situation. Miss Tan listened to Cheryl attentively and assured her that she would speak to Cheryl’s mother.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Family'),
(198, 'After meeting Cheryl’s mother, Miss Tan could sense the intense protectiveness of Cheryl’s mother towards Cheryl. When Cheryl’s mother knew that Cheryl had been crying, she was also upset and promised not to hit her again. She gave Cheryl a hug and apologized. For the first time in many months, a smile broke out on Cheryl’s face.', 'Conclusion', 'Family'),
(199, 'The cool water of the condominium swimming pool surrounded me as I floated on my back. Turning to my side to look out for my buddies, I saw them — they had just arrived. I stopped floating immediately and began to tread water at the same spot, making slot of splashes as I hollered and waved frantically, trying to attract their attention.\r', 'Introduction', 'Humour'),
(200, 'Suddenly, a large orange and white ''doughnut'' landed in front of me. Passers-by looked at me. Some giggled while others whispered to one another. Then came another splash. Turning towards the direction of the second splash, I spotted the lifeguard swimming towards me. He then looped his arm around my neck and swam towards the edge of the pool, pulling me along.\n\n''Hey! Let go of me!'' I shouted, flailing my arm in the air, struggling to break free from the burly lifeguard''s grasp. Upon reaching the edge, the guard asked me if I was okay and if I needed the hospital. Puzzled, I asked ''Why would I need to be hospitalised?'' Annoyed, the guard answered, ''You were drowning!'' Rolling my eyes, I sighed and mumbled, was only waving to my friends.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Humour'),
(201, 'The guard''s face turn pink as a wave of embarrassment washed over him. After apologising profusely, he rushed back to his duties. When my friends heard about the incident, they burst out laughing. I then became the laughing stock amongst these ''buddies'', even now. ', 'Conclusion', 'Humour'),
(202, 'The sun was shining brightly in the azure sky. Fluffy white clouds that looked like white cotton candy drifted across the horizon lazily. It was a pleasant morning. Ella had brought her pet dog, Mocha Chip, out for a walk in the neighbourhood park. Mocha Chip was Ella’s miniature dachshund and was brown in colour. Ella had had it since she was five. It was Ella’s most beloved pet. Ella looked forward to enjoying her stroll in the park. She took a deep breath of the cool, fresh air. Mocha Chip was sniffing left and right. There were many interesting things on the path.\r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(203, 'All of a sudden, Mocha Chip yelped loudly. This was very unusual behaviour for the dog. Then, it broke free and dashed off. Ella was taken aback. Mocha Chip had never behaved that way before. She shouted at Mocha Chip to stop but to no avail.\nAnxiously, Ella followed Mocha Chip. When she caught up with her dog, she yanked its leash tightly. Then, Ella’s eyes widened in surprise. She had discovered a brown bag among the bushes. She looked around for someone to claim the bag but there was not a single soul in sight. Out of curiosity, Ella opened the bag.\n\nElla’s jaw dropped. There were wads of notes in the bag! Stunned, Ella rubbed her eyes. She wanted to keep the money as she recalled an incident that took place the previous week. She and her mother had seen a beautiful sapphire necklace in a jewellery shop while they were window shopping. She remembered how Mother had looked at the necklace yearningly. Ella was tempted to keep the money to buy the necklace as a present for her mother as it was her mother’s birthday next week. But in the end, she decided not to.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(204, 'After thinking for a while, Ella took the bag to the police station which was a street away. She related the whole incident to the police officers on duty. The police commended her for her honesty. Ella was glad that she had done the right thing because honesty is the best policy.', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(205, 'Christmas was in the air. As I strolled towards Ngee Ann City, I could not help noticing that Orchard Road was lined by creamy and carmine little trees. When I entered Ngee Ann City, I was immediately greeted by a huge emerald Christmas tree which towered over me. There were turquoise, lavender and crimson balls hanging from it, with ''snow'' that never melts scattered convincingly across the tree and right at the very tip of it was a huge lilac star. Hordes of shoppers thronged the mall and the excited buzz of the crowd drowned out the faint muzak playing in the background.\r', 'Introduction', 'Action'),
(206, '''She''s late again,'' I thought exasperatedly. I had arranged to go shopping with my friend, Jia Rong, to purchase some Christmas gifts for the upcoming festive season.\n\nJust as I was about to call Jia Rong, a loud explosion rocked the building. After that, the mall was filled with the screams of ladies, wails of children and shouts of grown men. For a moment, I froze, to stunned to react.\n\nThat moment of paralysis was shattered when some men wielding guns appeared and began shooting into the crowd randomly. The shoppers, so jolly some moments ago, descended into a madness of desperate chaos. Screaming and shouting in terror, they charged blindly towards the exits, pushing me along. In the pandemonium, I saw a man collapse. He had been hit by a bullet and crimson blood was gushing out from his chest. No one bothered to help him He twitched in agony for a while and then stopped moving. My heart turned icy with fear.\n\nSuddenly, I saw some masked men in uniforms. From their attire, I recognised our counter-terrorist forces! My hopes rose only to plummet seconds later. Gunshots rang out as the criminals and law enforcers exchanged shots. Caught in the crossfire, I crouched as low as possible, praying that I would not be hit by any stray bullet.\n\nAs the soldiers engaged the outlaws, I clumsily made my way towards the exit of the mall, trying to move forward while staying as low as I could. After some time, my muscles were straining and my lungs felt like they were burning. But I ignored the discomfort and focused on the exit. Just when I was a few feet away from the sliding glass, my left leg seized up in agony. Of all times to be struck by cramps!\n\nI could have wept in despair. Then unexpected, a figure darted in, grabbed me and hauled me out of the mall. It was Jia Rong! The moment I stepped out of the mall, I slumped against her and started crying uncontrollably. Relief, fear, sadness - they all coursed through me, separate at times and mingling at others.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Action'),
(207, 'Later, I would learn that it was a terrorist attack. I was of course immensely grateful to Jia Rong. If she had not risked her life to save me, I do not know what would have happened to me. The incident left such a scar on me that I refused to go out for weeks. I also did not care to read the newspaper reports about it. Maybe after a long, long time has passed, I may become curious and then I would want to find out the whys, the whats and the hows. But not yet. Currently, all I want to do is to forget the incident.', 'Conclusion', 'Action'),
(208, 'I sniffed. Fresh air at last! It was a relief to be away from the toxic fumes on the road. I barked happily and leapt all around my owner., who had taken me to the park near our home for my daily walk.\r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(209, 'After sniffing for a while, I smelled a cat. Scanning my surrounding, I spotted the feline some distance away. Growling with determination to catch the cat, I leapt away from my owner and ran as fast as my legs could carry me to the scrawny cat.\n\nHowever, after running for a distance, I saw two pairs of legs in front of me. Looking up, I saw two boys. One was holding a stick and snickering. Feeling threatened, I lunged forward and bit the stick held by the boy. Alarmed, the boy swung the stick with all his might and flung me onto the rough pavement.\n\nI yelped as I tried to stand up and flee. A sharp pain exploded in my front leg. The boy told his partner beside him, “Get it!” To my horror, the youngsters started attacking me. One was using a stick to hit me while the other pulled me by my tail to prevent me from escaping.\n\n“Hey! Put it down now!” Turning my head I saw my superhero – my master. The cowardly boys fled for their lives at once. My master came up to me, carried me and brought me to the animal hospital.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(210, 'There the nurses treated my wounds and placed my right front limb in a cast, as it was broken. From this experience, I earnt of my master’s true care and love for me. He visited the hospital daily to take care of me. From that day onwards, I would never dare to leave my master’s side whenever we were out for walks.', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(211, 'Lightning streaked across the darkened sky. For a few seconds, the sky brightened ominously, but the five boys dashing for the football in the field were oblivious to it. Each side was bent on scoring the next goal for the game hung in a balance.\r', 'Introduction', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(212, 'Fat raindrops were falling fast. “Crack!” The sound of thunder seemed to split the air. Kai Feng and Ravi jumped. Ravi glanced at the dark rolling clouds, worry furrowing his brows. The distant rumble of thunder was beginning to unnerve him. He opened his mouth to utter a warning to his friends to take shelter but the words were stuck in his throat. Wide-eyed, he stared at the lightning bolt which shot to the ground, forming a bridge that stretched from heaven to earth momentarily. It missed him by a few centimetres. Next to him, he saw James fall to the ground. Everything seemed to freeze, like a snapshot captured on camera.\n\n“James!” The name was torn from Ravi’s throat in a long drawn-out shriek.\n\nPanic propelled their feet. Ravi and Kai Feng sprinted towards James as Ahmad and\nKevin rushed towards the shelter. Ahmad took his mobile phone out and called for the ambulance, panting out the location to the operator. Ravi and Kai Feng half-squatted as they ran towards James, keeping as low as they could to the ground. James lay unmoving, faced down. Both boys did not dare ask the dreaded question. Ravi reached out a hand and pressed two fingers against James’ neck. He let out a sigh of relief. Beneath his fingers, he could feel a faint pulse. The rain was pelting down, stinging the boys’ bare skins mercilessly. Together, the two boys rolled James over. His lips were blue and his hair was singed. There was a black burn mark on his right arm. Should they try to carry him to the shelter? Staying out in the open seemed too dangerous.\n\nAnother bolt of lightning streaked across the sky, followed closely by the inevitable clap of thunder. That helped them make up their minds. As they began lugging James to the shelter, Ahmad and Kevin came over hesitantly, ashamed that they had thought of their own safety first instead of attending to their fallen friend. The water made it too slippery for them to get a firmer grip on James; however, bit by bit, they pulled him towards the shelter.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(213, 'Soon, the paramedics showed up. After a quick check, they lifted James into the ambulance and sped away. Once more, Ahmad’s fingers reached for his mobile phone. This time, it was to call James’ mother. His voice shook as he delivered the bad news. Solemnly, the boys waited till the storm abated before heading home, each one a sorry bedraggled figure. They met again at the hospital the following day, and were thankful to know that James would recover. The five friends had learnt their lesson painfully – it was definitely unwise to ignore a storm. They realised now that Mother Nature could sometimes be ruthless; she was not always benign.', 'Conclusion', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(214, 'I zoomed down the slope, turned round a bend and sailed towards the lift lobby in less than ten seconds. I glided back and forth in front of the lift, jumping up and making a complete turn at each end. Plugged into my MP4, I was in a world of my own. I was about to make another turn when I noticed a brown canvas haversack next to one of the lifts. I looked around and realised that the lift lobby was deserted. The bag looked like the one that I carried to school. There were two compartments—a main one and a smaller one in front. There were two netted pockets at the side of the haversack which were empty.  When I got nearer, I realised that there was something protruding from inside the bag.\r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events');
INSERT INTO `astarcompo_templates` (`templateID`, `template`, `templateType`, `theme`) VALUES
(215, 'Overcome with curiosity, I squatted in front of the bag and stared hard at it. I poked it, wondering if there was an abandoned pet inside, but nothing moved. I lifted it and realised that it was much heavier than my school bag, almost like the sack of rice that i helped my father to carry home from the supermarket. Remembering what I had watched on television recently about terrorist attacks, I decided to call the police immediately.\n\nAfter a brief conversation, I stood a short distance from  the bag to wait for the police to arrive. I stood there with my arms folded, feeling like a security guard keeping watch over a valuable item. Two police officers arrived before long and I pointed out the bag to them. In my excitement, I exaggerated what I had seen and how I felt. I told them that I thought I sae a figure running away in the distance. I even told them that I had seen several suspicious characters walking past the void deck in the past few weeks.\n\nI was told to step aside while the police officers examined the bag. Their faces turned grim when they heard my story. I watched as one of the officers picked up the gloved hands and opened the zip. He pulled the cover apart cautiously at first and peered in. Suddenly, he burst out laughing and opened the bag further. Suddenly small rocks tumbled out. "They are just rocks, boy," he called out. "The sharp object that you thought was a knife is actually a twig. See," held up the L-shaped twig and waved it in the air.\n\nMy face flushed. I was so embarrassed that I wished the ground would open up and shallow me. However, the police officers told me that I had done the right thing. It was better to have a false alarm than to ignore a real bomb. Feeling slightly better, I nodded and turn to return home.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(216, 'That night, I told my parents what had happened. They praised me for being alert and calling the police.  I did not tell them about the story that I had spun to make the situation more exciting. I regretted doing that. I realised how tensed the situation must have been and how my story had created unnecessary anxiety for the police officers. ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(217, '“Ding dong…” went the recess bell.  I got out of my seat and ran down to the canteen.  I went to the toilet near the field before buying my food as I needed to change my clothes for my physical education class.  Just when I was coming out, I heard a muffled sound of sobbing from behind me.  Curious, I spun around to see what had happened.  To my surprise, I saw my best friend, Laura, sobbing in distress.  I walked to her briskly and asked her what had happened.  She refused to answer and stood there sobbing.  She said that I would not understand.  Not discouraged by her words, I asked her again.  Finally, she answered.\r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(218, '“Jack and his gang…they slapped me…and they jeered at me…” she murmured even though she was sobbing.\n\nHearing that, I was so furious that my eyes turned red.  Jack and his gang were a group of boys that bullied all the students in school.  He was tall and big, nobody dared to go against him.  Asked laura if she wanted to confront him  she shook he head almost immediately and said that she had no confidence at all and did not want to see him again.\n\n“Laura, a friend is one who believes in you even when you have ceased to believe in yourself.  I know you can do it,” I said firmly.\n\nFinally, after a moment of silence, Laura gave me a teary smile and nodded her head.  Together, we went to find him.  Finally, we saw him.  Both of us gulped as our hearts beat fast.  As we looked at each other, we gave a nod of encouragement to give ourselves moral support.  However, before we could even speak, jack spoke first.\n\n“Look at these berries, I found them in our garden. Hah!  I get to eat them while you don’t, ha ha!” he laughed naughtily before popping them into his mouth.\n\nKnowing that he had eaten poisonous berries, I froze with my eyes popping out as my jaw opened wide.  I gulped, knowing that the thing I dreaded was going to happen.  There laid Jack, his face as pale as a piece of paper.  He had fainted.  Hurriedly, I told his gang to help Laura and me carry him to the sick bay.  Due to his heavily-built body, we had a hard time carrying him.  Fortunately, some strong students saw us in distress and kindly came to help us.  After sometime, Jack finally got his treatment and went home to rest.\n\n“We did not even had to confront him, he got his own punishment by himself,” I said to Laura with a smile plastered on my face.\n\nA week later, Jack finally recovered and was allowed to get back to school.  The most surprising thing was that he was a new person.  I was shocked when I saw him helping those in need and I simply could not help but to smile.\n\n“This incident might have caused him pain because of his terrible headache, but it also caused him to turn over a new leaf too!” I whispered to Laura.\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(219, 'Suddenly, he came towards us and apologised for being so awful.  We smiled and told him it was alright.  This incident would always be etched in my mind.  I would pass the message down to my children that no bullies could escape punishment.   ', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(220, 'It was last Saturday and I had arranged to meet my friend Jo in the park. We both wanted to practise some new moves on our roller blades. I had to go shopping with my mother first, so we agreed to meet at 4 o''clock. \r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(221, 'When we got home from the shops, I changed my clothes, grabbed my rollers, shouted goodbye to my parents and dashed off to the park. I arrived on the dot of four, but there was no sign of Jo. I sat down on a bench to wait for her. I knew she would not be very late because she was a very punctual person. While I was waiting, I watched the other children on their bikes, their roller blades and skateboards. Some of them were really good and some of them were not so good, like Jo and me. I glanced down at my watch. It was 4.20. I was puzzled as this really was not like Jo at all. If there was a problem, she could ring or message me. So, I tried ringing her but she did not reply.\n\nI was getting a bit impatient and time seemed to be passing slowly. I kept looking at my watch — 4.30... 4.40... 4.50. There was still no sign of Jo. I tried ringing several times, but still she did not answer. By now I was more worried than angry. I decided to wait 10 more minutes and then catch the bus to her house. On the dot of 5 o''clock, I left the park, walked to the bus stop and caught the bus. As I was getting off the bus, I saw Jo and her family near the entrance of their apartment block.\n\nI ran towards them. "Hello Mr Lee, hello Mrs Lee," I said breathlessly. "Jo, what happened? I''ve been waiting for you for ages!" It was only then that I noticed Jo''s brother, Phil, standing behind his father. He was walking on crutches and looked very pale. "Oh!" I exclaimed. "Are you all right, Phil?" Then, Jo explained. Phil had been playing with his friends, running around, when he had stumbled over a piece of wood and hurt his ankle badly. Jo had seen the accident and had quickly got her parents. They had rushed Phil to the hospital and then waited. Fortunately, it turned out that Phil had just sprained his ankle. Nothing was broken.\n\n"I couldn''t ring you to tell you what had happened," Jo explained. "Because the hospital asks you to turn off all mobile phones and I didn''t want to leave Phil and my parents to go outside to ring."\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(222, '"Of course," I said. "Making sure Phil was all right was much more important. We can always go to the park another time." ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(223, 'Friday morning I was a little late leaving the house for school. I could not find my Science homework and spent some time searching for it in my bedroom. Then I realised that in fact I had already put it in my bag! This made me late and instead of getting the usual bus, I caught the next one. This still meant I would arrive on time, but would not have any extra time with my friends before going into class. \r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(224, 'The bus arrived at the school bus stop and I jumped off. I could just see some of my friends and wanted to join them. I rushed up to them and we started chatting about various things. Then the bell rang. We all lined up to go into the classroom and I looked around for my bag. My heart sank when I realised that I did not have my bag. I felt my face go all hot and I put my hand up.\n\n"Yes, Katy, what is it?" the teacher asked me.\n\n"Miss Lee," I started, "I think I''ve left my school bag on the bus." Everyone turned round and stared at me and I felt really awful.\n\n"Well, that was a stupid thing to do!" Miss Lee replied, but then she went on. "Never mind. We''ll sort something out.  Let''s go in everyone." to the classroom.\n\nWe filed in to the classroom. I was still feeling awful but at least I knew the bag was clearly marked with my name, address and telephone number and the name of my school. The teacher lent me some pens and i pencils and we started lessons as usual. She said during recess she would phone the bus company and see if anyone had found my bag.\n\nWhen the bell rang for recess, there was as a knock at the door and the school secretary came into the room. She was carrying my school bag! All the other students left the room and I stayed to thanks the secretary.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(225, 'Apparently, a passenger on the bus had noticed that I had forgotten my bag. This lady picked up the bag and she saw the name of my school inside. She very kindly went out of her way to bring  bag to my school for me! Luckily, the school secretary asked the lady for her name an address so that I could write a letter to thank her for her kindness. ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(226, 'Last Saturday I was really excited. Tom and his family had invited me to join them for a beach barbecue. Tom had explained that it was his brother''s birthday and that his parents were going to organise lots of games. It sounded like really good fun. My parents had agreed to take me to the train station to get the train to the coast. It was only a 15 minute journey and I had taken the same train many times.\r', 'Introduction', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(227, 'We arrived at the station. "Now, have you got everything? Swimming trunks, towel, beach shoes, sun cream?" my mother asked.\n\n"Yes, I''ve got everything!" I replied.\n\n"Well, have a lovely day! And we''ll be here to pick you up at five o''clock,"  she said.\n\nI jumped on the train, waved goodbye to my parents, and settled down for the short journey. I looked out of the windows. The city passed quickly and we were in the  countryside heading for the coast. After another five minutes, I could see the sea shining blue in the sunshine.\n\nI got off the train and looked at the instructions Tom had sent me. "Take the beach road from the station. When you get to the beach, go to the right. You will see a small café and a souvenir shop. We will be on the beach just in front of these." I walked along the road until I came to the beach. It looked beautiful with clean, golden sand. I turned right and walked along till I came to the café and shop. I scanned the beach but could not see Tom or his family.\n\n"Was I in the right place?" I asked myself. I checked the instructions again carefully. Yes, I was in the right place. I checked the time. It was midday, just as Tom had said. "Perhaps, they are caught in the traffic," I thought. I knew the roads out of the city were very busy on Saturdays.\n\nI waited for about 15 minutes and then began to get a bit worried. I could not decide whether to ring Tom or to ring my parents. I thought it might be a bit rude to ring Tom. He might think I was angry at him for being late. So, I decided to ring home. I explained what had happened to my mother. She asked me if I was sure that I had followed the instructions carefully. I told her I had. "Well," she said. "I''ll just go and look at the email Tom sent you and check. Wait a moment."\n\nThrough the phone I could hear my mother walking into my room and clicking on the computer. "Right!" she said. "I''ve got the email in front of me" She went through the instructions again. "Yes," I said impatiently. "I''m standing in front of the café and souvenir shop and I''ve been here since midday!" Then i heard her say, "Oh, dear!"\n\n"What''s the matter?" I asked. "Well, you''ve got the right place and the right time. But, the barbecue is on Sunday not today! You''d better get the next train home!"\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(228, 'I walked back to the station feeling a bit upset until I remembered! I had not missed the barbecue because I could come back to the beach again tomorrow! ', 'Conclusion', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(229, 'I love animals but, unfortunately, my parents have always said that we cannot have any pets. One of my sisters is allergic to animal hair and we live in a fairly small flat so keeping pets is not really possible. Our neighbour, Mr Jones, on the other hand, has quite a few pets. He has a small dog, a canary and some fish. I get on well with Mr Jones and often walk his dog with him and help out looking after his animals. \r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(230, 'One day, I had finished all my chores at home and was at a loose end. I saw Mr Jones leaving his flat and asked if there was anything he wanted me to do. He said he was just going to the supermarket, but if I wanted to go into his flat and play with his dog, I could.\n\nI checked with my mother that it was all right for me to go next door and then I went. The dog was asleep in its basket and did not even raise its head when I went in, so I thought I would leave it alone. The bright yellow canary, Tweetie, was chirping merrily in its cage, so I went over to it and started talking to it. I noticed that its cage was not very clean so I decided to clean it. It would be a nice surprise for Mr Jones.\n\nI had already seen Mr Jones clean the cage, so I knew how to do it and where the clean sand was kept. But I forgot one important thing! I got everything ready, old newspapers, a plastic bag for the used sand and opened the cage door. The bird looked at me calmly for a moment and then it hopped into the doorway of the cage and before I could do anything, it flew out and straight over to the window. It was that moment that I realised I had forgotten to check that the windows were closed and to my horror I saw that one was open!\n\nI called out the bird''s name and tried to stay calm,  "Tweetie! Tweetie! Come here! " The bird just stayed where it was. I got some bird food and put it in a little dish near the cage. I rattled the food. The  bird hopped along the window sill, and cocked its head at me.\n\nAt that very moment, I heard a key turn in the front door. Mr Jones was back! At the sound of the door opening, Tweetie flew away from the window and perched on top of its cage. I rushed over to the window and shut it just as Mr Jones walked into the room.\n"Oh that''s nice," said Mr Jones. "You''re cleaning the cage for Tweetie." He, gave a quick glance over to the window. "I''m glad you closed the window first, though. Tweetie could easily have flown away."\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(231, 'I am afraid to say that I did not confess that I had only just closed it and that indeed,   Tweetie had nearly flown away. I just mumbled something and finish cleaning the cage. I felt incredibly relieved that everything had turned out all right in the end. ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(232, 'My mother and I had been to the cinema together. After the film, we were walking along the street talking about what we had seen and saying how much we had enjoyed it. It was about five o''clock in the afternoon, and the street was not very busy. Most people must have done their shopping and gone home. Ahead of us, I noticed an elderly woman carrying two heavy-looking bags of groceries.  \r', 'Introduction', 'Action'),
(233, 'I was just about to mention her to my mother and to suggest that we could help the woman with her bags, when an extraordinary thing happened! I watch the event as if it were in slow motion, like a scene from a film! This young man in jeans and a white T-shirt came rushing across the road, bumped into the old woman, and then ran off. My mother and I rushed up to the old woman and helped her to stand up. I started collecting her fallen groceries, when she cried, "He''s taken my handbag!"\n\nI look up quickly to where the young man had run.  At the far end of the street, I just spotted him turning into the shopping mall.  Mother got out her mobile phone to ring the police. In what seemed like a few seconds, a police car arrived. We explained what had happened and I described the young man and told the police I had seen him go into the shopping mall.\n\n"Well done!" said the police officer. "Now if you could stay with this poor woman until another police car arrives, my colleague and I will try to find this thief."\n\nOf course, we agreed to stay and helped the old woman to a nearby bench. We knew she was not hurt, but she was very upset by the experience. So, we just chatted about everyday things to try and keep her mind off her lost handbag. After about ten minutes, the second police car arrived and the officers offered to take the woman home.\n\nWe were just helping to put her shopping bags into the back of the car when the first car returned. The police officer came over to the car. He was smiling. "Is this your bag, madam?" he asked the elderly woman.\n\n"Oh, you''ve found it!" she excilaimed. "Oh thank you. Thank you so much!\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Action'),
(234, '"Yes, we found the bag and thanks to this sharp-eye young person, we''ve got the thief as well!" the officer said. ', 'Conclusion', 'Action'),
(235, 'It was my mother''s birthday last Friday and I decided that I would surprise her and make a cake. There is a simple recipe we use for a delicious chocolate cake and I know that it is a favourite with all the family. \r', 'Introduction', 'Family'),
(236, 'I got all the ingredients ready, carefully followed the instructions and prepared the mixture. I checked with my older brother that it was all right to use the oven and he helped me set it at the right temperature and put the cake inside. He then told me he was going out, so I would have to do the rest on my own. That was fine because I knew what to do next.\n\nI set the timer and started to clear up the kitchen, washing the dishes I had used. I had just about finished when my phone rang. It Was my best friend and he wanted to discuss the Maths homework. I went into my room, chatting to Mark on the phone. I sat down at my desk and got out my Maths book.\n\nI suppose I was on the phone for quite a while. When I had finished and left my room I could hear an unfamiliar beeping noise. I went into the kitchen and to my horror could see smoke coming out of the oven. The beeping was the timer that I had forgotten about. Just at that moment I heard the front door opening. Oh no! I thought it was Mum coming back and I had ruined everything.\n\nLuckily it was not Mum, but my brother.\n\n"What''s going on here?" he asked. "What''s that terrible smell?" He threw open the windows in the kitchen, turned off the oven and, taking a pair of thick oven gloves, took my blackened cake out and put it out on the balcony.\n\n"What are we going to do? The cake''s burnt, the kitchen smells of smoke and it''s Mum''s birthday!" I moaned. I felt terribly disappointed that I had ruined everything.\n\n"Never mind," said my brother. "We''ll leave all the windows open for a while. That will get rid of the smell. You make Mum a really special card with a nice picture on it. I''ll phone Dad and ask him to buy something."\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Family'),
(237, 'It all turned out all right. Dad bought Mum a lovely bunch of flowers. My brother made her a CD of her favourite songs and Mum was delighted with my card. Of course, she found out about the cake, but said, "It is the thought that counts." ', 'Conclusion', 'Family'),
(238, 'Last week, I had a most peculiar experience. I had gone to bed at my usual time and had fallen asleep quickly. However, a bit later I woke up again. I looked around my bedroom and wondered what could have woken me up. It was then I realised that there was a lot of light in my room and it seemed to be coming through the window. I checked my watch. It was two o''clock in the morning, so it certainly was not the sun! \r', 'Introduction', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(239, 'I got out of bed and went to the window. Drawing the curtains apart, I looked out. Immediately I could see where the light was coming from. There was a strange object in the sky and it was brightly lit up. What could it be?" I asked myself.\n\nI continued to look and watch, absolutely fascinated. I was sure it was an alien spaceship! I thought about waking up the rest of the family, but thought I might miss something while I was getting them. The object seemed to be getting nearer and nearer, and the lights got brighter and brighter. And then suddenly, it disappeared from sight!\n\nAt this point, I rushed into my parents, room and told them what I had seen. They both thought that I had imagined or dreamt it. "No, it was real!" I said. "I was wide-awake. I didn''t dream it and I''m sure it was aliens from outer space!" My father returned with me to my bedroom and we both looked out of the window. There was nothing to see. "Go back to sleep," my father said. "you''ve got a busy day tomorrow."\n\nI got back into bed very disappointed that my parents did not believe what I had seen. However, I soon fell asleep.\n\nThe next morning, I got up as usual and went into the kitchen. My mother was preparing breakfast and my father was listening to the early morning news on the radio.\n\n"The city council wishes to apologise for any inconvenience caused by last night''s fire-prevention practice," the newscaster said. "Helicopters from the Fire Department were practising over the city and a number of citizens have complained about the disturbance. However, the council wishes to remind citizens that, for their own security, these drills are essential."\n\n"So, that''s what you saw!" my father said "The Fire Department''s helicopters."\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(240, '"It must have been," I said. But secretly I still believed that I had seen an alien spaceship! ', 'Conclusion', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(241, 'Last Saturday I cycled through the park on my way to my piano lesson. I had plenty of time and was enjoying the ride. Suddenly, I realised that ahead of me, there was a small child lying on the ground. \r', 'Introduction', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(242, 'I slowed down when I got closer to the child and jumped off my bike. A boy of about three or four years old was lying on the ground, crying. I laid my bike on its side and went up to the boy. I was worried that he was seriously injured, but he sat up and looked at me as I approached.\n\n"What''s the matter?" I asked him. "Are you all right?"\n\nThe small boy just started sobbing even louder.\n\n"Come on," I tried to calm him down. "It''s all right. My name''s Emily. What''s your name?" I sat down on the grass next to him. I wanted to be friendly and gain his trust.\n\n"James," he said after a pause.\n\n"Well, James," I replied, "what are you doing here?"\n\n"I fell over," he sobbed.\n\n"Right, well, are you hurt?" James showed me his grazed knee, which was bleeding a little. Eventually, he told me that he was having a picnic with his family and that they were playing hide and seek. He had run off to hide behind a tree, but had fallen over and no one had come to look for him. I decided that I would have to take him round the park until I found his family. I heard a voice shouting "James! James!" A girl of about my age came into view and James told me it was his sister. We shouted and waved at her.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(243, 'When the girl came up to us, she started to tell James off for wandering so far away, but when she saw that he had been crying, she softened her tone. James cheered up when he saw her and I explained what had I happened. She thanked me for consoling him and led him back to the family picnic. I picked up my bike and I went on my way.  ', 'Conclusion', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(244, 'Jack and I were best friends. One day he told me about a great new DVD film he had watched, and so I asked him if I could borrow it. Of course, he agree straightaway. I took the film home and thought I would watch i over the weekend. \r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(245, 'The next Saturday, my brother and I sat down to watch the film. Jack was right. It was a great film and my brother and I really enjoyed it. I remember that at the end of watching, my mother called and said it was time to go to bed. I ejected the DVD, put it back in its box and put it on the shelf above my desk.\n\nA couple of weeks passed and Jack asked for the film back. "Sure," I said. "I''ll bring it to school tomorrow." That evening, I went home and decided to put the DVD into my school bag while I remembered. I went into my bedroom and looked on the shelf. The DVD was not there! "Where could it be?" I thought to myself. I searched my bedroom, looking on my desk and in my drawers. It was nowhere to be seen!\n\nThen, I thought perhaps my brother had borrowed it. I went to him and asked but he said he had not seen the DVD since we had watched it together. Now, I was really worried. What would Jack say if I told him I had lost his DVD? I searched again but still could not find it.\n\nThe next morning I woke up for school with a heavy heart. Jack would be very angry. Perhaps, my parents would advance my pocket money so that I could buy him a new copy. I went into the kitchen and explained the situation to my parents.\n\n"Would it be possible to have my pocket money in advance so that I can replace Jack''s film?" I asked.\n\n"Do you mean this DVD?" my mother asked, holding up Jack''s film.\n\n"Yes, where did you find it?" I replied.\n\n"Well, I knew you had borrowed the film from Jack so I thought I''d leave it here in the kitchen so you wouldn''t forget to return i.t You know how untidy your bedroom is!" said my mother.\n\n"Thank goodness!" I said. "Now, I can give it back to Jack."\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(246, 'I went to school feeling happy. When I saw Jack, I rushed over to him and returned the film. I told him how much my brother and had enjoyed it. But, I did not tell him that I thought I had lost the DVD forever! ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(247, 'On Saturday my mother wanted to clean the flat from top to bottom and she asked me to take my little sister out for a walk. \r', 'Introduction', 'Family'),
(248, '"Take Polly to the park," said Mum. "Here''s some money to buy her an ice cream and something for yourself as well."\n\nI took Polly''s hand and we set off to the park. As we were walking along, I wondered what to buy for myself. It was a hot afternoon so an ice cream would have been nice, but finally I decided to go to the newsagent''s and see if I had enough money to get the magazine Science Today, which I enjoyed reading.\n\nWe walked through the park to the ice cream stand. There Polly took a long time to decide on the flavour she wanted. Eventually she chose strawberry. I bought the ice cream for her and handed it over to her. Her face lit up and she gave me a big smile as she thanked me. We started to walk towards the park gate as I explained that I wanted to go to buy my magazine. Polly was gazing at her ice cream and not where she was walking. Suddenly she tripped over a stone and stumbled. She tabbed my hand and dropped the ice cream onto the sandy path.\n\nPolly turned to me aghast. She bent down to pick up the ruined ice cream, but I had to stop her.\n\n"No, Polly," I started, "you can''t have it now. It''s all dirty." Her little face crumpled up and she started to cry. Her sobs were heart-rending and I was sorry to see her so upset\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Family'),
(249, 'I took the money I had left out of my pocket and coined it. I had enough to buy Polly another ice cream, but that meant I would not be able to  afford my magazine. One more glance at my unhappy sister convinced me. I turned back to the ice cream stand and I bought her another ice cream. This time I took her over a bench and we sat while she ate it. I would have to wait another week to buy my magazine.', 'Conclusion', 'Family'),
(250, 'Last night I went to bed quite early because I was very tired. I had been at school during the day and then had been to basketball practice. I got into bed and thought about reading for a while, but in the end I just turned off the light and went straight to sleep.\r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(251, 'Then, in the middle of the night, I woke up suddenly. I wondered what had woken me up, but the house was quiet and all my family were asleep. I picked up my glass, had a sip of water, and then tucked down into bed to go back to sleep. It was then I heard a noise! It was a strange sound like a creaking but not very loud. I listened again but the noise had stopped. I settled back into bed when the noise started again.\n\nBy now, I was wide awake so I turned on the light and kept listening. This time the sound continued. “What could it be?” I wondered. I was not afraid because I was sure the noise was not made by a person. “Could it be an animal?” I asked myself.\n\nThere was only one way to find out. I got up and opened the bedroom door. The hall was dark and quiet. I did not turn the light on because I did not want to wake the rest of the family. I could hear the noise more clearly now. It seemed to be coming from the sitting room. I tiptoed down the hall, and slowly opened the door into the living room.\n\nYes, the noise was definitely louder. I turned on the light and looked around. The noise stopped so I could not see where it was coming from. Then I noticed that the door onto the balcony was slightly open. This made me a little scared and I thought about getting my parents. Then, I felt a slight breeze and the balcony door moved in the wind… and creaked as it moved!\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(252, '“That’s all it is!” I said to myself with some relief. I closed the door firmly and went back to bed. “I must remember to tell the others to check the door the next time!” I thought. “I don’t want my sleep disturbed again!”', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(253, 'I love going to the park with my grandmother. She always enjoys seeing the beautiful flowers and she is incredibly knowledgeable about all the different types of flowers and plants. She tells me their names and origins and something interesting about them. Each year the park has different displays of flowers and for us city-dwellers it is a haven of peace and beauty.\r', 'Introduction', 'Family'),
(254, 'One day however, my grandmother and I were shocked to see a girl walking in a flowerbed and bending over as if she was examining the flowers.\n\n“What is she doing?” I asked my grandmother.  “She’ll damage the flowers by walking through the bed like that.”\n\nMy grandmother did not reply and we made our way slowly towards the girl. As we got closer, we could see that she was picking some of the flowers and that she already had quite a big bunch in her hand! I was amazed that someone should do such a thing. I felt angry and wanted to make the girl stop.\n\n“What are you doing?” I shouted to the girl.\n\nThe girl looked a little embarrassed and said, “They’re not for me. These flowers are for my mother.”\n\n“You can’t pick them!” I started to shout at her crossly. My grandmother stopped me with a hand on my arm.\n\n“Come here,” she said to the girl. The girl obeyed and stepped out of the flowerbed to where we were standing.\n\n“Now look at the flowers,” Grandmother went on. “Tell me what you can see.”\n\nThe girl look rather surprised but she replied, “Flowers; I can see the beautiful flowers.”\n\n“Yes,” said grandmother,  “and they are for everyone to enjoy. If you pick some, there will be fewer left. If everyone picked some, there would be none there. If your mother likes flowers, why doesn’t she come to the park to see them?”\n\n“My mother is ill. She can’t leave the house,” the girl replied, sadly.\n\n“Well, in that case,” said Grandmother, “why don’t you paint her a picture of the flowers and give that to her? Then, when she is better, she can come here and there will still be lots of flowers for her to enjoy.”\n\n“Yes, you’re right,” said the girl. “I’ll do that. I’m sorry. I was only thinking about my mother and I wanted to make her happy. But I didn’t think about what would happen if everyone did the same as me!”\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Family'),
(255, 'Grandmother and I continued our walk through the park. I was impressed that Grandmother had made the girl realise her mistake, without shouting at her or getting cross.', 'Conclusion', 'Family'),
(256, 'I woke up last Saturday morning really looking forward to the day. It was my friend Mei’s birthday that day, and she had invited me to her birthday party. I had planned to go shopping in the morning to buy her a present.	\r', 'Introduction', 'Celebrations'),
(257, 'After breakfast, my older sister and I went to the shopping mall to look for something suitable. We were just passing the bookshop when I remembered that Mei was reading the ‘Wizard’ series of books, but she was only up to the second book. “I know!” I told my sister. “I’ll buy Mei the third book in the ‘Wizard’ series. She really enjoyed the first two.“  I bought the book and some nice wrapping paper and a card.\n\nThat afternoon, after wrapping the present carefully. I walked the short distance to Mei’s flat. I rang the bell and Mei answered the door. “Happy birthday!” I said and gave her the present and card.\n\n“Oh, thank you!” she said. “What lovely wrapping paper! I’ll open the card now but save the present for later.”\n\nWe went in to the living room and I greeted her parents, and said hello to the other guests. Then, Mei’s mother organised some party games and we all had a wonderful time.\n\n“Right!” said Mei’s mother. “Let’s have a break from games now and I’ll bring in the tea.”\n\nWhile Mei and her mother organised the food, I wandered over to the side table to look at Mei’s cards and presents. To my horror, I saw a copy of the same ‘Wizard’ book that I had bought!\n\nWe had our tea but Mei noticed that I was a bit quiet. She came over to me and said, “What’s the matter? Aren’t you enjoying yourself?”\n\nYes, I am.” I said. “I’m having a lovely time but I’m really disappointed about the present I’ve got you.” I explained about the book.\n\n“Oh, don’t worry!” Mei said. “It was a lovely thought and I know what to do.”\n\n“What?” I asked.\n\n“I’ll take one copy of the book back to the bookshop and exchange it for the fourth book in the series! What could be better?”\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Celebrations'),
(258, '“Oh, what a good idea!” I said. “Now, I can really enjoy the party!”', 'Conclusion', 'Celebrations'),
(259, 'Yesterday after school I decided to take a short cut on the way home. Instead of following the main road I turned off and took a narrow street. It was getting dark and the streets were quite dimly lit. There was no one else around.\r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(260, 'There was a sharp bend in the road and as I turned the corner I came face to face with a large angry-looking dog. It was loose, just standing in the middle of the street. There was no one near it; no one else in the street.\n\nI do not really like dogs and this one was quite scary. It seemed to stare at me in a threatening way. A low growl came from its throat as it took a few paces towards me. I began to feel very frightened. I knew that if I started to run, the dog could run faster. That would be a stupid thing to do. I also remembered hearing that if you are frightened, animals can sense it.\n\nI took a deep breath and in a stern voice said, “Go away! Go home!”\n\nThe dog stopped growling and looked at me.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(261, '“Go on!” I continued. “Go home!” I stood still and waited. After a while the dog lost interest in me and turned away. I waited until it had walked off a few metres, then I too, turned around and quietly walked back to main road. I could feel my heart beating loudly in my chest, but I controlled my desire to run as fast as I could. I walked calmly on and safely reached the busy main road. I decided not to take that short cut again!', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(262, 'My father and I had had a great day out together walking through the park. We had walked for miles and It was getting late. “I think it is time to go home,” my father said.\r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(263, '“Yes,” I agreed because it was beginning to get dark and I was quite tired.\n\nWe walked briskly back to where we had parked the car. We got in, put on our seat belts, and Dad started the car. We had been travelling for about half an hour, happily chatting about all the things we had seen in the park, when the car suddenly started making a funny noise.\n\n“What’s that noise, Dad?” I asked.\n\n“I’m not sure,” he replied. “But I don’t think it is anything serious.”\n\nHe had hardly finished speaking when the car started to slow down and then came to a complete standstill.\n\n“Oh, no!” said Dad. He turned on the warning lights, got out of the car and quickly found the warning triangle. “I’ll put this up so that other drivers are warned,” he told me. “Meanwhile, just stand away from the road and car.”\n\nI did what he said. When he came back, he lifted the bonnet and started to examine the engine but he could not see what the problem was. By this time, I was feeling very tired and It was beginning to get cold too. I started worrying that we could never get home!\n\n“What are we going to do, Dad?” I asked.\n\n“Don’t worry!” he replied. “I’ll phone the insurance company and they’ll send a pick-up for us.” Dad phoned the emergency number he had on his mobile phone and explained the situation. Then he turned to me and said that he expected the tow truck to arrive in about 30 minutes.\n\n“I’ll just ring your mother so that she isn’t worried,” he said.\n\nAs we waited, I realised that no other traffic passed us. It was a very quiet and isolated place to break down. I thought it was a bit scary. I kept looking up the road hoping to see the headlights of the rescue vehicle.\n\nEventually, after what seemed like hours, I looked up the road again, and in the distance I could just make out some faint lights.\n\n“Look, Dad!” I exclaimed. “Lights!”\n\nThe lights grew stronger and brighter and suddenly the rescue vehicle was parked beside us, its orange lights flashing.\n\nThe mechanic quickly looked over the car engine and then explained that the car would need to go into a garage to be fixed. He told my father that he would put the car onto the back of his vehicle and he would drive us home.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(264, 'This was almost the best part of the day, sitting up in a big truck with orange lights flashing and knowing we would get home safely after all.', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(265, 'For my birthday I had been given some money by my grandparents, and this meant that I could buy the DVD I wanted. So, last Saturday I went to the shopping mall with my mother. Mum wanted to look round some clothes shops, so we arranged to separate and meet up later.\r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(266, 'I walked into the busy CD and DVD shop and looked through the racks of DVDs on sale. I quickly found the one I wanted, but spent a few minutes sifting through the new releases.\n\nAfter a while, I looked at my watch and realised that if I did not hurry up, I would be late meeting my mother. I went to the cashier and paid for my DVD, put it in my backpack and started to walk out of the shop.\n\nI was just going through the doorway when the shoplifting alarm was set off. A loud repetitive noise echoed all over the mall. I stopped immediately and looked around. I was the only person walking through the doorway at that time. A security guard was making his way over to me and I thought that everyone had stopped to stare at me. I felt nervous, but knew I had done nothing wrong.\n\nThe security guard asked me to follow him over to the desk. He asked me to open my bag and show him what was inside. I did as he asked and took everything out of my backpack. When the guard saw the DVD, he asked if I had a receipt for it. Of course I did, so I gave it to him. He nodded his thanks and examined it carefully. He then picked up the DVD and took it over to the cashier.\n\nA few minutes later, the guard came over, gave me my DVD and accompanied me through the doorway. This time no alarm went off.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(267, '“It was just a problem with the anti-theft device on the DVD. It’s all right now,” the security guard explained to me. “Thank you for your understanding!” The guard left me and I hurried off to meet Mum and tell her why I was a few minutes late!', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(268, 'About three weeks ago, I noticed a competition that was being advertised on the school notice board. It looked very interesting. The competition was to write an essay describing what the world would be like in 2050. I really like science fiction and technology and things like that, so I thought the topic was very interesting. What’s more, the prize for the best essay was a bike. I had wanted a new bike for some time as my old one was now too small for me. So, I decided to enter the competition.\r', 'Introduction', 'Technology'),
(269, 'I spent the next few days racking my brains for ideas. I thought about ‘intelligent’ houses. I thought about how people would travel. And, I thought about the climate and how it might have changed. When I had enough ideas, I sat down and wrote my essay. When I was finished, I read through it and checked for mistakes. I made a few changes and then read the essay again. I thought it was quite good.\n\nI went to the competition site on the web and looked at how to submit my entry. It was all very easy and I sent the essay off. Almost instantly, I got an email saying that my entry had arrived. The instructions said that the essays would be judged two weeks later, on the 25th and that the names of the winners, and their essays would be posted on the website.\n\nThe next two weeks passed as usual and then it was the 25th. That morning, I got up quickly and immediately checked the website but the results were not on it. Perhaps it was too early. Unfortunately, it was a school day so I could not spend any more time on the computer. The school day seemed to pass slowly because I really wanted to find out if I had won the competition or not!\n\nAt the end of the school day, I rushed home and went straight to my room and clicked on the competition website. Instead of seeing that page, there was a notice saying there was no connection. I looked at the bottom of my computer screen. Oh no! There was no WiFi! I had no internet connection! “What can I do?” I thought to myself. “The public library has computers but it is too far away. I don’t know anyone with a computer on this floor.” Then I had a great idea.\n\nI rang my best friend, Lee, and explained the situation to him. I told him how desperate I was to find out if I won a bike or not. I gave him the web address, and waited with bated breath while he logged on.\n\n“Are the results there?” I asked.\n\n“Hold on a minute!” he said. “I’m just looking, yes, the results are here. Oh, that’s a pity!”\n\n“I haven’t won,” I said miserably.\n\n“Well, you haven’t won the first prize, but you have won the second prize of $100! Well done!”\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Technology'),
(270, '“$100!” I exclaimed. “That’s brilliant! Now I can start saving to buy myself a new bike!”', 'Conclusion', 'Technology'),
(271, 'I love going swimming and I usually go at least once a week. I am hoping to get into the school swimming team and for that I must practise regularly. I also like having fun in the water with my friends, and often meet them in the pool.\r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(272, 'Last Saturday afternoon, I had arranged to meet my friends outside the pool. We went in and paid the entrance fee. In the changing room, I started to get my things out of my bag but to my horror I realised that my swimming things were not there! I had forgotten to put them in. I felt such a fool. How could I manage to forget something like that?\n\n“Oh no!” I exclaimed.\n\n“What’s the matter?” Lucy asked me.\n\n“I’ve forgotten my swimming things.”\n\n“What? Are you sure?” Molly questioned.\n\n“What am I going to do?” I moaned.\n\nLucy suggested that perhaps they had swimming things you could borrow or hire at the entrance desk. I went off to see. Unfortunately, the lady at the desk could not help me. They did not have anything I could borrow. I could not go swimming.\n\nI went back to my friends and explained the situation. At first I thought I would have time to go back home, get my things and then join them at the pool. But in the end we worked out that it would take too long. I felt disappointed, but had only myself to blame.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(273, 'I left my friends at the pool and went home. Mum was surprised to see me back so soon. When I explained what had happened, she consoled me saying, “Oh well, you won’t do that again, I’m sure!” And she’s right. I will always make doubly sure that I have all my things when I go swimming again!', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(274, 'It was a late Saturday afternoon and my parents, my brother and I were all at home. I was playing chess with my brother, my mother was emailing my aunt, and my father was settling into his armchair to read the paper. \r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(275, '“Bother!” my father suddenly exclaimed. “I must have left my reading glasses in the car.”\n\n“I’ll get them for you, Dad,” I offered.\n\n“Thank you!” replied my father as he handed me the car keys.\n\nI left the flat, went down in the lift, and came out into the street. “Now, where has Dad parked the car?” I asked myself. Then I spotted the black saloon a hundred metres or so down the road. I walked down the road till I got to the car. I put the key in the lock to open the door and suddenly, there was aloud wailing noise. It was the car alarm! At the same time, a man who was walking down the street, started running towards me and shouting.\n\nI was so astonished I dropped the car keys. As I bent down to pick them up, the man grabbed hold of my arm.\n\n“Are you trying to steal my car?” he demanded.\n\n“No, sir!” I stuttered.\n\n“Then what exactly are you doing?” he asked still holding onto my arm. It was at that point that I looked at the car more carefully. Oh no, it was not my Dad’s car!\n\nI explained what had happened to the man and showed him the keys. He looked at me carefully and then released my arm.\n\n“We had better try to find your father’s car,” he said.\n\nWe both looked up and down the road and then I spotted it.\n\n“That’s it!” I said pointing to the black saloon just across the road. “That’s my father’s car!”\n\n“Well,” said the man, “be more careful next time! You don’t want someone else thinking you are a car thief, do you?”\n\nI crossed the road and this time, I checked the care carefully before putting the keys in. it was all right! It was my father’s car! I found his glasses and then locked the car. I went back to the flat and gave the glasses to my father.\n\n“Thank you!” he said. “But what took you so long?”\n\nI told him what had happened.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(276, '“The man was right,” my father said. “You must be more careful in future.”', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(277, 'Every Friday, I take the bus to go visit my grandparents. Last Friday, I was waiting at the bus stop as usual when I was witness to a car accident.\r', 'Introduction', 'Accidents and Disasters');
INSERT INTO `astarcompo_templates` (`templateID`, `template`, `templateType`, `theme`) VALUES
(278, 'I was the only person waiting at the bus stop. I checked my watch. It was 4.50pm. Normally, there would be a bus in five minutes. It was quite a busy time of the day so there was a reasonable amount of traffic. I hoped that the bus would not be held up.\n\nThen I noticed a young man coming down the street with a dog at his heels. The dog looked vey well-behaved and was calmly trotting along. Suddenly the dog stopped. Suddenly, the dog stopped. It was then I noticed that it was not on a lead. The young man called to it but the dog ignored him. It lifted its head and looked across the road. The next thing I saw was the dog running into the traffic to cross the road!\n\nA car coming along the road swerved to the right to avoid the dog and drove straight into another car, coming in the opposite direction! There was a loud bang as the two cars collided. Passers-by stopped and stared as the two drivers got out of their cars, thankfully, unhurt.\n\nEveryone stayed calm but the driver of the second car could not understand why the first car had driven into him.\n\n“I swerved to avoid the dog,” the first driver said.\n\n“What dog?” asked the second driver.\n\nSure enough, there was no trace of the dog or its owner. “Did anyone else see a dog?”\n\nI realised that I has a duty to inform the drivers that I had seen everything. So I walked over to them and explained what I had seen. By that time, someone had called the police and within a few minutes they arrived. The police took control of the situation and asked everyone to move along. They took my name and address as a witness and I described the dog and its owner.\n\nThe police interviewed a few other witnesses including one person who recognised the dog owner. It was clear that the dog owner was responsible for causing the accident. The police said that they would be contacting him very soon.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(279, 'After about fifteen minutes, the road was cleared and the traffic was moving normally. In fact, the cars were not badly damaged and both of them could be driven away. I caught my bus and had a story to tell my grandparents when I finally arrived!', 'Conclusion', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(280, 'Last weekend I had a very embarrassing time. It was a normal Saturday. Dad was out playing golf, my sisters had gone to see some friends, my mother was preparing dinner, and I was playing a game on my computer. \r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(281, 'Then, my mother called out to me. "Would you mind going to the supermarket for me? I need a few things but I need to keep an eye on the cooking."\n\n"No, of course not I replied. "\n\nMy mother gave me handed me the Shopping basket. "Take some money from my purse,"  she said\n\nI went into  my room to get my shoes and jacket. I looked at the computer screen and realised that I had nearly finished the game. "I''ll quickly finish this game and then I''ll go to the supermarket," I said to myself.\n\nThe next thing I knew, my mother came into the room. "Haven''t you gone  yet?" she dinner asked.   "Hurry up! I  need those things for dinner."\n\nI dashed out of the flat and down to the shop. I wandered around the supermarket gathering all the things on my mother''s list. Then I went to the checkout. There was a long queue. I waited and waited and slowly moved forward. Eventually, it was my turn. The young woman on the till, scanned my shopping and then said , "That will be 20 dollars and 15 cents."\n\nI felt in my pocket for the money. "Oh, no!" I said. "I''m sorry I''ve left my money at home! What shall I do?" I felt myself go very red. I could feel the other people in the queue behind me getting a bit impatient.\n\nThe young woman was very kind. "Put the shopping in your bag and leave it with me. Go and get your money and when you come back, you can come straight to me. You don''t have to queue again."\n\nI thanked her and rushed home. As I burst in through the door, I saw my mother standing there holding her purse. I apologised to her and explained what the young woman had said.\n\n"Go back to the shop and pay," my mother said. "But do not jump the queue. It was your mistake so you must line up again!"\n\nI returned to the supermarket and did what my mother had said. I joined a long queue and waited patiently for my turn. When I got to the checkout, the young woman said, "I told you to come to the front of the queue."\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(282, '"It was my mistake," I explained. "So it was only right that I should queue again." ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(283, 'My family and I are very lucky to live in a house with a garden. My sisters and I love playing outside and my parents are keen gardeners. My mother is particularly  fond of her flowerbeds and grow a stunning array flowers. My father is keener on growing vegetables and we all enjoy the delicious fresh vegetables he grows.\r', 'Introduction', 'Family'),
(284, 'One day I walked outside into the garden where my parents were busy mowing the grass and weeding the flowerbeds. They looked so busy I thought I ought to offer help.\n\n"Is there anything I can do to help?'' I asked.\n\nMy parents exchange glances.\n\n"Thank you," said Dad "Actually, you can tidy the vegetable patch. That would be a great help".\n\nI was not quite sure what he meant, but  before I could ask he a started up the mower again and was cutting the grass. I looked at my mother enquiringly.\n\n"Here you are!" she said and handed me some tools. "Put the weeds in the a wheelbarrow." She too turned away and continued her work.\n\nI do not know much about plants but I do know that weeds are plants that grow where you do not want them. Usually, they are small and not very interesting to look at. I felt quite excited about the task I had been given and pleased that I could help my parents in this responsible way.\n\nThe vegetable patch had been divided up into rows. I could see lines of small plants and guessed that they must be something my father had planted. One end of the patch looked a bit messy. There was nothing growing there but someone had left some sticks in the ground. That must be what my father meant, I thought. I carefully picked up all the sticks and Iaid them on the ground in a heap. Then I looked round to see what else I could do.\n\nJust then Dad came over. "How are you getting on?" he asked.\n\n"Fine." I replied.\n"Oh no!" Dad exclaimed. "Someone has removed all my markers." He picked up the sticks had taken out.\n\n"Oh, that was me," I confessed.\n\nDad explained that he had sown seeds in that area and the sticks were in fact markers showing where and what he had had sown. I had not noticed that the sticks had writing on them, ''carrots'', ''leeks'' and so on. I felt terrible. I had ruined the garden just then Mum came over.\n\n"Don''t worry," she said when she heard what I had done. "When the seeds grow a few inches in height we will recognise the plants. You haven''t really done any damage." Dad agreed with her. He had just been a bit surprised.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Family'),
(285, 'Afterwards, Mum gave me another job but this time she explained carefully how to do it!', 'Conclusion', 'Family'),
(286, 'It started off a normal day. I had got up and got ready for school as usual. I picked up my school bag, said goodbye to my parents, and left the flat. I walked down the corridor to the lift and pressed the button. \r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(287, 'The doors opened immediately and I stepped into the lift. I pressed the button for the ground floor and the doors closed. But the lift did not move. I pressed the button again. The lift still did not move. I decided to get out of the lift and walk down the stairs but when I pressed the door button, nothing happened. I was stuck!\n\nAt first I was not worried because I had decided to ring my parents on my mobile and they could come and help me. I took out my phone and tried to ring them, but there was no signal inside the lift. I looked at the emergency buttons but I did not want to inconvenience the security people. However, I was beginning to feel a little nervous and a little bit hot.\n\nI decided to bang on the lift door and hope someone would hear me and come to my rescue. I started banging on the door and Shouting. "Help! Help!" I called. "I''m stuck in the lift. Can anyone hear me?"\n\nThere was no reply. I tried again but there was still no reply. By now I was getting a little scared. I wondered how much air there was in the lift.\n\nI decided that I would bang on the door and shout one more time and, if nobody came, I would press the emergency button. I lifted up my fist to bang on the door, when he doors opened and in front of me I saw my father.\n\n"What are you doing here?" he asked.\n\nI explained what had happened. "That must have been a bit frightening," he said gently.\n\n"It was a bit," I replied.\n\n"Well, let''s put a notice on the lift so that no one else gets trapped, and then we''ll walk down the stairs and report the fault to the security guard."\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(288, 'I have to say I was very relieved to get out of the lift and very happy to walk down the stairs. ', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(289, 'This term we get quite a lot of homework and yesterday was no exception. I had just finished my Maths exercises and starting the Science exercises, when my mother said she was going out to the supermarket to get something she had forgotten. My father was not home yet, so I would be on my own for a few minutes. \r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(290, 'As Mum closed the front door I settled down to read through the work that had been set. After a few minutes, I was startled by the phone ringing in the living room. I got up to answer it and as I lifted the receiver I heard a woman''s voice asking to speak to me.\n\n"Speaking," I replied.\n\n"This is Fiona Lee," the lady continued. "I work for Kingsdown Publications. You recently entered a short story competition and I have some very good news for you."\n\nMy heart started beating faster as I remembered the competition that I had seen in my monthly magazine. I had written a short story entitled ''My happiest day'' and had sent it in at least two months ago. I had almost completely forgotten about it.\n\n"Yes?" I stuttered.\n\n"Congratulations! Your story has won first prize," Ms Lee continued. "You will be receiving confirmation by post in a few days, but I wanted to tell you straightaway.\n\nI was delighted to hear the news and could hardly believe it. I thanked Ms Lee profusely. She said she was looking forward to meet me at the prize giving in a few weeks'' time. I then remembered the prize was a notebook computer!\n\nI hung up the phone and could not wait for my mother to comeback from the shops. After what seemed like ages I heard the front door opening and I ran out to greet my mother.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(291, '"You''ll never guess what!" I said, and then proceed to give her my very good news.', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(292, 'I love singing an choir and so I joined the school. The choir master had very standard and we practised very hard. But he was very kind, and we all enjoyed the work. Last month we had had to practise even harder because we were giving a concert. I was going to sing a solo for the first time, so I was excited and a bit nervous too. \r', 'Introduction', 'Family'),
(293, 'The night before the concert, we had one last rehearsal. At the end of it, the choir master said, "Well done, everyone! You have all worked very hard. Good luck for the concert tomorrow and I advise everyone to get an early night!" We all left happily. When I arrived home, I told my parents all about the rehearsal and about the choir master''s advice.\n\n"I think that is very sensible advice," my mother said. "You should have an early night tonight so that you are bright and ready for the concert.\n\nI went to bed quickly and fell asleep. The next morning my alarm clock went off, and I woke up. I felt awful! I had a terrible headache! I got up and went into the kitchen an told my mother how I felt. She was very sympathetic because she knew how much I was  looking forward to concert.\n\n"Listen!" she said. "Its Saturday and the concert is not until this evening. Go back to bed and I''ll bring you a headache tablet and some water. Then you can try to rest\n\nI felt so unhappy about the concert that I nearly cried. I took the tablet, drank some water and fell asleep again. I woke up a few hours later when my mother came into my room.\n\n"How are you feeling now?" she asked.\n\n"It''s gone!" I exclaimed. "My headache''s gone. I feel fine!"\n\n"Good!" said my mother. "It was probably all the excitement and worry about the concert that gave you the headache. You must spend the rest of the afternoon quietly and then I''m sure you will be fine for the concert this evening."\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Family'),
(294, 'I did what my mother said and she was right! That evening I sang my solo at the concert. My dream had come true. ', 'Conclusion', 'Family'),
(295, '"Ni hao, wo de ming zi jiao Hong Yi" greeted   the petite girl in Beijing accented Mandarin. Her pink, rosy cheek and large sparkling eyes accentuated her china-dool features, set out on a canvas of porcelain smooth and clear skin. Standing in front of the class of forty boys and girl, she smiled shyly as she introduced herself. The meaning of her greeting was ''Hello, my name is Hong Yi.''\r', 'Introduction', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(296, 'Hong Yi was a Grade Five student from a public school in Beijing, China. She and five other students from her school were in Singapore for a weeklong student exchange programme. They would be attending our school for a week to experience what it was llike to be a student in Singapore. Each student was posted to a different class and Hong Yi was to join my class. In addition, I had volunteered to be a student host, so Hong Yi would stay with me and my family during her visit in Singapore.\n\nBeing an only child, I was looking forward to having company around for a week. My bilingual education in Singapore made it easy for me to communicate with Hong Yi in Mandarin. However, Hong Yi tried to use as much English as she could. She was in he presence of competent speakers of the English language and did not want to pass up the opportunity to practise her English.\n\nHong Yi sat next to me during lessons. She was a keen student and absorbed conducting experiments in the laboratory. Hong Yi was also very everything like a sponge. Science lessons were the most enjoyable for her. She loved conducting experiments in the laboratory. Hong Yi was also very impressed with the the Internet in the computer laboratory during an English lesson on the theme of ''local festivals''.\n\nHong Yi was a Mathematics whizz who put the rest of the class to shame during Mathematics lessons. Needless to say, she breezed through our Higher Chinese lessons. Our Chinese teacher encouraged us to interact more with Hong Yi and learn more about her language and culture. After all the academic lessons, Hong Yi welcomed Physical Education. That week, we played table tennis and Hong Yi beat all of us.\n\nOutside the classroom, we tried to make Hong Yi feel welcomed by asking her to join us for recess. Hong Yi also introduced her other Chinese friends to us. They were being hosted by students from other classes. However, she did not keep to he own circle of Chinese friends but tried to mix with the local students. Everyone was interested to find out more about the Chinese students. Hong Yi and her counterparts gamely fielded questions pertaining to their country, their culture,  the education system in China and even more personal question about their hobbies, likes and dislikes.\n\nOutside of school, my family and I did our utmost to make Hong Yi feel at home. My Parents took time off from work to take us to a different place of Interests each afternoon after school. In all, we visited the Singapore Zoological Gardens, the Science Centre, National Museum, Singapore Discovery Centre and Chinatown. During the weekend, we took Hong Yi on a fun-filled trip to Sentosa. There was so much to see and do that we were unable to squeeze in all the sights, sounds and tastes for Hong Yi to experience in a mere week. However, we manage to give her a better understanding of Singapore and a sampling of what our sunny island had to offer.\n\nBefore we knew it, it was time for Hong Yi and her fellow Chinese students to go.  On their last day at our school, they treated us to a Chinese dance  performance as a gesture of thanks and appreciation. After that they left for the airport with their chaperone.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(297, 'Student exchange programmes such as this are an excellent way for Singaporean students to interact with their foreign counterparts and vice versa. In this way, we can bring about a better understanding of one another''s country, culture and traditions to promote bonding among people on our small planet. ', 'Conclusion', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(298, 'The moon had retreated to seek refuge behind the clouds. The road leading to Peter''s house was almost pitch-dark, save for a row of streetlights which cast a yellowish glow to dimly light up the path. Peter had just alighted from the bus and was on his way home. He had a pro ject meeting at school which ended at eight. \r', 'Introduction', 'Criminal Acts'),
(299, 'Suddenly, as if out of nowhere, came two youths. If Peter had met the duo elsewhere, he would have thought they were ordinary teenagers, dressed in casual clothes. However, there were sinister expressions on the twoyouths'' faces. Up close, they looked mean and menacing. They surrounded Peter, preventing him from moving forward. One of them whipped out a knife from his pocket and held it to Peter''s neck.\n\n"Hand over your wallet, kid!" demanded the robber. Trembling with fear, Peter did not dare to utter a word. Beads of sweat were streaming down his face. Fearing for his own safety, Peter reached into his pocket for his wallet and handed it over to the robber. The robber grabbed the wallet from Peter''s hand and the two fled in different directions.\n\nPeter stood rooted at that spot, his legs felt like jelly. However, he soon regained his composure and ran home to tell his parents about the incident. Peter''s father immediately accompanied him to a nearby neighbourhood police post to report the robbery. Fortunately, Peter did not forget what he had been told during a personal safety talk given by a police officer at his school a few months before. During the robbery, Peter had studied the facial and bodily characteristics of each of the two robbers and committed it to memory. He was able to give the police a detailed description of what the robbers looked like. Following his description, a sketch artist was able to draw impressions of the two suspects.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Criminal Acts'),
(300, 'Within a week, the robbers were caught when they attempted to prey on another helpless schoolboy. Peter was called to the police station to confirm that the robbers were the same pair who had robbed him previously. After the robbers were caught, Peter and his parents were relieved. Nevertheless, as a safety measure, his parents made it a point to pick him up from the bus stop whenever he came back late from school.', 'Conclusion', 'Criminal Acts'),
(301, '"Come on! This is the best time to do it. If we work together, we will definitely not get caught," Susan whispered conspiratorially into Jasmine''s ear. The pint-sized girl hesitated for a moment before nodding her head in agreement. Recess was the perfect time to steal Naomi''s wallet. The silly girl always boasted about bringing a lot of money to school, yet she never took the wallet with her for recess. When the bell rang, Susan and Jasmine walked in the opposite direction from their classmates, pretending to look for the form teacher. Once they were certain that all their cIassmates had left, they darted back to the classroom and slipped in from the backdoor.\r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(302, 'Susan headed straight to Naomi''s seat and rummaged about in her bag. Jasmine raced to the front door and stood guard. She looked left and right to make sure that no one was walking down the corridor outside. Occasionally, she glanced at Susan. Jasmine was peering to her right when John and Sandy turned the corner and walked down the corridor. Just then, Sandy saw Susan in the classroom. Tugging at John''s sleeve, she pointed at Susan. Shocked, they watched in silence as Susan took out a wallet from the bag. John''s brows creased in a frown. "A thief?" he wondered. His suspicions were proven seconds later when Susan waved the wallet triumphantly in the air and called out, "I''ve found her wallet! Let''s go before we get caught!" The two girls pumped their fists in the air and were about to dash out when they came face to face with John and Sandy.\n\n"We . . . we forgot something," stammered Jasmine while Susan immediately tried to shove the wallet into her pocket.\n\n"I don''t think so." John said firmly. "The both of you are coming with us to the office right now!" Jasmine and Susan tried to push him aside. However, John raised his hands at his sides and formed a barrier to stop Jasmine from escaping. Sandy grabbed Susan''s hands to stop her from running. They then dragged the two girls to the office with them.\n\nAt the office, they recounted the incident to the discipline master. When he demanded to know what had happened, Susan lied that it was Naomi who wanted them to get the wallet from her. However, her lie was exposed when the discipline master called Naomi into the office. Naomi insisted that she never asked Susan to get her wallet for her and was so horrified that she immediately grabbed her wallet to count if all her money was inside. As the theft had been foiled, Susan and Jasmine were let off with a stern warning.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(303, 'The two girls were so shaken by what had happened, that they promised never to steal again. Naomi on the other hand, had learnt her lesson. She never brought large sums of money to school again and she always took her wallet with her when she went for recess.', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(304, 'I let out a voluminous sigh and turned the page of my Science textbook. My eyes were getting blurry and I wished that I did not have to study for the examinations. However, my parents had gone to Malaysia for a few days and I had promised them that I would study hard after school every afternoon. To keep myself awake, I decided to listen to music. I was about to reach for my MP3 player when I heard a loud crash. It resounded throughout the entire house. Jumping up from my seat, I dashed out of my room.\r', 'Introduction', 'Family'),
(305, 'Stepping out of my room, I saw my older brother, Matthew, run out of the front door. "Matt! Where are you going?" I called out, but he ignored me. In a flash, he had disappeared around the corner. Without a second thought, I rushed out of the house to see if I could find out where Matthew had gone. However, he was nowhere to be seen. Scratching my head in bewilderment, I made my way back to the house. Just then, I remembered the loud crash I had heard earlier. Scanning the living room, I noticed that one of the windows was open. We seldom opened that window. When I walked closer, I was stunned to see that my mother''s favourite crystal glass was no longer on the coffee table. It had shattered a distance away, near the kitchen.\n\nAt that moment, Matthew ran back into the house. He was panting and beads of sweat dripped down his forehead in a continuous stream. Bending down to catch his breath, he motioned for me to get the phone. When I did, he immediately called the police. I stared at him, dumbfounded. From what he said on the phone, I gathered that a burglar had entered our house, but Matthew had heard him and chased him out of the house and down the street. He lost sight of him a short distance away. I shook my head in disbelief. I could not believe that anyone would have the audacity to burgle a place in broad daylight.\n\nWhen the police arrived, I listened intently as Matthew recounted what had happened to one of the officers. He was getting a drink from the kitchen when he heard a sound coming from the living room. A stranger in a black T-shirt, black pants and dark grey cap was stealing money from a drawer. When Matthew yelled at him, he was so startled that he grabbed the vase and threw it at him. After stuffing all the money into his pocket, he flung open the front door and ran out of the house. The police officers told us to search the rest of the house to check if anything else was missing. To our relief, only the three hundred dollars in the drawer was gone. Everything else was intact.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Family'),
(306, 'We decided not to inform our parents about the burglary until they returned from their trip since no one was hurt. However, we did keep all the doors and windows tightly locked from that day onwards.', 'Conclusion', 'Family'),
(307, 'Jordan squinted at the colourful object on the floor near the swimming pool. It looked exactly like the latest toy car that he had seen on television. He swivelled about to ask his mother if he could take a closer look at the toy, but she was talking incessantly into the phone and paying no attention to him. Jordan was in a dilemma. His mother had told him not to go too near the swimming pool, but he really wanted to look at the toy. Unable to contain his curiosity, Jordan sprinted towards the toy. He did not notice the huge puddle of water on the floor right in his path.\r', 'Introduction', 'Action'),
(308, '"Help!" shrieked Jordan as his feet slid out from under him and he felt himself falling to the floor. He flailed his arms about, but there was nothing for him to hold on to. He scraped his knee on the rough tiles and landed with such a thump on the floor that the pain shot through his body. Jordan''s face was creased in pain. For several seconds, he sat there, stunned. No one came to his help. His mother was still on the phone and oblivious to what had happened. The life guard was sprawled on a deck chair. He had dozed off.\n\nJordan''s mouth puckered and he looked as though he was about to cry. He glanced around and realised that his slippers had fallen off. They were too far for him to reach. He tried to stand up, but his leg and back hurt too much. Just then. a lady ran towards him, shouting, "Are you all right? You should not run on the wet floor!" She was about to help Jordan up when his mother turned around. She had stopped talking and was startled to see Jordan sitting on the floor. Together with the kind lady, they helped Jordan to his feet. A young boy, the lady''s son, retrieved Jordan''s slippers for him and placed them near Jordan''s feet.\n\nJordan was about to put on his slippers when he remembered the toy. Still unrepentant about what he had done, Jordan pushed his slippers aside and ran towards the toy on the floor. He picked it up and began examining it gleefully. It was the toy that he had always wanted. "Hey! That''s my toy! I dropped it earlier and was coming back to look for it!" shouted the boy who had just kindly given Jordan his slippers a while ago. He snatched the toy from Jordan''s hands and marched off. His mother merely nodded and smiled apologetically at Jordan and his mother before scurrying after her son.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Action'),
(309, 'Jordan looked at his mother dejectedly, but she told him firmly that he should not have been so greedy. It was his greed that had made him fall. Jordan put on his slippers quietly and walked towards the cafe on the second floor with his mother. ', 'Conclusion', 'Action'),
(310, 'Each time I use the purple and green thumb drive that my mother had bought for me, it will remind me of the time I misplaced my thumb drive on the day of an important presentation. The presentation was crucial as my friends and I had worked a long time on the project. As the project leader, I was the one who saved all the work we did. I was responsible for ensuring that our project was a success. \r', 'Introduction', 'Action'),
(311, 'I woke up extra early that Wednesday morning as I was meeting my friends for a last rehearsal before the presentation. After breakfast, I checked my haversack before leaving to make sure that the thumb drive was in it. To my horror, the palm-sized silver thumb drive was missing! A sense of panic coursed through me. I took out everything in my bag and turned it inside out. I even checked the side pockets. The thumb drive was nowhere to be found!\n\nWhen my parents came down for breakfast, they were puzzled to see me kneeling on the floor with all my books and stationery spread out around me. I was almost in tears when I told them what had happened. My mother immediately searched through my things on the floor. However, my father frowned and glared at me. "How could you not have made a backup copy? Why didn''t you print a hardcopy? How could you be so dumb?" he stood there with his arms folded across his chest, chastising me in his harshest tone. I racked my brains, trying hard to remember if one of the other team members had another copy of the presentation. With a sinking feeling, I realised that I had confidently boasted that our presentation would be intact and there was no need to print a hardcopy or for anyone else to do a backup copy. My mother and I turned my entire room upside down to search for the thumb drive. My mother even crawled behind the computer table to search for the thumb drive. Eventually, my father gave in and helped us to search the rest of the house. Still, none of us was able to find the thumb drive.\n\nI had no choice but to leave for school twenty minutes later as I did not want to be late. I had never felt so despondent before. My friends just stared at me open-mouthed when I broke the news to them. I knew that they were livid, but there was nothing that they could do. I took the blame for everything and asked the teacher if we could present our project a week later. She agreed, but she would deduct a few marks from our final score as we were late with our presentation.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Action'),
(312, 'My friends and I spent the next week pouring over our notes and drafts to redo the presentation. This time, we made a backup copy at the end of each day. Each of the team members also saved a copy in his own thumb drive. Although I was still the leader, I knew that my team members no longer trusted me like they used to.', 'Conclusion', 'Action'),
(313, 'The usually quiet basketball court roared with excitement and anticipation. A crucial basketball match between two schools was going on. Greenville Primary players in dark coloured singlets were playing Baxton Primary players. The two teams were equally matched. There were only a few more minutes left to play. A cacophony of sounds were emanating from the place. Team members and supporters were shouting themselves hoarse; many were clapping; some were singing and chanting. Jack, the star player from Baxton Primary school, saw the ball sail into the air towards him. Immediately, he jumped up to grab it. \r', 'Introduction', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(314, 'Adamant about stopping Jack, Terry, the captain of the Greenville team, rushed forward, intending to shove Jack as hard as he could. If Jack fell, Terry would be able to get the ball and his team would win the match. Two of his teammates watched smugly. They knew what Terry had up his sleeve. Jack would surely fall and injure himself. Jack was in midair when he felt a strong shove from behind.\n\n"Ooh," he groaned, stumbling forward. He lost his footing and twisted his left ankle, causing him to fall hard on the ground. Andrew, one of his teammates ran towards him.\n\n"What happened?" he asked, but even before Jack could answer, there was a roar from the spectators and a whistle was blown long and hard. Jack''s heart sank. It was the signal for the end of the match. He did not manage to score before the whistle was blown. Greenville Primary had won. Jack rubbed his swollen ankle and stared in dismay as he watched the players from Greenville Primary hug each other. They carried Terry and tossed him into the air, shouting gleefully. Out of the corner of his eye, Jack realised that there was bedlam among the supporters of his team. They were shouting and gesturing angrily at the Greenville players. Jack felt a lump in his throat and anger rising within him at the same time. He did not see Terry push him, but his gut feeling told him that he must have. He recalled seeing Terry glare at him when he was running towards the hoop. He wondered if any of their supporters had seen Terry push him. Still, the game had already ended. There was nothing they could do about the score now.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(315, 'However, one of the players from Greenville detached himself from his team and walked towards Jack. He mumbled an apology before running off to join his teammates. As Jack was hobbling off the court, the rest of his teammates ran towards him. They told him not to feel discouraged and in unison told him that they would all train harder so as to defeat Greenville in the next match.', 'Conclusion', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(316, '"Nothing exciting ever happens at the park," I grumbled aloud as I trudged along the footpath. It was after a downpour and the place was wetter than usual. Mud was everywhere and I had a hard time avoiding the muddy puddles on the ground. Humming quietly, I wondered where the birds and animals were. I did not even see a single bird this bright and cheery day and the park was devoid of any other insects.\r', 'Introduction', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(317, 'I was about to turn towards a small stall selling drinks when I saw a sudden movement in the bush next to me. I backed away, staring hard at the spot where I had seen the movement. I wondered if my eyes were playing tricks on me. Then, I saw it again. The movement was less now, hardly perceptible. I took several cautious steps forward. I was about to bend down when the leaves rustled loudly and the entire bush shook. Then, the movements stopped and everything was still. Taking a deep breath, I decided to find out what had happened. Afraid that there was an animal there and I would get bitten, I picked up a stick and used it to poke at the leaves. Suddenly, an ear piercing screeching sound came from the bush.\n\n"Ah!" I screamed and fell backwards in fright. I backed away on my hands, hoping that I had not antagonised something dangerous in the bush. Seconds later, one bright yellow feather protruded from the bottom of the bush. Before long, a lemon coloured bird with a myriad of colours on its tail hopped out from the bush. It had a dull red beak and a navy blue crown. I had never seen such a colourful bird before. Noticing me, the bird made the most horrible screeching sound I had ever heard. Then, it reached into the bush and pulled something out. I stared at the round dark red mound in its beak, wondering if it was just a piece of dirt. Then the mound twitched and I noticed a tiny yellow beak. It was a baby bird! It was so tiny. It must have only just hatched. It needed to be in a nest.\n\nLooking around me, I picked up as many twigs as I could find. Some were thin while some were much bigger. Gathering some mud from the wet ground around me, I used it to hold the sticks together to build a small nest. Then, lifting it as gently as I could, I placed it on a low tree branch. I backed away and watched as the bird hopped about watching me for a while. Then, when it was sure that it was safe, it picked up its chick and flew into the nest. I must have done something right because the bird stopped its detestable screech and began chirping a melodious tune.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(318, 'The interesting incident at the park certainly made my walk less boring. My hands felt icky from touching the mud, but I did not mind. As I left, I saw the bird picking up a worm from the ground. I could not wait to return the next day to see if the bird and its chick would still be there.', 'Conclusion', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(319, 'Jessie had always wanted to feed the fish at the pond, so when her mother suggested that they go to the park that afternoon, Jessie was thrilled. Using her own pocket money, she bought two loaves of bread on her way to the park. The fish pond was just as she had imagined it to be. The small black and grey fish swam swiftly about in the pond. Sometimes, they went so deep that it was hard to find them in the slightly murky water. Jessie settled down by the pond with her bag of bread while her mother laid out the picnic mat a short distance away and took out a book to read.\r', 'Introduction', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(320, 'Jessie watched in fascination as the fish swam towards her to get the bread. The more bread she threw in, the more the fish swam towards her. There were scores of fish in the pool, more than Jessie had ever imagined. She watched them, enthralled at the way their sleek bodies moved gracefully in the water. Jessie was so engrossed in what she was doing that she did not hear the rustling of leaves behind her and the high pitched chattering sound. She was so focused on the fish that she did not even see a long hairy arm reach past her and bony fingers curl around her bag of bread. It moved quickly and in a flash, it had already grabbed the bread. However, instead of going back into the tree, the monkey continued hanging by its tail. It started breaking off pieces of bread and putting them into its mouth.\n\n"A monkey!" hollered a young girl who was standing opposite Jessie. She clapped her hands in delight and laughed out loud as she watched the monkey stuff mouthfuls of bread into its mouth. The boy next to her gasped in horror and immediately shouted for Jessie to get out of the way. His shouts caught the attention of Jessie''s mother who looked up just in time to see the monkey swing back into the tree. Afraid that the monkey would attack Jessie, she rushed towards her daughter to pull her to safety. A jogger nearby noticed the monkey still in the tree, now staring hard at the packet of biscuits that Jessie''s mother had been munching on. He shouted at the monkey to try to scare it away. Fortunately, the monkey chattered loudly; then left, still clutching the bag of bread.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(321, 'The encounter with the monkey had scared Jessie''s mother out of her wits. Worried that even more monkeys would return and aggressively look for food, she refused to let Jessie stay at the pond. Disappointed, Jessie left the park with her mother. She would have liked to stay longer but she was glad that she still had the chance to feed the fish.', 'Conclusion', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(322, '"Stop!" came a feeble cry. I glanced around to see where the sound was coming from. Out of the corner of my eye, I spotted a young man weaving through the tables and stools at the food centre. He navigated his way through a group of boisterous teenagers and sprinted towards the bus stop that I was waiting. At his heels was an elderly lady who was sprightly for her age. Although she was panting, she was almost catching up.  \r', 'Introduction', 'Criminal Acts'),
(323, 'However, the man managed to speed up. The distance between him and the lady widened and he was soon much further ahead of her. I expected the man to run past me, but instead, he slowed down when he neared me. Before I could move out of the way, he thrust a small hard object in my hand and ran off. I was about to protest, but he was already out of sight. I stared after the man, my hand still at my side, clutching the object. For several seconds, I did not dare to move. My mind was in a whirl and I was too afraid to even look at what I was holding. Finally, I looked down and saw that I was holding a multi-coloured purse. It was made of many tiny stones stuck closely to one another. At the top was a small silver clasp. I guessed that the purse belonged to the elderly lady.\n\nI scanned the crowd around me to search for the elderly lady that I had seen earlier. The man was probably a thief and her cries must have frightened him, so he decided to abandon the purse. I was looking past the group of teenagers at the spot where I had last seen the elderly lady when I felt someone grip my arm firmly. Looking up, I saw a stern looking policeman with the elderly lady who was running earlier. "She''s his accomplice!" she insisted, glaring at me. Snatching the purse from my hand, she opened it. She cried out in dismay when she found it empty. "Where''s my money?" she demanded in a booming deep voice that attracted the attention of almost everyone nearby. I started protesting, but the policeman silenced me and insisted that I go to the police station with him.\n\n"No! No! No!" I cried, backing away. "There was a man! He shoved the purse in my hand and then he ran off!" I insisted repeatedly, but neither the elderly lady nor the policeman would believe me. After a while, I realised that I was creating a spectacle of myself. I decided to go to the police station with him instead.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Criminal Acts'),
(324, 'After giving my statement, I was allowed to leave the station. As I walked out, the elderly lady glared at me and mumbled that she would get her revenge on those who dared to steal from her. I hurried away. Despite having a head full of white hair and walking almost half bent, she was certainly a feisty person. I hoped the police would apprehend the thief soon. ', 'Conclusion', 'Criminal Acts'),
(325, 'Wonderful! The car park was deserted! Brandon rubbed his hands gleefully. It was the perfect time to practise his skateboard stunts. He would be able to make use of the slope to try out his new back flip. With his skateboard clutched tightly in his hand, Brandon raced up the steps to the fourth floor. He would be safe there. Even if cars entered the car park, they would not park at the top floor of the car park. Brandon positioned himself at the top of the slope. He was ready!\r', 'Introduction', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(326, '"Whee!" Brandon shouted as he flew down the slope. He moved so quickly that his skateboard hardly touched the ground. In seconds, he found himself almost at the bottom of the slope. He was about to brake when he heard the screeching of tires. "Look out!" someone shouted. Brandon turned and saw the car coming at him just before it crashed into him. He flew off his skateboard and was tossed into the air. His body slammed into a wall before crashing onto the ground. Almost immediately, a dark pool of blood oozed from his head. He moved his head slightly and moaned in pain. Still conscious, he moved his arms and mouthed the word ''help me''. However, seconds later, he felt a darkness overcoming him and he no longer had the strength to move.\n\nThe driver of the car sat stock still behind the wheel, frozen with horror. With both arms still gripping the steering wheel, he stared wide-eyed at the young boy lying on the ground. At that moment, a woman ran towards them. She slowed down when she neared the car and gestured wildly for the driver to come out to help her. When he continued staring blankly ahead, she screamed at him in frustration and ran towards Brandon. She reached into her handbag to get her mobile phone, pressing the keypad furiously with one hand while checking Brandon for a pulse with the other hand. She felt a faint pulse and heaved a sigh of relief. She hit Brandon''s cheek and tried to rouse him, but there was no reaction. "What happened?" a man yelled as he ran down the slope. He had been on the fifth floor when he saw Brandon lying at the bottom of the slope. Neither of them dared to move Brandon as they were afraid of hurting him even more.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(327, 'Fortunately, the ambulance and police arrived less than ten minutes later. Brandon was lifted gingerly onto a stretcher and whisked away to the hospital. The police opened the car door and helped the trembling man out of his seat. He appeared to be in a daze and was unable to say a word. Without protesting, he allowed himself to be led away. The lady agreed to go to the police station to give her statement. It was certainly a tragic day. Everyone prayed that Brandon would be all right. ', 'Conclusion', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(328, 'Popular songs were blasting from the tiny speakers mounted at the corners of the store. I was in my favourite stationery shop looking for pieces of paper with unique designs to make a scrapbook for my mother for her birthday. It was a shop in a row of shophouses. As it was still early, there were only a handful of people in the store. The owner was busy arranging several pieces of brightly coloured paper stars on a shelf. I carried a basket with me to pick up what I needed. Stepping into the far corner of the shop, I bent to search through a box with packets of paper cut outs of birthday cakes.\r', 'Introduction', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(329, 'I found a packet that I liked and was about to check out the corner where they sold colourful ribbons when I heard a series of sharp bleeps. It sounded like a fire alarm. I ignored it at first as fire alarms were always being set off accidentally. However, the bleeps did not stop. Looking around, I saw the few people in the shop tossing aside their things and hurrying out. Even the store owner was nowhere to be seen. I panicked and threw down my basket. I hastened towards the front door, but I was so nervous that I kept getting lost among the maze of shelves. Just then, I felt someone grabbing my arm and dragging me towards the front door. I tried to shake off the person, but he was much stronger than I was. Looking up, I saw that it was the store owner. He was looking straight ahead, his eyes narrowed and his mouth set in a straight line.\n\nWe were halfway out of the shop when there was a sudden whiff of smoke. Alarmed, I slowed down and turned around to find out the source of the smoke. "Walk!" the store owner commanded in such a firm voice that made me so petrified, I hurried as fast as I could. We crossed the road and waited across the street with the rest of the customers who had left the shop. While waiting for the fire fighters to arrive, the store owner told me that he had been boiling water on a portable stove in the storeroom when he accidentally dropped a stack of paper near the fire. Although he managed to put out the fire on the paper, there was so much smoke in the room that the alarm went off. He was so flustered that he forgot to turn off the stove. In his haste, he tossed the papers aside and left the storeroom. Afraid that the entire shop would be on fire, he immediately told everyone to leave the shop.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(330, 'Fortunately, the fire fighters arrived soon. The fire that the store owner had expected did not occur. Everyone heaved a sigh of relief, but the store owner was severely warned by the fire fighters not to use a stove in the store. There were too many fire hazards around. The store owner readily agreed and I saw him throwing out the portable stove when I was leaving the store later that morning.', 'Conclusion', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(331, 'When everyone had settled down and the clapping had stopped, Cheryl looked uncertainly at the mountain of presents around her. She did not know which one to open first. There was silence as everyone waited for Cheryl to reach for the first present. Cheryl shrugged and glanced about at her friends with a smile. Almost immediately, everyone pushed their presents towards her to ask her to open it first. After much hesitation, Cheryl picked a wrapper with pictures of deer all over it. The box was heavier than she had expected and there was a clinking sound when she shook it slightly. \r', 'Introduction', 'Characters');
INSERT INTO `astarcompo_templates` (`templateID`, `template`, `templateType`, `theme`) VALUES
(332, 'Cheryl tore open the wrapper and eagerly opened the dark blue cardboard box. "Oh no!" she exclaimed in dismay when she looked in. "What a beautiful present! But it''s ruined!" Everyone fought to see what was in the box. There were gasps of shock and the clicking of tongues as everyone stared at the broken pieces of glass in the box. It was a heart-shaped glass jewellery box with coloured stones around it. In the middle of the cover had been a huge pink butterfly. However, the wings of the butterfly had broken off and part of the glass casing was also broken. There was a large chip on the cover as well. Cheryl held the box open in front of her and stared at it, flabbergasted. She remembered putting all her presents safely in her room during the party. How could it have been damaged?\n\nEmma, Cheryl''s best friend, cried out in anger when she saw the damaged jewellery box. It was her present to Cheryl. She could not believe that it was damaged. When Cheryl found out that the jewellery box was from Emma, she was so upset that she burst into tears. She tried to pick up the pieces to put them together, but Emma immediately told her not to touch the pieces or she would hurt herself.\n\nSeeing how upset his sister was, Cheryl''s brother walked meekly towards her and mumbled an apology. At first, Cheryl did not understand why her brother was apologising. Then, it struck her that he must have caused the damage. "Did you?" she hollered so loudly that her friends immediately tried to calm her down. Her brother backed away, almost falling backwards in fright. He ran away, wailing. Cheryl wanted to go after him, but her friends held her back. Even Emma, who was so upset about the broken box told Cheryl that her brother probably did not mean to damage the present.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(333, 'Cheryl was about to apologise to Emma for spoiling her present, but Emma told Cheryl that she would buy her another jewellery box. Everyone encouraged Cheryl to open the other presents and the jewellery box was soon forgotten.', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(334, 'I stared after my father in disbelief. What could have set off such behaviour? First, his jovial demeanour had disappeared. His face turned ashen and without a word he had run out of the supermarket. I was tempted to go after him but I was weighed down by a heavy basket of fruits and a trolley filled with toiletries and raw food. My father had left me standing in the middle of the supermarket. I was an obstacle to those around me and after several irritated ''excuse me'', I tried my best to navigate the trolley to the side. \r', 'Introduction', 'Family'),
(335, 'Fiddling with my mobile phone, I wanted to call my father. To my dismay, the battery icon blinked twice and then my screen went dark. I shoved it back into my back pocket in frustration. Leaving the trolley and basket at the corner, I walked towards the front door of the supermarket to check if my father''s car was still in the parking lot. As I had expected, it was gone. I stamped my foot in frustration as my wallet was in the car, along with my haversack. I was about to make my way back to the trolley when I heard my name being called through the loudspeakers in the supermarket.\n\nI rushed towards the counter near the entrance. The lady behind the counter told me that my father had called and wanted to speak to me. The colour drained from my face once I heard what my father had to tell me. My mother had been in a serious accident and was in a coma in the hospital. My father told me to leave the groceries we had picked and to take a taxi to the hospital. He would meet me at the taxi stand and pay for the fare. Throwing down the receiver, I dashed out of the supermarket and flagged the first taxi that I saw.\n\nWhen I arrived at the hospital, I saw my father waiting by the taxi stand. He pulled open the taxi door even before the taxi came to a complete stop. Throwing a ten-dollar note at the taxi driver, he told me to hurry and ran back into the hospital. I almost fell as I was running after him. My heart was pounding and I felt dizzy from rushing around.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Family'),
(336, 'Minutes later, I was staring at the slim delicate figure of my mother through a glass window. Numerous tubes were attached to her and a huge bandage was around her head. A machine monitored her heartbeat and a drip was attached to her left arm. I burst into tears. Less than half an hour ago, my father and I were just thinking of a surprise feast to cook for my mother that night.', 'Conclusion', 'Family'),
(337, 'Anna approached the stage uneasily. She was participating in her school''s Storytelling Competition for the first time. Anna had entered the competition with her teacher''s encouragement. \r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(338, 'She breathed in deeply and looked at the judges. She shifted her feet, cleared her throat, and began her story. "My... my... child... childhood..." stammered Anna. Her poor start made her feel even more nervous. Although she had memorised her story, she could not overcome her stage fright.\n\nJust as Anna was about to throw in the towel, her teacher began to clap. The rest of the teacher-judges joined in the clapping. Anna was hugely encouraged by the teachers'' actions. Closing her eyes and taking a few deep breaths, Anna opened her eyes and started her story again. This time, she recited her story fluently and smoothly. As her confidence grew, she became more lively.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(339, 'When she had finished her story, Anna took a deep bow, to the enthusiastic applause of her teachers.', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(340, 'The class had just finished a test. Their teacher Miss Smith, was collecting the test papers when someone in the class shouted, "Help, help, Miss Smith!"\r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(341, 'Miss Smith looked up to see Jonathan, who sat in the back row, waving frantically at her. She strode hurriedly to the back of the classroom and asked what the matter was. Some curious pupils turned their heads around to see what the commotion was about.\n\n"Aaron has a fever and has fainted, Miss Smith," explained Jonathan. "Earlier on, he told me that he was feeling unwell. He burnt the midnight oil last night for today''s test."\n\nQuickly, Miss Smith asked Jonathan to help her carry Aaron to the sick bay. She popped two paracetamol tablets into Aaron''s mouth while Jonathan placed a cold face towel on Aaron''s forehead.\n\nWhen Aaron regained consciousness, he saw the concerned look on Miss Smith and Jonathan''s faces. "I''m sorry for the trouble I''ve caused," said Aaron apologetically.\n\n"Although the test is important, so is your health," said Miss Smith gently. "You really should get enough rest."\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(342, 'Hearing Miss Smith''s advice, both Aaron and Jonathan nodded their heads in agreement. "Don''t worry, Miss Smith," said Jonathan. "We''ll make sure we stay healthy and also do well in our exams!', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(343, '"Quick! Shoot the ball in," said Kevin. He and his two friends were having a game of basketball at the court near his block. In front of the basketball court was a car park.\r', 'Introduction', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(344, 'Kevin aimed a shot and threw the basketball with all his might. The basketball hit the board and rebounded. It went over the fence that surrounded the basketball court and rolled into the middle of the road.\n\n"I''ll get it," volunteered Kevin. As he ran to retrieve the basketball, a van was turning into the car park. With bated breath, Kevin''s two friends helplessly watched the moving van heading towards Kevin.\n\nThe van screeched to halt. It narrowly missed Kevin by an inch. Opening the van door, the driver got out and helped Kevin to his feet. Kevin''s friends ran to him immediately.\n\n"That was dangerous, boy," said the van driver. "I could have knocked you down. Luckily you''re all right."\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(345, 'Kevin was glad that he had escaped unhurt. He told himself that he would never again run into the road. ', 'Conclusion', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(346, 'I was walking home from school one day, when I heard the sound of running footsteps behind me. A man ran into me and knocked me to the ground.\r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(347, 'The fall almost winded me. The man looked as if he were being chased by a dog, I thought to myself, as I tried to get up.\n\nJust then, a dog ran past me, hot on the man''s heels. It pounced on the man and pinned him to the ground. Another man, holding a stick, arrived on the scene almost immediately. He stopped and helped me to my feet. I thanked him for his assistance.\n\n"This man here is a burglar," said the man who helped me up. "He climbed over my fence with the intention of breaking into my house. He tried to escape, so I set my dog on him."\n\nThe burglar struggled to get free, but the huge dog had him pinned under his big strong paws. The police came a few minutes later, and the burglar was put into the police car.\n\n"Thank you, boy," said the owner of the house to me. "If it weren''t for you, my dog might not have been able to catch the burglar."\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(348, 'I flashed him a wry smile and went home. I do not want to have an experience like that again!', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(349, 'It was a crowded Saturday afternoon at the shopping centre. Shoppers thronged the shopping complex. "What luck!" thought Gavin to himself. "I can breeze through the crowd easily in my new pair of Heelys."\r', 'Introduction', 'Unexpected Events'),
(350, 'A newcomer to the fad of roller-shoes, Gavin rolled forward unsteadily. He brushed past several shoppers as he moved along the narrow passageway. Some people cast disapproving looks at him. To stop rolling, Gavin stretched out his arms for the nearest wall. In his haste to get there, he did not notice a woman with a baby in her arms in front of him, and almost knocked her down. Fortunately, she stopped in her tracks just in time to avoid a collision.\n\nA security guard who had witnessed the incident approached Gavin and said, "It''s dangerous to skate in crowded places. What if you hurt someone? How would you feel?"\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Unexpected Events'),
(351, 'Realising his folly, Gavin apologised to the woman, who accepted his apology. He also promised the security guard that he would not be so reckless again. He went outside and found a quiet place to practice using his Heelys properly.', 'Conclusion', 'Unexpected Events'),
(352, 'The school bell rang for recess. It was raining heavily outside as the pupils walked slowly from their classrooms to the canteen. Suddenly, there was a loud thud. Everybody turned to look. \r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(353, '"Ouch!" cried Johnny, lying sprawled on the canteen floor. While Bradly rushed forward to help his classmate up, Charlie went to alert the teacher. Johnny howled in pain while Bradly tried to touch him. Soon, Charlie had returned with their form teacher, and the three of them carried the injured Johnny to the sick-bay.\n\nJohnny had suffered a slight cut to his chin and had bruises on his arms. As Johnny''s teacher dressed his wound, he asked what had caused Johnny to fall.\n\nGuiltily, Johnny said, "I was hungry, and I didn''t want to be the last in the queue . In my haste, I slipped and fell."\n\nJohnny''s teacher shook his finger at Johnny and said, "You should have realised that it''s dangerous to run about, especially on rainy days. Floors tend to be slippery, and accidents will happen if one isn''t careful."\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(354, 'Johnny hung his head in shame. As a punishment to Johnny, his teacher made him write a composition about the accident. It reminded Johnny not to make the same mistake again.', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(355, 'Aaron and Harry were playing a game of marbles. Being avid players, they often visited each other''s block for a friendly game. Aaron aimed a shot at Harry''s favourite marble which he had been eyeing for some time. However, he misjudged the distance, and his own marble missed Harry''s marble by a few centimeters. The marble rolled to the second landing of the staircase. \r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(356, 'While Aaron was jogging down the stairs to retrieve his marble, he saw a box at the bottom of the staircase. "Hey, Harry! Take a look at this. I wonder what''s inside," said Aaron.\n\nAs Aaron reached for the box, Harry stopped him. "Be careful! It could be something dangerous," warned Harry.\n\nThe two boys then approached the box cautiously. Aaron used a stick to lift the flap of the box slowly. When the opening was big enough, the boys peered into the box to see what mysterious object it contained. To their surprise, it was a star tortoise. Someone must have abandoned the animal.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(357, 'Harry suggested that they inform the authorities at the Agri-Food and Veterinary Office. Within an hour, two officers came and picked the tortoise up. Before leaving, one of the officers said to them, "Thank you, boys, for saving an endangered star tortoise."', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(358, 'It was time for recess. Nicholas opened his bag. He dug deep into the bag, looking for something, and was dismayed to find that it was missing. \r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(359, '"My wallet is gone!" cried Nicholas.\n\nRoger walked over to Nicholas''s table, concerned. "I had my wallet with me this morning. Then we had a P.E. lesson. Someone must have sneaked into the classroom to steal my wallet. Or maybe it''s someone in our class," Nicholas concluded.\n\nRoger advised Nicholas to search his bag thoroughly again, but Nicholas was too agitated to listen to him. Just then, Danny walked into the classroom. Nicholas'' eyes widened when he recalled that Danny had missed the P.E. lesson. Before Roger could stop Nicholas, he had already stormed towards Danny and demanded that he return his wallet.\n\nA baffled Danny denied having taken Nicholas''s wallet. But Nicholas insisted, "You must be the one, because you were alone in the classroom just now."\n\nSuddenly, Roger called out to them. Dangling Nicholas''s wallet from his little finger, he said, "I found your wallet in the front pocket of your hag. You must have missed checking there."\n\nA red-faced Nicholas then apologised to Danny for jumping to conclusions. Danny graciously accepted his apology. "Hey! We''ve only ten minutes left for recess. Hurry!" said Roger.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(360, 'And so the three boys hurried to the canteen for their lunch. ', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(361, 'It started out as an ordinary work day for Linda, a bank teller who worked at the Bukit Timah branch of Singapore Savings Bank. From the time the bank opened its doors, there had been a steady stream of customers Linda had to attend to. By three thirty in the afternoon, half an hour before closing time, the crowd started to dwindle. There were only four customers left in line at Linda’s counter, the only remaining counter which was kept open.\r', 'Introduction', 'Criminal Acts'),
(362, 'Next in line at Linda’s counter was a short and stout Chinese man in his thirties. He was dressed casually in a T-shirt and jeans, with a leather jacket to complete the biker look. He was also carrying a motorcycle helmet in one arm. When he came face to face with Linda, he promptly reached into his helmet and whipped out a pistol, pointing it directly at Linda. Staring down the barrel of a gun, this was the closest Linda had ever been to death’s door.\n\n“Hand over all the cash in the bank’s vault!” demanded the robber in a gruff voice. The three customers behind him were scared speechless. The staff members at the bank were also taken by surprise.\n\n“Don’t try to move or I’ll shoot!” threatened the armed robber, directing his threat to everyone in the bank. With trembling hands, Linda opened the drawer behind the counter and took out wads of cash, placing them before the robber. She also discreetly pressed the silent alarm located below the counter. This would not produce any sound to alarm the robber but would instead activate a distress signal to be sent directly to the police.\n\nMeanwhile, the robber, not content with the wads of cash he had taken and put into a black bag he had with him, demanded that Linda hand over more cash.\n\n“O-Only the manager has the key to the vault,” stammered Linda in a tone as soft and meek as a mouse. Realizing that it would be a risk to remain in the bank for too long and demand that the vault be opened, the robber grabbed the loot that he already had and dashed for the main door.\n\n“Bang!”\n\nThe robber fell to the ground the moment he stepped outside. Unknown to him, following Linda’s pressing of the silent alarm, the police had already arrived at the scene. Armed officers were hiding at the strategic positions outside the bank, waiting to ambush the escaping robber.\n\nThe robber was shot in the leg and slumped on the ground, writhing in pain. He was handcuffed and arrested before being taken by an ambulance to a hospital for treatment. For Linda and the other staff and customers at the bank, their ordeal was finally over.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Criminal Acts'),
(363, 'As she made her way home from work that evening, Linda pondered over the most eventful day of her career. She was thankful that there had been no causalities. Despite the fact of Singapore being a very safe and secure country, it did not mean that everyone could rest on their laurels. As the slogan for the police force’s advertising campaign goes, “Low crime does not mean no crime.”', 'Conclusion', 'Criminal Acts'),
(364, 'It was a beautiful early afternoon. The sun was at its height, but the harsh heat was soothed by a blanket of fluffy white clouds across the sky. At exactly twelve noon, John bade goodbye to his mother and went to the nearby pick-up point to wait for his school bus. There, he met his two fellow schoolmates, Lisa and Daniel. The trio would wait at that spot daily for their school bus, which usually arrived at around twelve fifteen.\r', 'Introduction', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(365, 'John was especially excited to see his two friends that day. He took out his gleaming red model toy car and showed it proudly to his friends. It was a gift from his father for doing well in his common test.\n\n“Wow! That’s cool! Can It move by itself?” enquired Daniel, who was obviously envious of John for his latest possession.\n\n“How fast can it go?” added Lisa.\n\nEager to show off the toy car’s capability for high speed, John gave his friends a demonstration. The miniature car zoomed along the pavement, racing down the kerb and onto the main road. In a haste to retrieve his prized to car, John dashed across the road without consideration for his personal safety.\n\nFrom a distance, the children’s school bus was fast approaching. When Daniel and Lisa saw the bus zooming towards John, they shouted out to warn John of the impending danger, but it was too late. The bus driver was travelling at quite a high speed. As soon as he spotted John running in his path, he applied his emergency brakes. Unfortunately, he was unable to stop in time. As the massive vehicle screeched to a halt, it knocked into John. Upon impact, the tremendous force of the bus flung John a few metres from his original position on the road.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(366, 'John’s limp and lifeless body landed on the hard tarmac. He had lost consciousness and was bleeding profusely from his forehead. The bus driver rushed out from his bus to check on John. Quick-witted Daniel whipped out his mobile phone and dialled ‘995’ for an ambulance. Meanwhile, the bus driver knelt beside John and tried to stop his bleeding with a handkerchief.', 'Conclusion', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(367, 'It was a cool and breezy evening. The football match was over. Dave and I were walking across the field when we saw a beam of multicoloured, blinking lights darting across the sky.\r', 'Introduction', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(368, 'Overcome with curiosity, we hid behind a clump of bushes to witness the scene that was to unfold before our disbelieving eyes. As the light got nearer, we could see that it was being emitted from a circular, disc-shaped object. The strange object was heading towards us at breakneck speed!\n\nCrash! When the dust had settled, the disc flipped open ad out came a pair of strange-looking creatures. They had conical-shaped heads atop a slender body. Their ears were large in proportion to their other facial features and protruded out awkwardly. Their bodies were clad in a spacesuit-like garment made of a reflective material. The duo seemed to be having a conversation in a strange-sounding language which comprised of electronic-like beeps.\n\nFrom our vantage position behind the bushes, we could hardly believe what we were witnessing. At first, I thought we had walked straight into a movie set, but there were no cameras in sight. The bald alien was gesturing wildly to his counterpart, presumably a female, who had long, flowing tresses atop her conical-shaped head. She then went back into the spaceship and emerged with a toolbox. The male alien took out a spanner and prised open a hutch on the spaceship. He looked like he was doing some repairs. Dave and I guessed that the aliens must have encountered some trouble with the engine during their flight and had to make an emergency landing on earth to repair their spaceship before they could resume their flight.\n\nA part of us wanted to go up to the aliens and introduce ourselves. After all, they did not look all that hostile to us. However, we decided against it as we did not know whether the aliens would welcome our intrusion. Furthermore, we did not speak their language and foresaw communication problems.\n\nAfter about ten minutes, the male alien closed the hutch and gestured to his female counterpart to come back up into the spaceship with him. The spaceship then flipped close and the blinking lights came on again.\n\nJust as quickly as they had appeared, the spaceship was gone. We wiped our eyes and slapped our faces to make sure that we had not been dreaming. Could it have been a mere figment of our imagination? To us, it seemed real enough.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(369, 'Although we would hardly contain our excitement and our first instinct was to run home to tell everyone what we had seen, we realized that our story seemed too incredible for anyone to believe. Telling our story would only make us laughing stocks. With that in mind, we decided to keep our encounter with the aliens a secret as we made our way home for dinner that evening.', 'Conclusion', 'Interesting Places and Events'),
(370, 'The day was cool and breezy. Despite the weather forecast of showers in the mid-afternoon, the fluffy cottony white clouds in the sky seemed to suggest the opposite. Amanda, Benny, Crystal and Dennis had decided to meet at a fast-food restaurant near their homes for a study session. The mid-year examinations were approaching and the four Secondary Two students wanted to do well so that they would be posted to a good class the following year.\r', 'Introduction', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(371, 'The foursome arranged to meet by the large canal and walked to the restaurant together. By one o’clock, Benny, Crystal and Dennis were already there. They were chatting when Amanda walked up to them. She was using her mobile phone to send a text message to her mother to inform her that she had arrived safely.\n\n“Wow, Amanda! You’ve got a new mobile phone!” exclaimed Benny.\n\n“Very stylish, eh? Looks expensive,” added Crystal.\n\n“Let me have a look at it,” said Dennis, grabbing the mobile phone from Amanda’s hand. He scrolled through her list of text messages in her inbox.\n\n“So many messages from this guy named tommy. Is he your boyfriend?” teased Dennis. Crystal and Benny rushed to huddle around Dennis to steal a look at the mobile phone screen.\n\n“Hey, that’s private!” retorted Amanda. She tried to grab back the mobile phone, but Dennis raised his arm to put it out of her reach. Dennis passed the mobile phone to Benny, who ran a distance along the canal with it. Amanda ran after him, followed by Dennis and Crystal. Amanda again tried to grab back her mobile phone. During the commotion, the mobile phone slipped out of Benny’s hand and landed into the murky waters of the canal.\n\nFeeling guilty to have been the one who had started it all, Dennis jumped into the canal to help to retrieve Amanda’s mobile phone. After a few minutes, seeing that Dennis was still swimming around in search of the phone, Benny removed his T-shirt and jumped in as well. Being a good swimmer, Amanda followed Benny by jumping into the canal. She was desperate to get back her mobile phone, which was a gift from her mother for her fourteenth birthday.\n\nCrystal looked over the railing as the trio swam around searching for the mobile phone. Suddenly, she realized that the water level in the canal was rising. It must have started to rain nearby and the rainwater was pouring in to fill up the canal. This water level was now rising more and more quickly. Crystal looked closer over the railing. She could no longer see Amanda and Benny. Dennis seemed to be having some difficulty in the water. Before she knew it, he too was swept away by the incoming currents. Crystal was at a loss about what to do. She immediately called ‘995’ on her mobile phone.\n\nWithin ten minutes, a civil defence team was at the scene. Two civil defence rescuers plunged into the raging canal waters to search for the missing trio. Before thirty minutes was up, they managed to find Benny and Dennis and carried them to safety. The boys were subsequently resuscitated and taken to a hospital for observation. Amanda, however, was yet to be found. By that time, Amanda’s parents’ had arrived at the scene. Crystal, too, had stayed on.\n\nAs the hours dragged on, hopes of finding Amanda alive diminished. Crystal, together with Amanda’s parents, kept a vigil at the site, clinging on to whatever little hope they had.\n\nAt around six the following morning, Amanda’s body was finally found floating in the canal, two hundred metres away from where she had originally jumped in. Everyone, especially Amanda’s parents, was devastated at the news.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(372, 'Following the tragedy, the civil defence reiterated their warning to the public to stay away from public canals, even during times of dry weather when the water level was very low. The water level in canals is unpredictable and could rise very quickly, showing no mercy to anyone inside. If Amanda’s death has convinced others of the dangers of swimming in public canals and in that way prevented similar tragedies from taking place, then at least her death would not be in vain. ', 'Conclusion', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(373, '“And the winner for this year’s Dr Tan Kim Mock Memorial Scholarship Award goes to … Jacob Ong of Achievement Primary School!”\r', 'Introduction', 'Family'),
(374, 'As Jacob walked up on stage to receive the award amid the thunderous applause from the audience, he was filled with pride and gratitude. He had emerged the top student in his constituency for the Primary School Leaving Examination with an aggregate score of 279. Along with the prestige of winning the award, Jacob would also receive a full scholarship to study at a top independent secondary school. The scholarship would cover his school fees, books and stationary, uniforms as well as a monthly allowance of one hundred dollars for four years. The scholarship was especially valuable to Jacob as it would ease his mother’s financial burden considerably.\n\nJacob came from a humble family background. His father was an alcoholic who has abandoned the family a long time ago. His mother worked as a washerwoman by day and a cleaner at a food court by night to make enough money to support Jacob and his two younger siblings, a seven-year-old sister and nine-year-old brother. The family of four survived on Mrs Ong’s meagre monthly income of about nine hundred dollars, which was barely enough to make ends meet. They lived in a sparsely furnished two-room rented flat. Jacob and his siblings did not receive any pocket money. They brought their own lunchboxes to school, which contained a sandwich made up of two slices of plain white bread spread with a thin layer of margarine in the middle. Their school books and uniforms were all second-hand, donated by their school, who knew of their family situation.\n\nSince their mother held two jobs, she was hardly home most of the time. Before she left for work each morning, she would prepare some food for the children’s breakfast, lunch and dinner. Being the eldest child, the task of looking after his younger brother and sister fell on Jacob’s young shoulders. The three Ong children went to school and came home together every day. In the afternoon and evening, Jacob would warm up the lunch and dinner respectively which his mother had prepared in advance. Knowing that his mother worked very hard outside and needed to rest when she was at home, Jacob also tried to lessen his mother’s burden by doing most of the household chores. Although his younger siblings sometimes helped out a bit, it was only after much nagging and persuasion from their elder brother. The bulk of the chores were still done by Jacob. In the absence of adult supervision, Jacob assumed the role of mentor and disciplinarian to his siblings. He tried his best to make sure that they did their homework and acted as a mediator when his brother and sister got into any squabbles.\n\nOn top of all that, Jacob also had to cope with his own schoolwork. Fortunately, he was good at time management. Being a hard-working and conscientious student, he made it a point to be attentive in class and revise his work consistently. After every lesson, he would go over what had been taught and if he had any doubts, he would clarify them with his teacher the following day. He completed all his assignments on time and never let his homework pile up. Although he did not have any additional help with his schoolwork outside of school in the form of tuition, he studied by himself and practised by doing old assessment books which were donated by his neighbours. Through his hard work and determination, Jacob excelled academically, leading to him clinching the scholarship award.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Family'),
(375, 'At the award presentation ceremony, seated among the audience was Jacob’s mother. Her weather-beaten face beamed with motherly pride as she watched her firstborn accept the award from the guest of honour. It was during moments like these that she felt all her sweat and toil in bringing up her children were not in vain. Seeing how Jacob had turned out made all her suffering worthwhile. Jacob was indeed her pride and job. His achievements gave her much hope for the future. She was also glad that her younger children had such an outstanding brother as a role model and hope that they, too, might one day follow in Jacob’s footsteps.', 'Conclusion', 'Family'),
(376, 'Warm sunshine bathed the earth. Cottony white clouds lined the pale blue afternoon sky, stretching as far as the eye could see. However, the endless stream of traffic whizzing past the busy street disrupted the idyllic setting, turning the place into yet another enclave of modern civilization in an urban landscape.	\r', 'Introduction', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(377, 'Every afternoon, Jeff had to brave the heavy traffic to reach his home, which was across the road from the bus stop where he usually alighted. Fortunately, a fine piece of modern engineering and architecture had been erected to enable Jeff and many other pedestrians to make it safely across the road at any time of the day. This was the overhead bridge.\n\nOn that fateful afternoon after a football practice, Jeff crossed the overhead bridge. He was just two steps away from reaching the ground at the other side of the road when he heard an earth-shattering crashing noise, accompanied by what he felt was the ground shaking. Jeff instinctively jumped down the last two steps and ran a short distance.\n\nWhen he turned around, he was horrified to see that a tall crane had crashed into the overhead bridge. Half of the bridge had collapsed, leaving the other half dangling precariously in midair. The collapsed section of the overhead bridge had flattened the back of the crane and two other passenger vehicles. Traffic in both directions came to a standstill.\n\nWithin minutes, the police were at the scene. They cordoned off the area to keep unauthorized personnel from getting too close to the scene. The wailing sirens and flashing red lights signalled the arrival of some ambulances and civil defence officers. There was a flurry of activity as rescue workers rushed to rummage through the broken concrete slabs and twisted metal heaps to get to the unfortunate victims in the vehicle which had been directly struck down by the falling overhead bridge.\n\nBy then, a huge crowd of curious onlookers had gather on the pavements on either side of the road next to the accident site. Jeff joined in the gawking crowd but his small stature prevented him from getting a clear view of the chaos. The burning question on his mind was whether there had been any pedestrians crossing the overhead bridge during the time of the collapse. He pondered on his narrow escape and shuddered at the thought that had he crossed the bridge just a minute later, he might not have been there standing in one piece.\n\nMeanwhile, the task of extricating the trapped victims was a delicate one. Heavy machinery could not be used for fear of causing further injury to the victims. At the same time, the rescue workers had to work fast since to the injured survivors, if any, time was of the essence. In the time that Jeff was at the scene, he saw two bodies being removed from the rubble. The pristine white sheet that covered their bodies told him that they did not make it out alive. After about half an hour, Jeff decided to head home as he did not wanted to cause his mother any unnecessary worry.\n\nThe next day, Jeff read about the collapse of the overhead bridge in the newspapers. It was reported that the tragic accident had claimed three lives: the drivers of the two cars which had been flattened and a front-seat passenger in one of the cars. Two rear-seat passengers seated in one of the cars suffered non-life-threatening injuries and were now in hospital. The crane driver escaped unhurt. He was currently being investigated by the authorities for driving a tall, heavy vehicle on a stretch of road that had a height limited for passing vehicles. There was a height limit sign on the overhead bridge to warn tall vehicles not to pass. Miraculously, there were no pedestrians on the overhead bridge at the time of the collapse. There were a few teenagers who were halfway up the stairs leading to the overhead bridge when the crash occurred. Since the stairs withstood the impact on the overhead bridge, the pedestrians were able to run down the stairs and were unhurt.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(378, 'For Jeff and his parents, they were only glad and relieved that Jeff was alive and well following his close shave with death.', 'Conclusion', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(379, 'Both John’s parents were working late again, for the fourth night in a row. John resented spending his evening alone in the cold, empty house. After a while, the novelty of having the whole house to himself had worn off. He had surfed all the interesting websites, played many games on his gaming console and watched all that was worth watching on television. He had even completed all his holiday assignment, both from school and tuition class. John was bored to tears being cooped up at home. He decided to go to the nearby community club to play basketball with his friends. John found it inconvenient to carry his house keys with him as he was wearing a singlet and a pair of basketball shorts which had no pockets. He was also aware of his tendency to lose his keys while on the road, so he decided to hide his keys in a flowerpot next to his main door.\r', 'Introduction', 'Criminal Acts'),
(380, 'Little did John know that he was being watched. After he left, nobody was home except Barky, his pet Alsatian puppy. Seizing the opportunity, a burglar broke into the house. In fact, he did not have to break in. He simply took the keys which John had left in the flowerpot and let himself in. Barky tried to play the part of a true guard dog and ‘growled fiercely’ at the intruder, but the burglar was unfazed by the yelps made by the puppy. Once inside, he made his way upstairs and began searching the rooms for valuables.\n\nWhen John came home, he did not sense that there was anything out of the ordinary until he went upstairs and caught the burglar red-handed in his parents’ bedroom trying to pry open the safe.\n\nThe burglar caught sight of John and grabbed him. He was not going to let a young boy foil his burglary attempt. The burglar tied John’s hands tightly to the bedpost with a rope and gagged his mouth with a rag. John struggled to free himself but was unsuccessful. He was also unable to scream for help.\n\nMeanwhile, the burglar continued to search the entire house for valuables worth stealing. When he was out of sight, Barky crept up to John. With a little prompting, Barky bit the rope around John’s wrists until it snapped, setting John free. Sensing the tension of the situation, John steeled himself to remain calm. He immediately latched the master bedroom door from inside, locking himself and Barky in. He also locked all the windows and drew the curtains. Once holed up in his safe enclave where the burglar could not get him, John took out his mobile phone and secretly phoned the police to make a report. He told the officer his predicament and gave him his address and location within the house. He also telephoned his parents to warn them not to enter the house should they arrive home before the police came.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Criminal Acts'),
(381, 'Within minutes, a posse of police officers arrived at the scene of the crime. They stormed John’s house and arrested the burglar just as he was coolly walking out through the main door with his loot. They then rushed upstairs to tell John that the worst was over and he could come out safely. By that time, John’s parents had returned home. His mother ran and hugged her son tightly. She was relieved that he was all right and promised not to work late and leave him alone at home again. John admitted to his mother that it was actually his fault since he had gone out and left the keys in the flowerpot. He then carried Barky in his arms and introduced the real hero to everyone. If Barky had not bitten the rope to set him free, he would not have been able to call the police and the burglar would have got away scot-free. Everyone patted Barky on the head and agreed that the cute little puppy deserved some credit for his crime-busting effort.', 'Conclusion', 'Criminal Acts'),
(382, 'Tomatoes were in season again. Ripe, red and juicy, they were going at just two dollars a kilogram. The prices of seafood were also very reasonable. At the butcher’s stall, the red meat was succulent and lean, perfect for dishing up her sweet and sour pork speciality. Madam Ong had a fruitful marketing trip that morning. Armed with bags of fresh produce, she was eager to get home to whip up a sumptuous meal for her family that evening.\r', 'Introduction', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(383, 'As she walked along the corridor leading to her flat, there was an uneasy silence. Usually, her two children, Mark and Tina, would be tearing the house down with their endless chatter and laughter. The two youngsters aged seven and nine, were indeed a handful.\n\nAs Madam Ong neared her flat, she could see smoke billowing out of the window, as well as from under the wooden door. Sensing that something was not right, Madam Ong quickly rushed to unlock the iron grille, followed by the wooden door. When she opened the door, she found the living room filled with clouds of thick, grey smoke. She shouted for her children, but her screams were muffled by the dense, suffocating smoke.\n\nFearing for her dear children’s lives, Madam Ong braved the flames and rushed into the house. She found Mark and Tina huddled together in a corner in the master bedroom. The smoke had started to seep in from under the door and despite having the window open, the room was becoming filled with smoke, causing the children to cough. Madam Ong grabbed Mark and Tina by the arms and told them to hurry out of the house. It was miraculous that she had come home in time before the smoke thickened and the fire spread. Madam Ong managed to make it safely out of the house with her children.\n\nBy this time, a few neighbours had already gathered outside Madam Ong’s flat. One of them told her that she had just called the fire brigade. From the outside, Madam Ong saw that her kitchen was on fire. Bright orange flames had engulfed the indoor clothes drying stand with a few pieces of laundry hanging on it. The fire seemed to be spreading to the wooden kitchen cabinets. As the wood fuelled the flames, the fire grew. The blankets of smoke became thicker and blacker as it continued to fill the place, reducing the visibility of what was happening inside the house. Standing outside the main entrance, Madam Ong and her children could feel the intense heat of the fire.\n\nThe firemen arrived within ten minutes in their compact but well-equipped red rhino emergency vehicle. Armed with protective gear and powerful water hoses, they braved the billowing layers of smoke to come face to face with the fire. The fierce, raging fire had met its match. The firemen directed their hoses at the angry fire spraying jets of water to douse the flames. They brought the fire under control in a matter of minutes and completely put it out soon after.\n\nPart of the kitchen was badly damaged in the fire. A row of cabinets had been reduced to ashes. The walls were blackened and a layer of soot covered everything in the kitchen. Fortunately, no one was hurt in the blaze and the fire did not spread beyond the kitchen or to the neighbouring units.\n\nMark and Tina later admitted that they had been playing with matches by the kitchen window while their mother was out. Five minutes before their mother returned, Mark had accidentally set fire to his father’s shirt, which was hanging on the clothes stand to dry. Seeing the flames on the burning shirt spread so quickly, the children panicked. Unable to run out of the house as they did not have the key, they hid in the master bedroom because it was furthest from the kitchen.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(384, 'Madam Ong chided her children for playing with fire. She told them that they were lucky she had come back in time or else the consequences would have been disastrous. Following the incident, Mark and Tina understood first-hand the dangers of playing with fire and promised never to do it again.', 'Conclusion', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(385, 'It was a Saturday afternoon and my best friend, Linda, and I were at the Central Shopping Mall. We did not want to buy anything in particular but were just enjoying walking around, looking in the shop windows, and chatting. \r', 'Introduction', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(386, 'I remember that as we were approaching a book shop we suddenly heard a loud crying noise. At first, we could not see where it was coming from, but as we turned the corner, we saw a little boy crying his heart out.\n\n"Look!" I said to Linda. "The poor little thing must have got separated from his mother."\n\nWe rushed up to the crying child and tried to comfort him.\n\n"What shall we do?" asked Linda.\n\n"I know," I replied. "You stay with the little boy and I will go and find a security guard. Perhaps, he can make an announcement that the boy''s mother will hear."\n\nSo, I left Linda with the child, who had now stopped crying so loudly, and went in search of a guard. Then, at the far end, I spotted a guard''s uniform.\n\nAs I ran towards him, I realised that he was speaking to a distraught-looking woman.\n\n"That must be the boy''s mother," I thought.\n\nI came up to them and said a bit breathlessly, "Are you looking for your son?"\n\nThe woman looked at me as if she could not believe her ears.\n\n"Yes! Yes! Have you seen him?" she said hurriedly.\n\n"Yes," I replied. "Don''t worry! He''s with my friend and he''s fine."\n\nI led the guard and the boy''s mother back to where Linda and her son were. She rushed up to the boy, picked him up and held him tight. Then she turned to Linda and me an said quietly, "I will never be able to thank you enough!"\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(387, '"You just have!" I replied. "It''s enough to see you and your son reunited." ', 'Conclusion', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(388, 'It was during the final part of the English exam when Marie suddenly noticed that a boy in front of her was bending over his desk in a strange way. She looked at him more closely and had the strong impression that he was listening to something up his sleeve. What could he be doing? The boy looked around him in a furtive way, as if he was checking that no one was watching him. \r', 'Introduction', 'School and You'),
(389, 'Marie felt shocked and worried. She had the distinct feeling that the boy was cheating. He appeared to be listening to an MP3 player that he had hidden on his arm, under his shirt. She knew that any kind of listening device was forbidden during school time, and if anyone was caught using one in a lesson, it would be confiscated immediately. During an exam this was obviously wrong and was a clear breach of the rules. Marie knew she had to report this to the teacher. It was unfair on the other students and it was her responsibility to inform the invigilator.\n\nMarie put up her hand and eventually the teacher noticed and came over to her desk. "Yes?" asked the teacher quietly. "I think that boy is listening to something up his sleeve," said Marie as she pointed to the boy who had by now removed his arm from the desk.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'School and You'),
(390, '"Are you sure?" asked the teacher. Marie nodded gravely. The teacher went over to the boy in question. She asked him a few questions quietly, then red-faced, the boy was led from his desk to the front of the room. Eventually, another teacher came and he was taken out of the exam room. ', 'Conclusion', 'School and You'),
(391, 'Last weekend, my mother and I decided to have a day out together. We planned to do some shopping and then have lunch. But things did not turn out quite the way we expected. \r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(392, 'We caught the bus into town and went to the department store. There, Mum saw a new collection of silk scarves.\n\n"Oh, look at those! Aren''t they lovely?" she said.\n\n"I could buy one for your Aunty May''s birthday."\n\nSo, we started looking through the scarves. They were all so nice it was difficult to choose. Mum kept picking up one, putting it down, and picking up another. I have to admit I began to get a little bored and started looking around at the other customers.\n\nIt was then that I saw her. She was a middle-aged woman, smartly-dressed in a fashionable coat. That was perfectly normal. What was not normal was the way she quickly put something into her coat pocket.\n\n"Mum, Mum!" I whispered.\n\n"What is it, darling?" she asked.\n\n"Mum, you see that woman over there?" I said quietly. "I just saw her shoplifting."\n\n"Are you sure?" replied Mum. "That''s a very serious accusation!"\n\n"I''m positive!" I replied and told her what I had seen.\n\nMum told me to wait at the scarf counter and not to move. She went off and returned very quickly with a man in a smart suit.\n\n"Tell this gentleman what you saw," said Mum.\n\nI repeated my story to the man, who was the chief security officer for the store. He listened carefully and then turned away to speak on his walkie-talkie. Seconds later, two women approached the shoplifter and escorted her away.\n\n"Follow me, please," instructed the officer.\n\nMum and I followed the man to an office. He told us to wait outside. I was beginning to feel very nervous. After a few minutes, the door opened and the officer came out with a big smile on his face. We entered the room and immediately saw the woman in the coat. She was smiling, too!\n\n"Is this the woman you saw?" asked the officer.\n\n"Yes, it is," I replied quietly.\n\nThe officer then explained that the woman had just been putting her mobile phone back into her pocket. She was not a shoplifter at all.\n\nI felt terrible. "I''m so sorry!" I stuttered.\n\n"Not at all," replied the woman kindly. "You did exactly the right thing."\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(393, '"Yes," said the security chief. "You should feel proud of yourself. Reporting what you think is a crime is always the correct thing to do!" ', 'Conclusion', 'Characters');
INSERT INTO `astarcompo_templates` (`templateID`, `template`, `templateType`, `theme`) VALUES
(394, 'My sister and I were walking along Wellington Avenue yesterday on the way to school, when she noticed something lying on the ground in front of us. At first I thought it was just a piece of litter and I was going to pick it up and put it in the litter bin, but as we got closer I realised that in fact, it was a wallet. \r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(395, 'My sister picked up the wallet and turned to me with a questioning look. I glanced around the street to see if there was anyone who might have dropped it. However, at that time of the morning there were only a few children making their way to school and this brown leather wallet obviously did not belong to a young person.\n\nWe quickly realised that the only thing to do was to take the wallet to the police. Fortunately, the police station was only a few hundred metres away, and if we hurried, we could drop in there and not be late for school. Just as we had made this decision, an elderly lady came out of the convenience store in front of us. She had a very worried look on her face and she was accompanied by the store assistant. They were looking for something and we could hear the lady say "I know I had it with this morning, because I bought some bread earlier on. It must have fallen out of my bag."\n\n"My sister approached the lady and said, "Excuse me. Have you lost something?"\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(396, 'It did not take long to establish that the lady had in fact lost her wallet and it was the one that we had found. With grateful thanks the lady recovered her lost wallet and we continued on our way to school. "All''s well that ends well" as they say! ', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(397, 'David was on his way to the park the other day when he heard a little girl crying. He looked over in the direction the sobs were coming from, and saw a little girl crying at the bottom of a tree. \r', 'Introduction', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(398, '"What''s the matter?" asked David. The little girl was so upset she could only point up at the tree. There, on one of the thin branches was a very scared-looking cat. Eventually, the girl managed to explain that her cat had been chased by a dog and had climbed the tree to escape the frightening dog. The cat had been there for at least 15 minutes and had not moved. The girl was convinced that it was stuck and could not get down on its own.\n\nDavid looked at the fairly low branches on the tree and decided he could quite easily climb up to the cat and rescue it. He took off his jacket and started to hoist himself up onto the first branch. Nimbly moving from branch to branch, he approached the frightened cat, which had still not moved. He grasped the thin branch where the cat was crouched but there was a terrible creaking noise and the branch bent low. Was it going to break? David looked down at the ground, which seemed suddenly far below him. The little girl cried out and the cat sprang onto a lower branch and, before they knew it, was safely in the arms of the little girl.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(399, 'David managed to get himself back down onto the ground and, leaving the ittle girl hugging her cat, and mumbling her thanks to him, he continued on his way to the park. ', 'Conclusion', 'Outdoor Activities'),
(400, 'Last weekend, my best friend, Tom and I went to the cinema to watch a movie. At the end of the movie, Tom and I decided to walk home together.\r', 'Introduction', 'Characters'),
(401, 'We were happily walking along the street, talking about the movie and making plans for the following weekend when Tom suddenly nudged me.\n\n"Look!" he said and pointed to the two teenagers in front of us. I looked but did not see anything strange. "What about them?" I asked.\n\n"Just keep watching them," Tom insisted.\n\nSo I did. I watched them as they walked along and then saw what Tom had seen. The two of them had packets of sweets and were eating them and then dropping the wrappers onto the pavement.\n\n"They shouldn''t do that!" I said.\n\n"I know," replied Tom. "But what can we do? They are much older than us."\n\nTom was right. They were older than us but this just made me angrier. Boys their age should know better.\n\n"I know what to do," I said to Tom. "First, let''s collect all their wrappers!"\n\nTom looked at me, puzzled, and then he smiled. We rushed back and collected all the wrappers the boys had dropped. Then we returned and caught up with the two culprits.\n\nTom and I looked at each other. We were both a bit nervous. I stepped forward, took a deep breathe and then tapped one of the boys on the shoulder.\n\n"Excuse me," I said, "I think these are yours." I held out the handful of litter we had collected.\n\nThe two boys looked at each other in surprise and then turned back to us. I thought they were going to shout at us or something. But, to my surprise, they put out their hands and took their wrappers back.\n\n"Sorry!" said one of the boys. "We just weren''t thinking about what we were doing. Thank you for reminding us.\n\nThey took the wrappers over to a rubbish bin and dropped them in. Then they turned back to us.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Characters'),
(402, '"Here said the boy with a big smile. "Have these sweets us a reward. But don''t be like us and drop the wrappers!" ', 'Conclusion', 'Characters'),
(403, 'Last Saturday, my family and I were driving along a country road on the way to see my aunt, when we came across an accident. Ahead of us, on the side of the road, a young cyclist was sitting next to his bike, holding his leg and looking worried. \r', 'Introduction', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(404, 'My father stopped the car and we all got out. My mother rushed up to the boy. She asked him if he was all right and he replied in a shaking voice that he was fine. Even so, my mother told him to go and sit in our car for a while. She gave him a drink of cold water while he explained what had happened.\n\nApparently, he was cycling along the road and, without any warning, he lost control of his bike. The boy fell off and hurt his leg slightly. My mother looked at the boy''s grazed leg, but it was not seriously injured. My father was looking around the road to see what might have caused the accident and it was then that he noticed shards of glass. He picked up the bike and realised that the front tyre was punctured. He brought the bike back to our car and explained to the boy that he must have ridden over some of the sharp glass and that had punctured the tyre. This had caused him to lose control of his bike.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(405, 'We put the bike in the boot of our car and gave the boy a lift back to his house, which was only a few kilometres out of our way. He thanked us profusely and invited us in to his house but we did not have much time, so we continued on our journey.', 'Conclusion', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(406, 'It was the first day of the new school year. When the bell went for break, we all rushed out into the basketball court to meet up with our friends and classmates again. There was a lot of noise as people cheerfully greeted each other. You could hear laughter and shouts as old friends chattered about their holidays and what they had done. \r', 'Introduction', 'School and You'),
(407, 'Gradually, we all settled into groups of friends. A few boys were throwing a ball around and some girls were skipping. I was with my best friends, Helen, Janet and Lily. We were excitedly catching up with each other''s news when I happened to look up and see a girl sitting alone on a bench. She looked very sad and lonely. I pointed her out to my friends.\n\n"Look at that girl. Doesn''t she seem sad? I wonder what the matter is," I said to them.\n\n"I haven''t seen her before," said Helen. "Perhaps she''s new."\n\n"Do you think we should do something?" asked Janet.\n\n"Yes," I replied firmly. "Just imagine if it was your first day at a new school and nobody talked to you!"\n\n"You''re right!" the others chorused.\n\nWe went over towards the girl. The nearer we got, the lonelier she appeared. I did feel sorry for her. I went up to her.\n\n"Hello," I said. "My name''s Sarah. What''s your name?"\n\n"My name''s May," she replied timidly.\n\n"Welcome to our school, May," I said. "Let me introduce you to my friends: Helen, Janet and Lily. We were wondering if you would like to join us."\n\n"Oh!" May said. "That would be ever so nice. You see, I''m new and I don''t have any friends yet."\n\n"Well, you do now!" said Lily.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'School and You'),
(408, '"Yes, you do now!" I repeated and the five of us went off together. ', 'Conclusion', 'School and You'),
(409, 'My brother and I were witnesses to an accident last week. We were coming out of a shop and in front of us an elderly lady was quietly walking along the pavement, carrying her bag of groceries. Suddenly, from out of nowhere two children appeared, apparently having a game of chase. They were weaving in and out of the shoppers, jumping over benches and generally frightening everyone. One of them swerved in front of the lady and the other was so close behind that he bumped straight into her! The lady stumbled and lost her footing. She dropped her shopping bag and the groceries spilled out over the pavement. \r', 'Introduction', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(410, 'The two children did not even stop to see the damage they had caused, but ran off down the street.\n\n', 'Main Body', 'Accidents and Disasters'),
(411, 'My brother and I quickly went to the aid of the lady. My brother started to collect the groceries while I led the lady to a bench. She sat down to compose herself and she reassured us that she was unhurt, just a little shaken up. Unfortunately, the two irresponsible children had disappeared and we were unable to identify them. What a terrible thing to happen to a poor elderly lady! ', 'Conclusion', 'Accidents and Disasters');
